/* Past-year questions for Rank mode · JEE Mains · Optics (ray optics and wave optics), grouped by topic and sub-topic.
   Sources: JEE Main, JEE Advanced, IIT-JEE and AIEEE papers. Wording as in the paper; solutions written for this site.
   Fields: y = exam and year, tp = topic, st = sub-topic, q = question, o + c = options and correct index (or array of indices),
   n = numerical answer, s = worked solution, fig = diagram (PhysicaFig spec). M(...) builds a multiple-choice entry, N(...) a numerical one, A(...) a written (multi-part) answer. */
(() => {
'use strict';
const M=(y,tp,st,q,o,c,s,fig)=>({y,tp,st,q,o,c,s,...(fig?{fig}:{})}),N=(y,tp,st,q,n,s,fig)=>({y,tp,st,q,n,s,...(fig?{fig}:{})}),A=(y,tp,st,q,a,s,fig)=>({y,tp,st,q,a,s,...(fig?{fig}:{})});
window.PhysicaRankPyqOptics=[
M("JEE Main 2022 · 27 Jun · Shift 2","lens","Cut lenses","A convex lens has power P. It is cut into two halves along its principal axis. Further one piece (out of the two halves) is cut into two halves perpendicular to the principal axis (as shown in figure: L₁ is the half that is not cut again; L₂ and L₃ are the two pieces cut perpendicular to the axis). Choose the incorrect option for the reported pieces.",["Power of L₁ = {P;2}","Power of L₂ = {P;2}","Power of L₃ = {P;2}","Power of L₁ = P"],0,"A cut along the principal axis leaves the curvatures unchanged, so the half lens L₁ still has power P (statement (d) is right, (a) is wrong).\nCutting across the thickness gives two plano-convex pieces, each of power {P;2}: (b) and (c) are right.\nThe incorrect option is (a)."),
M("JEE Main 2021 · 27 Aug · Shift 1","lens","Combination of lenses","Find the distance of the image from object O, formed by the combination of lenses in the figure (three lenses with f = +10 cm, −10 cm and +30 cm; the object O is 30 cm from the first lens; the second lens is 5 cm beyond the first and the third lens is 10 cm beyond the second).",["75 cm","10 cm","infinity","20 cm"],0,"Lens 1: v = 15 cm, which is 10 cm beyond lens 2. For lens 2 this virtual object gives {1;v} = −{1;10} + {1;10} = 0, so the beam leaves parallel.\nLens 3 focuses it at 30 cm beyond itself.\nDistance from O = 30 + 5 + 10 + 30 = 75 cm."),
M("JEE Main 2021 · 31 Aug · Shift 1","lens","Image position and magnification","An object is placed at the focus of concave lens having focal length f. What is the magnification and distance of the image from the optical centre of the lens?",["{1;4}, {f;4}","{1;2}, {f;2}","Very high, ∞","1, ∞"],1,"f_lens = −f and u = −f: {1;v} = {1;f_lens} + {1;u} = −{2;f}, so v = −{f;2}.\nm = {v;u} = {1;2}."),
M("JEE Main 2021 · 20 Jul · Shift 1","refr","Refraction at a spherical surface","Region I and II are separated by a spherical surface of radius 25 cm whose centre of curvature C lies in region I (μ_I = 1.25; region II has μ_II = 1.4). An object is kept in region I at a distance of 40 cm from the surface. The distance of the image from the surface is",["9.52 cm","37.58 cm","55.44 cm","18.23 cm"],1,"Take light travelling from I to II; C is on the side the light comes from, so R = −25 cm, and u = −40 cm.\n{1.4;v} = {1.4 − 1.25;−25} + {1.25;−40} = −0.006 − 0.03125 = −0.03725.\nv = −37.58 cm (a virtual image on the object's side)."),
M("JEE Main 2021 · 16 Mar · Shift 2","lens","Lens in a liquid","The refractive index of a converging lens is 1.4. What will be the focal length of this lens if it is placed in a medium of same refractive index? Assume the radii of curvature of the faces of lens are R₁ and R₂ respectively.",["zero","{R₁R₂;R₁ − R₂}","1","Infinite"],3,"In a medium of the same index the lens has {n;n_m} − 1 = 0, so its power is zero and f is infinite."),
M("JEE Main 2021 · 17 Mar · Shift 1","lens","Lens maker's formula","The thickness at the centre of a plano convex lens is 3 mm and the diameter is 6 cm. If the speed of light in the material of the lens is 2 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹. The focal length of the lens is",["1.5 cm","0.30 cm","30 cm","15 cm"],2,"n = {3 × 10⁸;2 × 10⁸} = 1.5. The curved face has sagitta 3 mm over a half-chord of 30 mm: R ≈ {30²;2 × 3} = 150 mm.\nf = {R;n − 1} = {150;0.5} = 300 mm = 30 cm."),
M("JEE Advanced 2021","lens","Combination of lenses","An extended object is placed at point O, 10 cm in front of a convex lens L₁ and a concave lens L₂ is placed 10 cm behind it, as shown in the figure. The radii of curvature of all the curved surfaces in both the lenses are 20 cm. The refractive index of both the lenses is 1.5. The total magnification of this lens system is",["0.4","0.8","1.3","1.6"],1,"Each lens has {1;f} = 0.5 × {2;20}, so f₁ = +20 cm and f₂ = −20 cm.\nL₁: u = −10: {1;v} = {1;20} − {1;10}, v = −20 cm, m₁ = 2. This image is 30 cm from L₂.\nL₂: u = −30: {1;v} = −{1;20} − {1;30}, v = −12 cm, m₂ = {12;30} = 0.4.\nm = 2 × 0.4 = 0.8."),
M("JEE Main 2020 · 6 Sep · Shift 2","lens","Lens maker's formula","A double convex lens has power P and same radii of curvature R of both the surfaces. The radius of curvature of a surface of a plano-convex lens made of the same material with power 1.5P is",["2R","{R;2}","{3R;2}","{R;3}"],3,"Double convex: P = (n − 1){2;R}. Plano-convex: 1.5P = (n − 1){1;R′}.\nDividing the second by the first: 1.5 = {R;2R′}, so R′ = {R;3}."),
M("JEE Main 2020 · 7 Jan · Shift 2","lens","Lens in a liquid","A thin lens made of glass (refractive index = 1.5) of focal length f = 16 cm is immersed in a liquid of refractive index 1.42. If its focal length in liquid is f_l, then the ratio f_l/f is closest to the integer",["5","1","17","9"],3,"{f_l;f} = {n − 1;n/n_l − 1}. Here n/n_l − 1 = 1.5/1.42 − 1 = 0.0563.\n{f_l;f} = {0.5;0.0563} = 8.9, closest to 9."),
M("JEE Main 2019 · 9 Jan · Shift 1","lens","Lens and glass plate","A convex lens is put 10 cm from a light source and it makes a sharp image on a screen, kept 10 cm from the lens. Now a glass block (refractive index 1.5) of 1.5 cm thickness is placed in contact with the light source. To get the sharp image again, the screen is shifted by a distance d. Then d is",["0.55 cm towards the lens","0","1.1 cm away from the lens","0.55 cm away from the lens"],3,"With u = v = 10 cm, f = 5 cm. The block (against the source) makes the source appear 1.5(1 − {1;1.5}) = 0.5 cm nearer to the lens: u = 9.5 cm.\n{1;v} = {1;5} − {1;9.5}, so v = 10.56 cm.\nThe screen moves 0.55 cm away from the lens."),
M("JEE Main 2019 · 10 Jan · Shift 1","lens","Combination of lenses","A plano convex lens of refractive index μ₁ and focal length f₁ is kept in contact with another plano concave lens of refractive index μ₂ and focal length f₂. If the radius of curvature of their spherical faces is R each and f₁ = 2f₂, then μ₁ and μ₂ are related as (f₂ is the magnitude of the focal length of the concave lens)",["3μ₂ − 2μ₁ = 1","μ₁ + μ₂ = 3","2μ₁ − μ₂ = 1","2μ₂ − μ₁ = 1"],2,"f₁ = {R;μ₁ − 1} and |f₂| = {R;μ₂ − 1}.\nf₁ = 2|f₂| gives μ₂ − 1 = 2(μ₁ − 1), so 2μ₁ − μ₂ = 1."),
M("JEE Main 2019 · 10 Jan · Shift 2","refr","Refraction at a spherical surface","The eye can be regarded as a single refracting surface. The radius of curvature of this surface is equal to that of cornea (7.8 mm). This surface separates two media of refractive indices 1 and 1.34. Calculate the distance from the refracting surface at which a parallel beam of light will come to focus.",["4.0 cm","1 cm","3.1 cm","2 cm"],2,"{n₂;v} − {n₁;∞} = {n₂ − n₁;R}: v = {1.34 × 7.8;0.34} = 30.7 mm = 3.1 cm."),
M("JEE Main 2019 · 11 Jan · Shift 1","lens","Moving object and image","An object is at a distance of 20 m from a convex lens of focal length 0.3 m. The lens forms an image of the object. If the object moves away from the lens at a speed of 5 m/s, the speed and direction of the image will be",["2.26 × 10⁻³ m/s away from the lens","3.22 × 10⁻³ m/s towards the lens","1.16 × 10⁻³ m/s towards the lens","0.92 × 10⁻³ m/s away from the lens"],2,"v = {uf;u − f} = {20 × 0.3;19.7} = 0.3046 m.\nImage speed = ({v;u})² × 5 = (0.01523)² × 5 = 1.16 × 10⁻³ m/s.\nWhen the object moves away, the image moves towards the lens (towards the focus)."),
M("JEE Main 2019 · 12 Jan · Shift 1","lens","Combination of lenses","What is the position and nature of image formed by lens combination shown in figure? (f₁, f₂ are focal lengths.) (A convex lens of f₁ = +5 cm and a concave lens of f₂ = −5 cm are 2 cm apart; the object O is 20 cm from the first lens.)",["70 cm from point B at right; real","{20;3} cm from point B at right, real","40 cm from point B at right, real","70 cm from point B at left, virtual"],0,"Lens 1: {1;v} = {1;5} − {1;20}, v = {20;3} = 6.67 cm.\nThis is 6.67 − 2 = 4.67 cm beyond lens 2: u = +{14;3}.\n{1;v} = −{1;5} + {3;14} = {1;70}, so v = 70 cm to the right of the second lens: real."),
M("JEE Main 2019 · 12 Jan · Shift 2","lens","Lens in a liquid","Formation of real image using a biconvex lens is shown here (the object is at 2f from the lens and the screen at 2f on the other side). If the whole set up is immersed in water without disturbing the object and the screen positions, what will one observe on the screen?",["Image disappears","Magnified image","Erect real image","No change"],0,"In water the lens is much weaker ({1;f} falls by a factor of about 4 for glass), so f becomes about 4f.\nThe object is now inside the new focal length; the image is virtual (or far away), so no image forms on the screen."),
M("JEE Main 2019 · 12 Jan · Shift 2","lens","Combination of lenses","A plano-convex lens (focal length f₂, refractive index μ₂, radius of curvature R) fits exactly into a plano-concave lens (focal length f₁, refractive index μ₁, radius of curvature R). Their plane surfaces are parallel to each other. Then, the focal length of the combination will be",["f₁ + f₂","{R;μ₂ − μ₁}","f₁ − f₂","{2f₁f₂;f₁ + f₂}"],1,"The two curved faces coincide, so only the interface between μ₂ and μ₁ bends the light.\n{1;F} = {μ₂ − 1;R} − {μ₁ − 1;R} = {μ₂ − μ₁;R}.\nF = {R;μ₂ − μ₁}."),
M("JEE Main 2019 · 8 Apr · Shift 2","mirror","Lens and mirror together","A convex lens (of focal length 20 cm) and a concave mirror, having their principal axes along the same lines, are kept 80 cm apart from each other. The concave mirror is to the right of the convex lens. When an object is kept at a distance of 30 cm to the left of the convex lens, its image remains at the same position even if the concave mirror is removed. The maximum distance of the object for which this concave mirror, by itself would produce a virtual image would be",["10 cm","25 cm","20 cm","30 cm"],0,"The lens alone images the object 60 cm to its right, that is 20 cm in front of the mirror. The image stays there only if the rays hit the mirror normally, i.e. this point is the mirror's centre of curvature: R = 20 cm, f = 10 cm.\nA concave mirror gives a virtual image only for an object inside its focus, so the maximum distance is 10 cm."),
M("JEE Main 2019 · 9 Apr · Shift 2","lens","Image position and magnification","A convex lens of focal length 20 cm produces images of the same magnification 2 when an object is kept at two distances x₁ and x₂ (x₁ > x₂) from the lens. The ratio of x₁ and x₂ is",["5 : 3","3 : 1","2 : 1","4 : 3"],1,"m = {f;f + u} (u negative). Real image (m = −2): u = −1.5f = −30 cm. Virtual image (m = +2): u = −0.5f = −10 cm.\nx₁ : x₂ = 30 : 10 = 3 : 1."),
M("JEE Main 2019 · 9 Apr · Shift 2","lens","Silvered lens","A thin symmetric convex lens L (refractive index = 1.5) is placed on a plane mirror M. When a pin is placed at A, such that OA = 18 cm, its real inverted image is formed at A itself, as shown in figure. When a liquid of refractive index μₗ is put between the lens and the mirror, the pin has to be moved to A′, such that OA′ = 27 cm, to get its inverted real image at A′ itself. The value of μₗ will be",["√3","√2","{4;3}","{3;2}"],2,"The image falls on the pin when the rays meet the plane mirror normally, so the pin is at the lens's focus: f = 18 cm. For a symmetric lens, {1;18} = 0.5 × {2;R}, so R = 18 cm.\nThe liquid forms a plano-concave lens of power −{μₗ − 1;18}. The combined focal length is 27 cm: {1;27} = {1;18} − {μₗ − 1;18}.\n{2;3} = 2 − μₗ, so μₗ = {4;3}."),
M("JEE Main 2018 · Online","lens","Silvered lens","A planoconvex lens becomes an optical system of 28 cm focal length when its plane surface is silvered and illuminated from left to right as shown in figure (A). If the same lens is instead silvered on the curved surface and illuminated from other side as shown in figure (B), it acts like an optical system of focal length 10 cm. The refractive index of the material of lens is",["1.75","1.51","1.55","1.50"],2,"(A) Plane face silvered: the light crosses the curved face twice, so the system is a mirror with f = {R;2(n − 1)} = 28 cm, i.e. R = 56(n − 1).\n(B) Curved face silvered: light passes the plane face and is reflected by a concave mirror of focal length {R;2} inside the glass, which seen from outside is {R;2n}: {R;2n} = 10, so R = 20n.\n20n = 56(n − 1) gives n = {56;36} = 1.55."),
M("JEE Main 2018 · Online","lens","Combination of lenses","A convergent doublet of separated lenses, corrected for spherical aberration, has resultant focal length of 10 cm. The separation between the two lenses is 2 cm. The focal lengths of the component lenses are",["18 cm, 20 cm","12 cm, 14 cm","16 cm, 18 cm","10 cm, 12 cm"],0,"Correction for spherical aberration needs d = f₁ − f₂ = 2 cm, which all four options satisfy.\nResultant: {1;F} = {1;f₁} + {1;f₂} − {d;f₁f₂}, so F = {f₁f₂;f₁ + f₂ − d}.\n(18, 20): {360;36} = 10 cm. The other pairs give 7, 9 and 6 cm."),
M("JEE Main 2017","lens","Combination of lenses","A diverging lens with magnitude of focal length 25 cm is placed at a distance of 15 cm from a converging lens of magnitude of focal length 20 cm. A beam of parallel light falls on the diverging lens. The final image formed is",["real and at a distance of 40 cm from convergent lens.","virtual and at a distance of 40 cm from convergent lens.","real and at a distance of 40 cm from the divergent lens.","real and at a distance of 6 cm from the convergent lens."],0,"The diverging lens turns the parallel beam into a beam diverging from its focus, 25 cm before it, i.e. 25 + 15 = 40 cm before the converging lens.\nFor the converging lens: u = −40, f = 20, so {1;v} = {1;20} − {1;40}, v = 40 cm: real."),
M("JEE Main 2015 · Online","lens","Silvered lens","A thin convex lens of focal length f is put on a plane mirror as shown in the figure. When an object is kept at a distance a from this lens-mirror combination, its image is formed at a distance {a;3} in front of the combination. The value of a is",["f","2f","3f","{3;2}f"],1,"The light crosses the lens twice, so the combination acts as a concave mirror of focal length {f;2}.\n{1;a} + {3;a} = {2;f}, so a = 2f."),
M("JEE Main 2014","lens","Lens in a liquid","A thin convex lens made from crown glass (μ = {3;2}) has focal length f. When it is measured in two different liquids having refractive indices {4;3} and {5;3}, it has the focal lengths f₁ and f₂ respectively. The correct relation between the focal lengths is",["f₁ and f₂ both become negative","f₁ = f₂ < f","f₁ > f and f₂ becomes negative","f₂ > f and f₁ becomes negative"],2,"{f_liquid;f} = {μ_g − 1;μ_g/μ_l − 1}.\nIn the liquid {4;3}: {0.5;0.125} = 4, so f₁ = 4f > f (still converging).\nIn the liquid {5;3}: {0.5;−0.1} = −5, so f₂ = −5f, which is negative (the lens diverges)."),
M("JEE Main 2014 · Online","lens","Displacement method","An object is located in a fixed position in front of a screen. Sharp image is obtained on the screen for two positions of a thin lens separated by 10 cm. The size of the images in two situations are in the ratio 3 : 2. What is the distance between the screen and the object?",["124.5 cm","144.5 cm","65.0 cm","99.0 cm"],3,"With the object-screen distance D fixed, the two lens positions have u and v swapped, so the magnifications are {v;u} and {u;v}.\n{(v/u);(u/v)} = ({v;u})² = {3;2}, so {v;u} = 1.2247. With v − u = 10: u = 44.5 cm, v = 54.5 cm.\nD = u + v = 99.0 cm."),
M("JEE Main 2013","lens","Lens maker's formula","Diameter of a plano-convex lens is 6 cm and thickness at the centre is 3 mm. If speed of light in material of lens is 2 × 10⁸ m/s, the focal length of the lens is",["10 cm","15 cm","20 cm","30 cm"],3,"n = {3 × 10⁸;2 × 10⁸} = 1.5. The sagitta is 3 mm over a half-chord of 30 mm: R ≈ {30²;2 × 3} = 150 mm.\nf = {R;n − 1} = 300 mm = 30 cm."),
M("JEE Advanced 2013","lens","Lens maker's formula","The image of an object, formed by a plano-convex lens at a distance of 8 m behind the lens, is real and is one-third the size of the object. The wavelength of light inside the lens is {2;3} times the wavelength in free space. The radius of the curved surface of the lens is",["1 m","2 m","3 m","6 m"],2,"λ in the lens is {2;3} of λ in free space, so n = 1.5.\nm = −{1;3} with v = 8 m gives u = −24 m, so {1;f} = {1;8} + {1;24} = {1;6}: f = 6 m.\nPlano-convex: R = (n − 1)f = 0.5 × 6 = 3 m."),
M("AIEEE 2012","lens","Lens and glass plate","An object 2.4 m in front of a lens forms a sharp image on a film 12 cm behind the lens. A glass plate 1 cm thick, of refractive index 1.50 is interposed between lens and film with its plane faces parallel to film. At what distance (from lens) should object be shifted to be in sharp focus on film?",["2.4 m","3.2 m","5.6 m","7.2 m"],2,"{1;f} = {1;12} + {1;240} = {21;240} cm⁻¹.\nThe plate shifts the image 1 × (1 − {1;1.5}) = {1;3} cm farther, so the lens alone must image at 12 − {1;3} = {35;3} cm.\n{1;u} = {21;240} − {3;35}, so u = 560 cm = 5.6 m from the lens."),
M("IIT-JEE 2012","lens","Lens maker's formula","A bi-convex lens is formed with two thin plano-convex lenses as shown in the figure. Refractive index n of the first lens is 1.5 and that of the second lens is 1.2. Both the curved surfaces are of the same radius of curvature R = 14 cm. For this bi-convex lens, for an object distance of 40 cm, the image distance will be",["−280.0 cm","40.0 cm","21.5 cm","13.3 cm"],1,"Thin lenses in contact: {1;f} = {n₁ − 1;R} + {n₂ − 1;R} = {0.5;14} + {0.2;14} = {1;20}.\n{1;v} = {1;20} − {1;40} = {1;40}, so v = 40 cm."),
M("IIT-JEE 2010","lens","Lens and mirror together","A biconvex lens of focal length 15 cm is in front of a plane mirror. The distance between the lens and the mirror is 10 cm. A small object is kept at a distance of 30 cm from the lens. The final image is",["virtual and at a distance of 16 cm from the mirror","real and at a distance of 16 cm from the mirror","virtual and at a distance of 20 cm from the mirror.","real and at a distance of 20 cm from the mirror."],1,"Lens: v = {30 × 15;30 − 15} = 30 cm, so the rays aim at a point 20 cm behind the mirror.\nThe plane mirror forms a real image 20 cm in front of it, 10 cm to the left of the lens. For the returning rays, this is a virtual object at 10 cm: {1;v} = {1;15} + {1;10}, v = 6 cm to the left of the lens.\nThat is 10 + 6 = 16 cm from the mirror, and it is real."),
M("AIEEE 2007","lens","Combination of lenses","Two lenses of power −15 D and +5 D are in contact with each other. The focal length of the combination is",["+10 cm","−20 cm","−10 cm","+20 cm"],2,"P = −15 + 5 = −10 D, so f = −{1;10} m = −10 cm."),
M("IIT-JEE 2006","lens","Silvered lens","Focal length of the plano-convex lens is 15 cm. A small object is placed at A, 20 cm from the lens as shown in the figure (the curved face towards the object). The plane surface is silvered. The image will form at",["60 cm to the left of lens","12 cm to the left of lens","60 cm to the right of lens","30 cm to the left of lens"],1,"The light crosses the lens twice and the plane mirror adds no power: the system is a concave mirror of focal length {15;2} = 7.5 cm.\n{1;v} = −{1;7.5} + {1;20} = −{1;12}, so v = −12 cm: the image is 12 cm to the left of the lens."),
M("IIT-JEE 2005","lens","Combination of lenses","A convex lens is in contact with concave lens. The magnitude of the ratio of their focal lengths is {2;3}. Their equivalent focal length is 30 cm. What are their individual focal lengths? (Options give the focal length of the concave lens, then of the convex lens.)",["−15, 10","−10, 15","75, 50","−75, 50"],0,"The convex lens must be stronger, so |f_concave| = 1.5 f_convex. Then {1;30} = {1;f} − {1;1.5f} = {1;3f}.\nf_convex = 10 cm and f_concave = −15 cm."),
M("AIEEE 2004","lens","Silvered lens","A plano convex lens of refractive index 1.5 and radius of curvature 30 cm is silvered at the curved surface. Now this lens has been used to form the image of an object. At what distance from this lens an object be placed in order to have a real image of the size of the object?",["20 cm","30 cm","60 cm","80 cm"],0,"Light enters through the plane face, so the object appears at n × u inside the glass. A real image of the same size needs the object at the centre of curvature of the silvered surface: nu = R.\nu = {R;n} = {30;1.5} = 20 cm."),
M("IIT-JEE 2003","lens","Combination of lenses","The size of the image of an object, which is at infinity, as formed by a convex lens of focal length 30 cm is 2 cm. If a concave lens of focal length 20 cm is placed between the convex lens and the image at a distance of 26 cm from the convex lens, calculate the new size of the image.",["1.25 cm","2.5 cm","1.05 cm","2 cm"],1,"The first image is 30 cm from the convex lens, so 4 cm beyond the concave lens: a virtual object, u = +4 cm.\n{1;v} = −{1;20} + {1;4}, v = 5 cm. m = {v;u} = {5;4} = 1.25.\nNew size = 2 × 1.25 = 2.5 cm."),
M("IIT-JEE 2000","lens","Lens in a liquid","A hollow double concave lens is made of very thin transparent material. It can be filled with air or either of two liquids L₁ or L₂ having refractive indices μ₁ and μ₂ respectively (μ₂ > μ₁ > 1). The lens will diverge a parallel beam of light if it is filled with",["air and placed in air","air and immersed in L₁","L₁ and immersed in L₂","L₂ and immersed in L₁."],3,"A double concave shape diverges light only when the material inside has a higher index than the surroundings, as for glass in air.\nAir in air does nothing; air in L₁ and L₁ in L₂ have a lower index inside, so they converge.\nL₂ (μ₂ > μ₁) inside L₁ diverges."),
M("IIT-JEE 1999","lens","Lens in a liquid","A concave lens of glass, refractive index 1.5, has both surfaces of same radius of curvature R. On immersion in a medium of refractive index 1.75, it will behave as a",["convergent lens of focal length 3.5R","convergent lens of focal length 3.0R","divergent lens of focal length 3.5R","divergent lens of focal length 3.0R."],0,"{1;f} = ({1.5;1.75} − 1)({1;−R} − {1;R}) = (−{1;7})(−{2;R}) = {2;7R}.\nPositive: a convergent lens with f = 3.5R."),
M("IIT-JEE 1997","lens","Combination of lenses","An eye specialist prescribes spectacles having combination of convex lens of focal length 40 cm in contact with a concave lens of focal length 25 cm. The power of this lens combination, in diopter, is",["+1.5","−1.5","+6.67","−6.67."],1,"P = {1;0.40} − {1;0.25} = 2.5 − 4 = −1.5 D."),
M("JEE Advanced 2024","refr","Silvered lens","A glass beaker has a solid, plano-convex base of refractive index 1.60, as shown in the figure. The radius of curvature of the convex surface (SPU) is 9 cm, while the planar surface (STU) acts as a mirror. This beaker is filled with a liquid of refractive index n up to the level QPR (the liquid surface touches the top P of the convex surface). If the image of a point object O at a height of h above P is formed onto itself, then, which of the following option(s) is(are) correct?",["For n = 1.42, h = 50 cm.","For n = 1.35, h = 36 cm.","For n = 1.45, h = 65 cm.","For n = 1.48, h = 85 cm."],[0,1],"Near the axis the liquid is a thin plano-concave lens on the glass lens. The image falls on O only if the rays strike the plane mirror normally, i.e. travel parallel to the axis inside the glass.\nSeen from the liquid, O is at apparent height nh. Refraction at the curved surface (liquid to glass) with the image at infinity: {1.6;∞} − {n;−nh} = {1.6 − n;9}, so h = {9;1.6 − n}.\nn = 1.42: h = 50 cm. n = 1.35: h = 36 cm. n = 1.45 would need 60 cm and n = 1.48 would need 75 cm. So (a) and (b)."),
M("JEE Advanced 2019","lens","Lens maker's formula","A thin convex lens is made of two materials with refractive indices n₁ and n₂, as shown in figure (the two materials form two plano-convex halves in contact, n₁ on the left and n₂ on the right, with a flat boundary perpendicular to the axis). The radius of curvature of the left and right spherical surfaces are equal. f is the focal length of the lens when n₁ = n₂ = n. The focal length is f + Δf when n₁ = n and n₂ = n + Δn. Assuming Δn ≪ (n − 1) and 1 < n < 2, the correct statement(s) is/are",["If {Δn;n} < 0 then {Δf;f} > 0","For n = 1.5, Δn = 10⁻³ and f = 20 cm, the value of |Δf| will be 0.02 cm (round off to 2ⁿᵈ decimal place).","|{Δf;f}| < |{Δn;n}|","The relation between {Δf;f} and {Δn;n} remains unchanged if both the convex surfaces are replaced by concave surfaces of the same radius of curvature."],[0,1,3],"Two plano-convex lenses in contact: {1;f} = {(n₁ − 1) + (n₂ − 1);R}. At n₁ = n₂ = n: {1;f} = {2(n − 1);R}. Changing n₂ by Δn changes {1;f} by {Δn;R}.\nSo −{Δf;f²} = {Δn;R}, and {Δf;f} = −{Δn;2(n − 1)}.\n(a) Δn < 0 gives Δf > 0: true. (b) |Δf| = {20 × 10⁻³;1} = 0.02 cm: true.\n(c) Since 2(n − 1) < n for n < 2, |Δf/f| > |Δn/n|: false.\n(d) With concave surfaces f = −{R;2(n − 1)} and Δ({1;f}) = −{Δn;R}, which again gives {Δf;f} = −{Δn;2(n − 1)}: true."),
M("IIT-JEE 1998","lens","Aberrations","A real image of a distant object is formed by a plano-convex lens on its principal axis. Spherical aberration",["is absent","is smaller if the curved surface of the lens faces the object","is smaller if the plane surface of the lens faces the object","is the same whichever side of the lens faces the object."],1,"For a parallel beam, the plano-convex lens refracts the rays most evenly when the curved surface faces the object: the bending is shared between the two faces and the marginal rays are brought closer to the paraxial focus.\nWith the plane face towards the object all the bending occurs at the curved exit face and the aberration is larger."),
M("IIT-JEE 1996","lens","Image position and magnification","Which of the following form(s) a virtual and erect image for all positions of the object?",["Convex lens","Concave lens","Convex mirror","Concave mirror."],[1,2],"A concave lens and a convex mirror are always diverging, so the image of a real object is always virtual, erect and smaller.\nA convex lens or concave mirror gives a real, inverted image when the object is beyond the focus."),
M("IIT-JEE 1995","lens","Image position and magnification","A diminished image of an object is to be obtained on a screen 1.0 m from it. This can be achieved by appropriately placing",["a concave mirror of suitable focal length","a convex mirror of suitable focal length","a convex lens of focal length less than 0.25 m","a concave lens of suitable focal length"],[0,2],"A real image on a screen needs a converging element: a convex mirror or concave lens only gives virtual images.\nA concave mirror with the object beyond its centre of curvature forms a real diminished image. A convex lens needs object-screen distance D ≥ 4f, so f ≤ 0.25 m (two positions exist; one is diminished)."),
M("IIT-JEE 1993","lens","Combination of lenses","Two thin convex lenses of focal lengths f₁ and f₂ are separated by a horizontal distance d (where d < f₁, d < f₂) and their centres are displaced by a vertical separation Δ as shown in the figure (the centre of the second lens is Δ above the axis of the first lens). Taking the origin of coordinates O, at the centre of the first lens, the x and y coordinates of the focal point of this lens system, for a parallel beam of rays coming from the left along the axis of the first lens, are given by",["x = {f₁f₂;f₁ + f₂}, y = Δ","x = {f₁(f₂ + d);f₁ + f₂ − d}, y = {Δ;f₁ + f₂}","x = {f₁f₂ + d(f₁ − d);f₁ + f₂ − d}, y = {Δ(f₁ − d);f₁ + f₂ − d}","x = {f₁f₂ + d(f₁ − d);f₁ + f₂ − d}, y = 0."],2,"The first lens focuses the beam at (f₁, 0), a virtual object for the second lens at u = f₁ − d beyond it, and a height Δ below its axis.\n{1;v} = {1;f₂} + {1;f₁ − d}, so v = {f₂(f₁ − d);f₁ + f₂ − d} and the magnification is m = {v;u} = {f₂;f₁ + f₂ − d}.\nx = d + v = {f₁f₂ + d(f₁ − d);f₁ + f₂ − d}.\nThe image is at −mΔ from the second axis, so y = Δ − {Δf₂;f₁ + f₂ − d} = {Δ(f₁ − d);f₁ + f₂ − d}."),
M("IIT-JEE 1989","inst","Astronomical telescope","An astronomical telescope has an angular magnification of magnitude 5 for distant objects. The separation between the objective and the eyepiece is 36 cm and the final image is formed at infinity. The focal length f_o of the objective and the focal length f_e of the eyepiece are",["f_o = 45 cm and f_e = −9 cm","f_o = 50 cm and f_e = 10 cm","f_o = 7.2 cm and f_e = 5 cm","f_o = 30 cm and f_e = 6 cm"],3,"Normal adjustment: f_o + f_e = 36 cm and M = {f_o;f_e} = 5.\nf_e = 6 cm and f_o = 30 cm."),
M("IIT-JEE 1986","lens","Image position and magnification","A converging lens is used to form an image on a screen. When the upper half of the lens is covered by an opaque screen",["half the image will disappear","complete image will be formed","intensity of the image will increase","intensity of the image will decrease."],[1,3],"Every part of the lens forms the whole image: rays from each object point pass through the lower half too, and meet at the same image point.\nHalf the light is cut off, so the image is complete but dimmer."),
M("IIT-JEE 1982","lens","Combination of lenses","A convex lens of focal length 40 cm is in contact with a concave lens of focal length 25 cm. The power of the combination is",["−1.5 dioptre","−6.5 dioptre","+6.5 dioptre","+6.67 dioptre."],0,"P = {1;0.40} − {1;0.25} = 2.5 − 4 = −1.5 D."),
N("JEE Main 2024 · 1 Feb · Shift 1","lens","Image position and magnification","The distance between object and its 3 times magnified virtual image as produced by a convex lens is 20 cm. The focal length of the lens used is ______ cm.",15,"A virtual image is farther than the object on the same side: v = 3u and v − u = 20, so u = 10 cm, v = 30 cm.\n{1;f} = {1;10} − {1;30} = {1;15}, f = 15 cm."),
N("JEE Main 2024 · 30 Jan · Shift 1","lens","Image position and magnification","The distance between object and its two times magnified real image as produced by a convex lens is 45 cm. The focal length of the lens used is ______ cm.",10,"A real image 2 times larger: v = 2u on the other side, so u + v = 3u = 45: u = 15 cm, v = 30 cm.\n{1;f} = {1;30} + {1;15} = {1;10}, f = 10 cm."),
N("JEE Main 2024 · 31 Jan · Shift 2","refr","Refraction at a spherical surface","Light from a point source in air falls on a convex curved surface of radius 20 cm and refractive index 1.5. If the source is located at 100 cm from the convex surface, the image will be formed at ______ cm from the object.",200,"{1.5;v} − {1;−100} = {0.5;20}, so {1.5;v} = 0.025 − 0.01 = 0.015: v = 100 cm beyond the surface.\nFrom the object: 100 + 100 = 200 cm."),
N("JEE Main 2023 · 10 Apr · Shift 2","lens","Combination of lenses","A point object, 'O' is placed in front of two thin symmetrical coaxial convex lenses L₁ and L₂ with focal length 24 cm and 9 cm respectively. The distance between two lenses is 10 cm and the object is placed 6 cm away from lens L₁ as shown in the figure. The distance between the object and the image formed by the system of two lenses is ______ cm.",34,"L₁: u = −6, f = 24: {1;v} = {1;24} − {1;6}, v = −8 cm (virtual).\nFor L₂ the object is 8 + 10 = 18 cm away: {1;v} = {1;9} − {1;18}, v = 18 cm beyond L₂.\nDistance from O = 6 + 10 + 18 = 34 cm."),
N("JEE Main 2023 · 11 Apr · Shift 1","lens","Lens in a liquid","The radius of curvature of each surface of a convex lens having refractive index 1.8 is 20 cm. The lens is now immersed in a liquid of refractive index 1.5. The ratio of power of lens in air to its power in the liquid will be x : 1. The value of x is ______.",4,"P ∝ ({n_lens;n_medium} − 1). In air: 0.8. In the liquid: {1.8;1.5} − 1 = 0.2.\nx = {0.8;0.2} = 4."),
N("JEE Main 2023 · 12 Apr · Shift 1","lens","Combination of lenses","Two convex lenses of focal length 20 cm each are placed coaxially with a separation of 60 cm between them. The image of the distant object formed by the combination is at ______ cm from the first lens.",100,"L₁ focuses the distant object 20 cm beyond itself, i.e. 40 cm before L₂.\nL₂: u = −40, f = 20: {1;v} = {1;20} − {1;40}, v = 40 cm.\nFrom the first lens: 60 + 40 = 100 cm."),
N("JEE Main 2023 · 13 Apr · Shift 2","lens","Cut lenses","A bi convex lens of focal length 10 cm is cut in two identical parts along a plane perpendicular to the principal axis. The power of each lens after cut is ______ D.",5,"The original power is {1;0.10} = 10 D. Each half is a plano-convex lens with one curved surface, so it has half the power: 5 D."),
N("JEE Main 2023 · 24 Jan · Shift 1","lens","Combination of lenses","As shown in the figure, a combination of a thin plano concave lens and a thin plano convex lens is used to image an object placed at infinity. The radius of curvature of both the lenses is 30 cm and refraction index of the material for both the lenses is 1.75. Both the lenses are placed at distance of 40 cm from each other. Due to the combination, the image of the object is formed at distance x = ______ cm, from concave lens.",120,"f_concave = −{30;0.75} = −40 cm and f_convex = +40 cm.\nThe concave lens gives a virtual image 40 cm before it, which is 80 cm before the convex lens: u = −80.\n{1;v} = {1;40} − {1;80} = {1;80}, so v = 80 cm beyond the convex lens: x = 40 + 80 = 120 cm."),
N("JEE Main 2023 · 25 Jan · Shift 2","lens","Lens and mirror together","An object is placed on the principal axis of a convex lens of focal length 10 cm as shown (object on the left). A plane mirror is placed on the other side of the lens at a distance of 20 cm. The image produced by the plane mirror is 5 cm inside the mirror. The distance of the object from the lens is ______ cm.",30,"The plane mirror's image is 5 cm behind it, so the lens's image (the object for the mirror) is 5 cm in front of the mirror: 20 − 5 = 15 cm from the lens.\n{1;u} = {1;v} − {1;f} = {1;15} − {1;10} = −{1;30}.\nThe object is 30 cm from the lens."),
A("JEE Advanced 2023","mirror","Lens and mirror together","An optical arrangement consists of two concave mirrors M₁ and M₂, and a convex lens L with a common principal axis, as shown in the figure (M₁, L, M₂ in that order). The focal length of L is 10 cm. The radii of curvature of M₁ and M₂ are 20 cm and 24 cm, respectively. The distance between L and M₂ is 20 cm. A point object S is placed at the mid-point between L and M₂ on the axis. When the distance between L and M₁ is {n;7} cm, one of the images coincides with S. The value of n is ______.","n = 220, or 150, or 80 (each gives an image on S)","Follow the light that starts towards M₂ (f = 12 cm, S 10 cm from it): {1;v} = −{1;12} + {1;10}, giving a virtual image 60 cm behind M₂.\nFor the lens these rays are an object 80 cm away: v = {80 × 10;70} = 11.43 cm, a real image on the M₁ side of L.\nIf M₁ is placed so that this point is at its centre of curvature (20 cm from M₁), the rays retrace the whole path and finish on S: d = 11.43 + 20 = {220;7}, n = 220.\nIf the point is at the pole of M₁ (d = {80;7}), the rays are reflected back from it and again retrace to S: n = 80.\nIf the point is at the focus of M₁ (10 cm from it, d = {150;7}), the reflected beam is parallel and the lens focuses it at 10 cm, exactly on S: n = 150."),
N("JEE Main 2022 · 25 Jul · Shift 2","mirror","Lens and mirror together","A convex lens of focal length 20 cm is placed in front of a convex mirror with principal axis coinciding each other. The distance between the lens and mirror is 10 cm. A point object is placed on principal axis at a distance of 60 cm from the convex lens. The image formed by combination coincides the object itself. The focal length of the convex mirror is ______ cm.",10,"Lens: u = −60, f = 20 gives v = 30 cm, i.e. 20 cm beyond the mirror (a virtual object for it).\nThe image coincides with the object only if the rays hit the mirror normally, i.e. aim at its centre of curvature: R = 20 cm.\nf = {R;2} = 10 cm."),
N("JEE Main 2022 · 26 Jul · Shift 1","lens","Lens maker's formula","The graph between {1;u} and {1;v} for a thin convex lens in order to determine its focal length is plotted as shown in the figure (a straight line of slope 1; it meets the {1;v} axis at 0.10 cm⁻¹ and the {1;u} axis at −0.10 cm⁻¹). The refractive index of the lens is 1.5 and its both the surfaces have same radius of curvature R. The value of R will be ______ cm. (Where u = object distance, v = image distance)",10,"{1;v} = {1;f} + {1;u}, so the {1;v} intercept is {1;f} = 0.10 cm⁻¹ and f = 10 cm.\nBiconvex with equal radii: {1;f} = (n − 1){2;R}, so R = 2(n − 1)f = 2 × 0.5 × 10 = 10 cm."),
N("JEE Main 2022 · 28 Jul · Shift 2","lens","Newton's formula","In an experiment with a convex lens, the plot of the image distance (v′) against the object distance (u′) measured from the focus gives a curve v′u′ = 225. If all the distances are measured in cm, the magnitude of the focal length of the lens is ______ cm.",15,"Newton's relation: the distances measured from the two foci satisfy u′v′ = f².\nf² = 225, so f = 15 cm."),
N("JEE Main 2022 · 24 Jun · Shift 1","lens","Lens in a liquid","Two identical thin biconvex lenses of focal length 15 cm and refractive index 1.5 are in contact with each other. The space between the lenses is filled with a liquid of refractive index 1.25. The focal length of the combination is ______ cm.",10,"Each lens has {1;15} = 0.5 × {2;R}, so R = 15 cm.\nSurface powers in turn: air to glass {0.5;15}; glass to liquid {0.25;15}; liquid to glass {0.25;15}; glass to air {0.5;15}.\nTotal = {1.5;15} = {1;10}, so f = 10 cm."),
N("JEE Advanced 2022","lens","Image position and magnification","A rod of length 2 cm makes an angle {2π;3} rad with the principal axis of a thin convex lens. The lens has a focal length of 10 cm and is placed at a distance of {40;3} cm from the object, as shown in the figure (one end of the rod is on the axis at {40;3} cm from the lens, and the rod's other end is farther from the lens). The height of the image is {30√3;13} cm and the angle made by it with respect to the principal axis is α rad. The value of α is {π;n} rad, where n is ______.",6,"End A is on the axis at u = −{40;3}: v = 40 cm.\nEnd B is 1 cm farther along the axis and √3 cm above it (a rod of length 2 cm at 120°): u = −{43;3}, v = {430;13}, m = −{30;13}, so its image is {30√3;13} below the axis, as given.\nA′B′ runs from (40, 0) to ({430;13}, −{30√3;13}): Δx = −{90;13}, Δy = −{30√3;13}.\ntan α = {30√3;90} = {1;√3}, so α = {π;6} and n = 6."),
N("JEE Main 2021 · 26 Aug · Shift 2","lens","Lens and mirror together","An object is placed at a distance of 12 cm from a convex lens. A convex mirror of focal length 15 cm is placed on other side of lens at 8 cm as shown in the figure. Image of object coincides with the object. When the convex mirror is removed, a real and inverted image is formed at a position. The distance of the image from the object will be ______ cm.",50,"The image coincides with the object only if the rays meet the convex mirror normally, i.e. aim at its centre of curvature, R = 30 cm behind it.\nSo the lens image is at 8 + 30 = 38 cm from the lens.\nWith the mirror removed this is the real image: distance from the object = 12 + 38 = 50 cm."),
N("JEE Main 2021 · 25 Feb · Shift 1","lens","Image position and magnification","The same size images formed by a convex lens when the object is placed at 20 cm or at 10 cm from the lens. The focal length of convex lens is ______ cm.",15,"At 20 cm the image is real (m = {f;f − 20}), at 10 cm it is virtual (m = {f;f − 10}). Equal sizes with opposite sign:\nf − 20 = −(f − 10), so 2f = 30 and f = 15 cm."),
N("JEE Main 2020 · 4 Sep · Shift 2","lens","Displacement method","The distance between an object and a screen is 100 cm. A lens can produce real image of the object on the screen for two different positions between the screen and the object. The distance between these two positions is 40 cm. If the power of the lens is close to ({N;100}) D where N is an integer, the value of N is ______.",476,"Displacement method: f = {D² − d²;4D} = {10000 − 1600;400} = 21 cm.\nPower = {1;0.21} = 4.76 D = {476;100} D, so N = 476."),
N("JEE Main 2020 · 8 Jan · Shift 1","lens","Lens maker's formula","A point object in air is in front of the curved surface of a plano-convex lens. The radius of curvature of the curved surface is 30 cm and the refractive index of the lens material is 1.5, then the focal length of the lens (in cm) is ______.",60,"{1;f} = (n − 1)({1;R} − {1;∞}) = {0.5;30}, so f = 60 cm."),
N("JEE Advanced 2015","mirror","Lens and mirror together","Consider a concave mirror and a convex lens (refractive index = 1.5) of focal length 10 cm each, separated by a distance of 50 cm in air (refractive index = 1) as shown in the figure (mirror on the left; the object is between them). An object is placed at a distance of 15 cm from the mirror. Its erect image formed by this combination has magnification M₁. When the set-up is kept in a medium of refractive index {7;6}, the magnification becomes M₂. The magnitude |{M₂;M₁}| is ______.",7,"Mirror (f = −10): u = −15 gives v = −30, m = −2. The image is 20 cm from the lens.\nLens in air: u = −20, f = 10 gives v = 20, m = −1. So M₁ = (−2)(−1) = 2 (erect).\nIn the medium the lens power changes by the factor (1.5/(7/6) − 1)/0.5 = 4/7, so f′ = 10 × 7/4 = 17.5 cm; the mirror is unchanged.\nLens: u = −20, f′ = 17.5 gives v = 140, m = −7, so M₂ = 14 and |M₂/M₁| = 7."),
N("IIT-JEE 2010","lens","Image position and magnification","The focal length of a thin biconvex lens is 20 cm. When an object is moved from a distance of 25 cm in front of it to 50 cm, the magnification of its image changes from m₂₅ to m₅₀. The ratio {m₂₅;m₅₀} is ______.",6,"m = {f;f + u} with u negative. m₂₅ = {20;20 − 25} = −4 and m₅₀ = {20;20 − 50} = −{2;3}.\nRatio = {−4;−2/3} = 6."),
M("JEE Main 2024 · 9 Apr · Shift 1","lens","Image position and magnification","Given below are two statements:\nStatement (I): When an object is placed at the centre of curvature of a concave lens, image is formed at the centre of curvature of the lens on the other side.\nStatement (II): Concave lens always forms a virtual and erect image.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below",["Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Statement I is true but Statement II is false.","Statement I is false but Statement II is true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false."],2,"For a concave lens with f = −{R;2(n − 1)} and an object at u = −R: {1;v} = −{2(n − 1);R} − {1;R} = −{2n − 1;R}.\nSo v = −{R;2n − 1}: the image is on the same side as the object, not on the other side, so Statement I is false.\nA concave lens always gives a virtual, erect, diminished image of a real object, so Statement II is true."),
A("IIT-JEE 2004","lens","Moving object and image","An object is moving with velocity 0.01 m/s towards a convex lens of focal length 0.3 m. Find the magnitude of rate of separation of image from the lens when the object is at a distance of 0.4 m from the lens. Also calculate the magnitude of rate of change of the lateral magnification.","The image moves away from the lens at 0.09 m/s; the magnification (magnitude 3 at that instant) increases at 0.3 s⁻¹","With u = 0.4 m: v = {uf;u − f} = 1.2 m and |m| = {f;u − f} = 3.\n{dv;du} = −{f²;(u − f)²} = −9, so the image speed is 9 × 0.01 = 0.09 m/s, moving away as the object approaches.\n{d|m|;du} = −{f;(u − f)²} = −30 m⁻¹, so |m| changes at 30 × 0.01 = 0.3 s⁻¹ (increasing)."),
A("IIT-JEE 2001","lens","Silvered lens","A thin biconvex lens of refractive index 3/2 is placed on a horizontal plane mirror. The space between the lens and the mirror is then filled with water of refractive index 4/3. It is found that when a point object is placed 15 cm above the lens on its principal axis, the object coincides with its own image. On repeating with another liquid, the object and the image again coincide at a distance 25 cm from the lens. Calculate the refractive index of the liquid.","1.6","The image coincides with the object when the rays strike the plane mirror normally, so the object is at the focus of the lens-plus-liquid system.\nWith liquid of index μ the glass lens has power {1;R} (equal radii) and the liquid forms a plano-concave lens of power −{μ − 1;R}: {1;F} = {2 − μ;R}.\nWater: {2 − 4/3;R} = {1;15} gives R = 10 cm. The other liquid: {2 − μ;10} = {1;25}, so μ = 1.6."),
A("IIT-JEE 2000","mirror","Lens and mirror together","A convex lens of focal length 15 cm and a concave mirror of focal length 30 cm are kept with their optic axes PQ and RS parallel but separated in vertical direction by 0.6 cm (RS is 0.6 cm below PQ), as shown in the figure. The distance between the lens and mirror is 30 cm. An upright object AB of height 1.2 cm (A the top, B on the axis PQ) is placed on the optic axis PQ of the lens at a distance of 20 cm from the lens. If A′B′ is the image after refraction from the lens and reflection from the mirror, find the distance of A′B′ from the pole of the mirror and obtain its magnification. Also locate positions of A′ and B′ with respect to the optic axis RS.","A′B′ is real, 15 cm in front of the mirror; magnification −1.5 (inverted, 1.8 cm tall); B′ is 0.3 cm above RS and A′ is 1.5 cm below RS","Lens: u = −20, f = 15 gives v = 60 cm (30 cm beyond the mirror) and m = −3: the image is 3.6 cm tall, inverted, with B₁ on PQ and A₁ 3.6 cm below it.\nRelative to RS: B₁ is +0.6 cm and A₁ is −3.0 cm. The image is a virtual object 30 cm behind the mirror: {1;v} = −{1;30} − {1;30}, v = −15 cm (real) and m = +0.5.\nB′ = 0.5 × 0.6 = +0.3 cm; A′ = 0.5 × (−3.0) = −1.5 cm. Total magnification (−3)(0.5) = −1.5."),
A("IIT-JEE 1997","lens","Lens and mirror together","A thin equiconvex lens of glass of refractive index μ = 3/2 and of focal length 0.3 m in air is sealed into an opening at one end of a tank filled with water (μ = 4/3). On the opposite side of the lens, a mirror is placed inside the tank on the tank wall perpendicular to the lens axis, as shown in figure. The separation between the lens and the mirror is 0.8 m. A small object is placed outside the tank in front of the lens at a distance of 0.9 m from the lens along its axis. Find the position (relative to the lens) of the image of the object formed by the system.","A virtual image 0.9 m from the lens, inside the tank (on the mirror side)","In air {1;0.3} = 0.5 × {2;R}, so R = 0.3 m. With water on the far side, the lens power is {0.5;0.3} + {4/3 − 1.5;−0.3} = 2.22 m⁻¹.\nGoing in: {4/3;v} = 2.22 − {1;0.9} gives v = 1.2 m, a converging beam aimed 0.4 m behind the mirror. The plane mirror makes a real image 0.4 m in front of it, i.e. 0.4 m from the lens.\nComing back (water to air), the object is 0.4 m away: {1;v} = 2.22 − {4/3;0.4} = −1.11, so v = −0.9 m."),
A("IIT-JEE 1996","lens","Cut lenses","A thin plano-convex lens of focal length f is split into two halves. One of the halves is shifted along the optical axis. The separation between object and image planes is 1.8 m. The magnification of the image formed by one of the half-lenses is 2. Find the focal length of the lens and separation between the two halves.","f = 0.4 m; the two halves are 0.6 m apart","Each half has the same focal length f and both images lie in the same plane. Take the half with m = −2: v = 2u and u + v = 1.8 m, so u = 0.6 m and v = 1.2 m. f = {uv;u + v} = 0.4 m.\nFor the other half to image the object in the same plane it must sit at the other conjugate position, u′ = 1.2 m (v′ = 0.6 m, m = −0.5).\nSeparation = 1.2 − 0.6 = 0.6 m."),
A("IIT-JEE 1984","lens","Lens maker's formula","A plano convex lens has a thickness of 4 cm. When placed on a horizontal table, with the curved surface in contact with it, the apparent depth of the lens is found to be 3 cm (the bottom-most point of the lens, seen through the plane face). If the lens is inverted such that the plane face is in contact with the table, the apparent depth of the centre of the curved surface is found to be {25;8} cm. Find the focal length of the lens.","75 cm","Plane face up: apparent depth {4;n} = 3, so n = {4;3}.\nPlane face down, looking through the curved face (glass to air, R measured from the light's direction: −R): {1;v} − {4/3;−4} = {1 − 4/3;−R} with v = −{25;8}: −{8;25} + {1;3} = {1;3R}, so {1;3R} = {1;75} and R = 25 cm.\nf = {R;n − 1} = {25;1/3} = 75 cm."),
A("IIT-JEE 1981","lens","Silvered lens","The convex surface of a thin concavo-convex lens of glass of refractive index 1.5 has a radius of curvature 20 cm. The concave surface has a radius of curvature 60 cm. The convex side is silvered and placed on a horizontal surface (concave face up).\n(i) Where should a pin be placed on the optic axis such that its image is formed at the same place?\n(ii) If the concave part is filled with water of refractive index {4;3}, find the distance through which the pin should be moved so that the image of the pin again coincides with the pin.","(i) 15 cm above the lens   (ii) about 1.15 cm closer to the lens (the pin at about 13.85 cm)","(i) The image coincides with the pin when the rays hit the silvered convex surface normally, i.e. diverge from its centre of curvature 20 cm above the lens: v = −20 cm after the concave face (R = −60 cm, light going down): {1.5;−20} − {1;u} = {0.5;−60}, so u = −15 cm.\n(ii) With water in the bowl, refraction is from water to glass: {1.5;−20} − {4/3;u′} = {1.5 − 4/3;−60}, so u′ = −18.46 cm in water terms.\nA pin in air at height h looks {4;3}h up in water terms: h = {3;4} × 18.46 = 13.85 cm. The pin moves 15 − 13.85 ≈ 1.15 cm closer."),
A("IIT-JEE 1979","lens","Silvered lens","The radius of curvature of the convex face of a plano-convex lens is 12 cm and its μ = 1.5.\n(a) Find the focal length of the lens.\nThe plane face of the lens is now silvered.\n(b) At what distance from the lens will parallel rays incident on the convex surface converge?\n(c) Calculate the image distance when the object is placed on the axis 20 cm from the lens.","(a) 24 cm   (b) 12 cm in front of the lens   (c) 30 cm in front of the lens (real)","(a) {1;f} = (1.5 − 1){1;12}, so f = 24 cm.\n(b) The light crosses the lens twice and the silvered plane face adds no power, so the system is a concave mirror of focal length {24;2} = 12 cm.\n(c) {1;v} = −{1;12} + {1;20} = −{1;30}: the image is 30 cm in front of the lens, real."),
A("IIT-JEE 1978","lens","Silvered lens","A pin is placed 10 cm in front of a convex lens of focal length 20 cm and made of a material of refractive index 1.5. The surface of the lens farther away from the pin is silvered and has a radius of curvature of 22 cm. Determine the position of the final image. Is the image real or virtual?","11 cm in front of the lens (on the pin's side); real","From {1;20} = 0.5({1;R₁} + {1;22}): the first surface has {n − 1;R₁} = 0.0545 cm⁻¹; the second has {n − 1;R₂} = 0.0227 cm⁻¹.\nThe light crosses the first surface twice and is reflected at the silvered one (power {2n;R₂} = {3;22}): P = 2(0.0545) + 0.1364 = 0.1909 cm⁻¹, a concave mirror of focal length 5.24 cm.\n{1;v} = −0.1909 + {1;10} = −{1;11}, so the image is 11 cm in front of the lens: real."),
M("IIT-JEE 1988","lens","Aberrations","A parallel beam of white light falls on a combination of a concave and a convex lens, both of the same material. Their focal lengths are 15 cm and 30 cm respectively for the mean wavelength in white light. On the other side of the lens system, one sees coloured patterns with violet colour at the outer edge. (True or False)",["True","False"],0,"The combined power at the mean wavelength is {1;30} − {1;15} = −{1;30} cm⁻¹, so the system diverges. Both lenses are of the same glass, so the net power is proportional to (n − 1).\nViolet has the larger n, so it diverges the most and ends up at the outer edge. The statement is true."),
M("IIT-JEE 1983","lens","Combination of lenses","A convex lens of focal length 1 metre and a concave lens of focal length 0.25 metre are kept 0.75 metre apart. A parallel beam of light first passes through the convex lens, then through the concave lens and comes to a focus 0.5 m away from the concave lens. (True or False)",["True","False"],1,"The convex lens focuses the beam 1 m beyond itself, i.e. 0.25 m beyond the concave lens: a virtual object at u = +0.25 m.\n{1;v} = −{1;0.25} + {1;0.25} = 0, so the beam emerges parallel and does not come to a focus. The statement is false."),
A("IIT-JEE 1997","lens","Combination of lenses","Two thin lenses, when in contact, produce a combination of power +10 dioptre. When they are 0.25 m apart, the power reduces to +6 dioptre. The focal lengths of the lenses are ______ m and ______ m.","0.125 m and 0.5 m","P₁ + P₂ = 10. Separated by d: P = P₁ + P₂ − dP₁P₂ = 6, so 0.25 P₁P₂ = 4 and P₁P₂ = 16.\nP₁ and P₂ are the roots of x² − 10x + 16 = 0: 8 D and 2 D.\nThe focal lengths are {1;8} = 0.125 m and {1;2} = 0.5 m."),
N("IIT-JEE 1987","lens","Lens in a liquid","A thin lens of refractive index 1.5 has a focal length of 15 cm in air. When the lens is placed in a medium of refractive index {4;3}, its focal length will become ______ cm.",60,"{f_m;f_a} = {n − 1;n/n_m − 1} = {0.5;1.125 − 1} = 4.\nf_m = 4 × 15 = 60 cm."),
N("IIT-JEE 1985","lens","Combination of lenses","A convex lens A of focal length 20 cm and a concave lens B of focal length 5 cm are kept along the same axis with a distance d between them. If a parallel beam of light falling on A leaves B as a parallel beam, then d is equal to ______ cm.",15,"A parallel beam in and out means the pair is afocal: the focus of A must coincide with the focus of B.\nd = f_A − |f_B| = 20 − 5 = 15 cm."),
M("JEE Main 2025 · 3 Apr · Shift 1","prism","Minimum deviation","Consider following statements for refraction of light through prism, when angle of deviation is minimum.\n(A) The refracted ray inside prism becomes parallel to the base.\n(B) Larger angle prisms provide smaller angle of minimum deviation.\n(C) Angle of incidence and angle of emergence becomes equal.\n(D) There are always two sets of angle of incidence for which deviation will be same except at minimum deviation setting.\n(E) Angle of refraction becomes double of prism angle.\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below",["A, B and E only","B, C and D only","A, C and D only","B, D and E only"],2,"At minimum deviation the ray passes symmetrically: parallel to the base, with i = e and r = A/2. So (A) and (C) are true and (E) is false.\nEach other deviation is obtained for two angles of incidence (i and e swapped): (D) is true.\nA larger prism angle gives a larger minimum deviation, so (B) is false."),
M("JEE Main 2025 · 23 Jan · Shift 2","prism","Minimum deviation","The refractive index of the material of a glass prism is √3. The angle of minimum deviation is equal to the angle of the prism. What is the angle of the prism?",["58°","60°","48°","50°"],1,"n = {sin((A + δ)/2);sin(A/2)} with δ = A: n = {sin A;sin(A/2)} = 2cos({A;2}).\n2cos({A;2}) = √3 gives A/2 = 30°, so A = 60°."),
M("JEE Main 2025 · 28 Jan · Shift 1","prism","Dispersion and dispersive power","A thin prism P₁ with angle 4° made of glass having refractive index 1.54 is combined with another thin prism P₂ made of glass having refractive index 1.72 to get dispersion without deviation. The angle of the prism P₂ is",["{16;3}","4","1.5","3"],3,"No net deviation: (n₁ − 1)A₁ = (n₂ − 1)A₂ (the prisms are placed in opposition).\n0.54 × 4 = 0.72 A₂, so A₂ = 3°."),
M("JEE Main 2023 · 30 Jan · Shift 2","prism","Dispersion and dispersive power","A thin prism P₁ with an angle 6° and made of glass of refractive index 1.54 is combined with another prism P₂ made from glass of refractive index 1.72 to produce dispersion without average deviation. The angle of prism P₂ is",["7.8°","4.5°","6°","1.3°"],1,"Zero average deviation: (n₁ − 1)A₁ = (n₂ − 1)A₂.\n0.54 × 6 = 0.72 A₂, so A₂ = 4.5°."),
M("JEE Main 2022 · 28 Jun · Shift 1","prism","Minimum deviation","The refracting angle of a prism is A and refractive index of the material of the prism is cot({A;2}). Then the angle of minimum deviation will be",["180° − 2A","90° − A","180° + 2A","180° − 3A"],0,"n = {sin((A + δ)/2);sin(A/2)} = {cos(A/2);sin(A/2)}.\nSo sin({A + δ;2}) = cos({A;2}) = sin(90° − {A;2}), giving {A + δ;2} = 90° − {A;2} and δ = 180° − 2A."),
M("JEE Main 2021 · 25 Jul · Shift 1","prism","Minimum deviation","A prism of refractive index μ and angle of prism A is placed in the position of minimum angle of deviation. If minimum angle of deviation is also A, then in terms of refractive index, value of A is",["2cos⁻¹({μ;2})","sin⁻¹({μ;2})","cos⁻¹({μ;2})","sin⁻¹(√({μ − 1;2}))"],0,"μ = {sin((A + A)/2);sin(A/2)} = {sin A;sin(A/2)} = 2cos({A;2}).\nSo A = 2cos⁻¹({μ;2})."),
M("JEE Main 2021 · 27 Jul · Shift 2","prism","Minimum deviation","The expected graphical representation of the variation of angle of deviation δ with angle of incidence i in a prism is",["a straight line sloping down","a straight line sloping up","a curve rising to a maximum and then falling","a curve falling to a minimum and then rising (U-shaped)"],3,"As i increases from grazing incidence, δ first falls, reaches its minimum value when i = e, and then increases again.\nThe δ–i graph is U-shaped."),
M("JEE Main 2021 · 16 Mar · Shift 1","prism","Minimum deviation","The angle of deviation through a prism is minimum when\n(A) incident ray and emergent ray are symmetric to the prism\n(B) the refracted ray inside the prism becomes parallel to its base\n(C) angle of incidence is equal to that of the angle of emergence\n(D) when angle of emergence is double the angle of incidence.",["Only statements (A) and (B) are true.","Statements (B) and (C) are true.","Statements (A), (B) and (C) are true.","Only statement (D) is true."],2,"At minimum deviation the ray passes symmetrically: i = e (C), the two faces are used symmetrically (A) and the ray in the prism is parallel to the base (B).\nWith i = e, the emergence angle is not double the incidence angle, so (D) is false."),
M("JEE Main 2019 · 11 Jan · Shift 1","prism","Dispersion and dispersive power","The variation of refractive index of a crown glass thin prism with wavelength of the incident light is shown (the index falls steadily from about 1.532 at 400 nm to about 1.515 at 700 nm). Which of the following graphs is the correct one, if D_m is the angle of minimum deviation?",["D_m falls steadily as λ increases","D_m first falls and then rises as λ increases","D_m rises steadily as λ increases","D_m rises with a flattening slope as λ increases"],0,"The minimum deviation increases with the refractive index (for a thin prism D_m = (n − 1)A).\nn falls as λ increases, so D_m also falls steadily with λ."),
M("JEE Main 2019 · 11 Jan · Shift 2","prism","Minimum deviation","A monochromatic light is incident at a certain angle on an equilateral triangular prism and suffers minimum deviation. If the refractive index of the material of the prism is √3, then the angle of incidence is",["60°","45°","90°","30°"],0,"At minimum deviation r = {A;2} = 30°. sin i = n sin r = √3 × {1;2}.\ni = 60°."),
M("JEE Main 2018 · Online","prism","Prism: deviation","A ray of light is incident at an angle of 60° on one face of a prism of angle 30°. The emergent ray of light makes an angle of 30° with incident ray. The angle made by the emergent ray with second face of prism will be",["0°","45°","90°","30°"],2,"δ = i + e − A: 30° = 60° + e − 30°, so e = 0°.\nThe emergent ray is along the normal to the second face, so it makes 90° with the face itself."),
M("JEE Advanced 2016","prism","Prism: limits of emergence","A parallel beam of light is incident from air at an angle α on the side PQ of a right angled triangular prism (right angle at Q, prism angle θ at P) of refractive index n = √2. Light undergoes total internal reflection in the prism at the face PR when α has a minimum value of 45°. The angle θ of the prism is",["15°","22.5°","30°","45°"],0,"At α = 45°: sin r₁ = {sin 45°;√2} = {1;2}, r₁ = 30°.\nTIR at PR needs incidence 45° (sin θc = {1;√2}); the incidence at PR is r₂ = r₁ + θ, and α is smallest when r₂ = 45°.\nθ = 45° − 30° = 15°."),
M("JEE Main 2015","prism","Prism: limits of emergence","Monochromatic light is incident on a glass prism of angle A. If the refractive index of the material of the prism is μ, a ray, incident at an angle θ, on the face AB would get transmitted through the face AC of the prism provided",["θ > cos⁻¹[μ sin(A + sin⁻¹({1;μ}))]","θ < cos⁻¹[μ sin(A + sin⁻¹({1;μ}))]","θ > sin⁻¹[μ sin(A − sin⁻¹({1;μ}))]","θ < sin⁻¹[μ sin(A − sin⁻¹({1;μ}))]"],2,"The ray emerges from AC only if its incidence angle there, r₂ = A − r₁, is less than the critical angle θc = sin⁻¹({1;μ}).\nSo r₁ > A − θc, and sin θ = μ sin r₁ > μ sin(A − sin⁻¹({1;μ})).\nθ > sin⁻¹[μ sin(A − sin⁻¹({1;μ}))]."),
M("JEE Main 2013","prism","Minimum deviation","The graph between angle of deviation (δ) and angle of incidence (i) for a triangular prism is represented by",["two straight lines meeting at a sharp minimum (V shape)","a horizontal straight line","a smooth curve with a maximum (hill shape)","a smooth U-shaped curve with a minimum"],3,"δ = i + e − A: it is large at grazing incidence, falls to a minimum at i = e and rises again.\nThe graph is a smooth U."),
M("AIEEE 2012","inst","Vernier and spectrometer","A spectrometer gives the following reading when used to measure the angle of a prism.\nMain scale reading: 58.5 degree\nVernier scale reading: 09 divisions\nGiven that 1 division on main scale corresponds to 0.5 degree. Total divisions on the vernier scale is 30 and match with 29 divisions of the main scale. The angle of the prism from the above data",["58.77 degree","58.65 degree","59 degree","58.59 degree"],1,"Least count = 1 MSD − 1 VSD = 0.5° × (1 − {29;30}) = {0.5;30} = 0.0167°.\nReading = 58.5° + 9 × 0.0167° = 58.65°."),
M("AIEEE 2012 · Online","refr","Total internal reflection","A glass prism of refractive index 1.5 is immersed in water (refractive index {4;3}) as shown in figure (the prism has angle θ at A; the beam is incident normally on the face AB and meets the hypotenuse AC at incidence angle θ). A light beam incident normally on the face AB is totally reflected to reach the face BC, if",["sin θ > {5;9}","sin θ > {2;3}","sin θ > {8;9}","sin θ > {1;3}"],2,"The glass-water critical angle: sin θc = {4/3;1.5} = {8;9}.\nThe incidence angle on the slanted face equals θ, and TIR needs θ > θc: sin θ > {8;9}."),
M("IIT-JEE 2008","prism","Minimum deviation","Two beams of red and violet colours are made to pass separately through a prism (angle of the prism is 60°). In the position of minimum deviation, the angle of refraction will be",["30° for both the colours","greater for the violet colour","greater for the red colour","equal but not 30° for both the colours"],0,"At minimum deviation the ray inside is symmetric: r₁ = r₂ = {A;2} = 30°, whatever the colour (the incidence angle differs but not the refraction angle)."),
M("AIEEE 2006","prism","Minimum deviation","The refractive index of glass is 1.520 for red light and 1.525 for blue light. Let D₁ and D₂ be angles of minimum deviation for red and blue light respectively in a prism of this glass. Then",["D₁ > D₂","D₁ < D₂","D₁ = D₂","D₁ can be less than or greater than D₂ depending upon the angle of prism."],1,"The deviation increases with the refractive index, whatever the prism angle: D_m = 2sin⁻¹(n sin{A;2}) − A.\nBlue has the larger n, so D₂ > D₁."),
M("IIT-JEE 2004","prism","Minimum deviation","An equilateral prism is placed on a horizontal surface. A ray PQ is incident onto it. For minimum deviation (the ray inside is QR and the emergent ray is RS)",["PQ is horizontal","QR is horizontal","RS is horizontal","any one will be horizontal."],1,"At minimum deviation the ray inside the prism is symmetric about the apex and parallel to the base.\nThe base is on the horizontal surface, so QR is horizontal."),
M("IIT-JEE 2001","prism","Minimum deviation","A given ray of light suffers minimum deviation in an equilateral prism P. Additional prisms Q and R of identical shape and of the same material as P are now added as shown in the figure (Q inverted between P and R, forming a trapezium with them). The ray will now suffer",["greater deviation","no deviation","same deviation as before","total internal reflection"],2,"Inside P the ray at minimum deviation runs parallel to the base. The prisms are of the same material and in contact, so it crosses the P–Q and Q–R boundaries without bending.\nIt leaves R through a face parallel to P's emergent face, so the final direction, and the deviation, are unchanged."),
M("IIT-JEE 1995","prism","Prism: deviation","An isosceles prism of angle 120° has a refractive index 1.44. Two parallel monochromatic rays enter the prism, parallel to each other in air, as shown (they are horizontal and enter normally through the base, the apex being on the right). The rays which emerge from the opposite faces",["are parallel to each other","are diverging","make an angle 2[sin⁻¹(0.72) − 30°] with each other","make an angle 2 sin⁻¹(0.72) with each other."],2,"Each ray meets an inclined face at an incidence angle of 30°: sin e = 1.44 × sin 30° = 0.72.\nEach ray is turned by e − 30° towards the axis, the two in opposite senses, so the rays converge and cross.\nThe angle between them is 2[sin⁻¹(0.72) − 30°]."),
M("JEE Advanced 2023","prism","Brewster angle and prism","A plane polarized blue light ray is incident on a prism such that there is no reflection from the surface of the prism. The angle of deviation of the emergent ray is δ = 60° (see figure 1). The angle of minimum deviation for red light from the same prism is δ_min = 30° (see figure 2). The refractive index of the prism material for blue light is √3. Which of the following statement(s) is (are) correct?",["The blue light is polarized in the plane of incidence.","The angle of the prism is 45°.","The refractive index of the material of the prism for red light is √2.","The angle of refraction for blue light in air at the exit plane of the prism is 60°."],[0,2,3],"No reflection means the incidence is at Brewster's angle, and only light polarized in the plane of incidence has zero reflection there: (a) is true. tan i = √3 gives i = 60° and r₁ = 30°.\nδ = i + e − A = 60° with r₂ = A − 30° and sin e = √3 sin r₂: e = A and tan A = √3, so A = 60° and e = 60°. So (b) is false and (d) is true.\nRed at minimum deviation: n = {sin 45°;sin 30°} = √2: (c) is true."),
M("JEE Advanced 2021","prism","Prism: deviation","For a prism of prism angle θ = 60°, the refractive indices of the left half and the right half are, respectively, n₁ and n₂ (n₂ ≥ n₁) as shown in the figure (the two halves are separated by the altitude of the prism). The angle of incidence i is chosen such that the incident light rays will have minimum deviation if n₁ = n₂ = n = 1.5. For the case of unequal refractive indices, n₁ = n and n₂ = n + Δn (where Δn ≪ n), the angle of emergence e = i + Δe. Which of the following statement(s) is (are) correct?",["The value of Δe (in radians) is greater than that of Δn.","Δe is proportional to Δn.","Δe lies between 2.0 and 3.0 milliradians, if Δn = 2.8 × 10⁻³.","Δe lies between 1.0 and 1.6 milliradians, if Δn = 2.8 × 10⁻³."],[1,2],"Here sin i = 1.5 sin 30° = 0.75, and the ray in the left half is parallel to the base (r₁ = 30°). It meets the dividing altitude at normal incidence, so it is not bent there, and it reaches the right face at r₂ = 30° whatever n₂ is.\nsin e = n₂ sin 30°, so cos e Δe = {Δn;2} and Δe = {Δn;2cos e} = {Δn;2 × 0.661} = 0.756Δn.\nSo Δe is proportional to Δn and smaller than it. For Δn = 2.8 × 10⁻³: Δe = 2.1 milliradians. So (b) and (c)."),
M("JEE Advanced 2017","prism","Minimum deviation","For an isosceles prism of angle A and refractive index μ, it is found that the angle of minimum deviation δₘ = A. Which of the following options is/are correct?",["For the angle of incidence i₁ = A, the ray inside the prism is parallel to the base of the prism","At minimum deviation, the incident angle i₁ and the refracting angle r₁ at the first refracting surface are related by r₁ = {i₁;2}","For this prism, the emergent ray at the second surface will be tangential to the surface when the angle of incidence at the first surface is i₁ = sin⁻¹[sin A √(4cos²{A;2} − 1) − cos A]","For this prism, the refractive index μ and the angle of prism A are related as A = {1;2}cos⁻¹({μ;2})"],[0,1,2],"At minimum deviation i₁ = {A + δₘ;2} = A and r₁ = {A;2} = {i₁;2}, and the ray is parallel to the base: (a) and (b) are true.\nμ = {sin A;sin(A/2)} = 2cos({A;2}), so A = 2cos⁻¹({μ;2}), not ½cos⁻¹: (d) is false.\nGrazing emergence: r₂ = θc with sin θc = {1;μ}, r₁ = A − θc, so sin i₁ = μ sin(A − θc) = sin A √(μ² − 1) − cos A, and μ² = 4cos²({A;2}): (c) is true."),
M("IIT-JEE 2010","prism","Prism: deviation","A ray OP of monochromatic light is incident on the face AB of prism ABCD near vertex B at an incident angle of 60° (see figure; the ray comes from above and travels downwards; the prism ABCD has ∠A = 90°, ∠B = 60°, ∠C = 135° and ∠D = 75°). If the refractive index of the material of the prism is √3, which of the following is (are) correct?",["The ray gets totally internally reflected at face CD.","The ray comes out through face AD.","The angle between the incident ray and the emergent ray is 90°.","The angle between the incident ray and the emergent ray is 120°."],[0,1,2],"At AB: sin r = {sin 60°;√3} = {1;2}, r = 30°. The ray inside is parallel to BC.\nAt CD it meets the face at incidence 45°; the critical angle is sin⁻¹({1;√3}) = 35.3°, so it is totally reflected (a). It then travels 60° below the horizontal and reaches AD at incidence 30° < θc, so it comes out through AD (b), at sin e = √3 sin 30°, e = 60°.\nIncident direction 60° below the horizontal (towards the right); emergent direction 60° from the vertical, 30° below the horizontal (towards the left): the angle between them is 90° (c)."),
M("IIT-JEE 1990","prism","Dispersion and dispersive power","A thin prism P₁ with angle 4° and made from glass of refractive index 1.54 is combined with another thin prism P₂ made from glass of refractive index 1.72 to produce dispersion without deviation. The angle of the prism P₂ is",["5.33°","4°","3°","2.6°"],2,"No deviation: (n₁ − 1)A₁ = (n₂ − 1)A₂, i.e. 0.54 × 4 = 0.72 A₂.\nA₂ = 3°."),
M("IIT-JEE 1989","refr","Total internal reflection","A beam of light consisting of red, green and blue colours is incident on a right angled prism, as shown in figure (the beam is normal to a face and meets the hypotenuse at 45°). The refractive indices of the material of the prism for the above red, green and blue wavelengths are 1.39, 1.44 and 1.47 respectively. The prism will",["separate part of the red colour from the green and blue colours","separate part of the blue colour from the red and green colours","separate all the three colours from one another","not separate even partially any colour from the other two colours."],0,"The critical angles are sin⁻¹({1;1.39}) = 46.1°, sin⁻¹({1;1.44}) = 44.0° and sin⁻¹({1;1.47}) = 42.8°.\nAt 45° incidence red (θc = 46.1°) is partly transmitted, while green and blue (θc < 45°) are totally reflected.\nSo red is separated from green and blue."),
N("JEE Main 2025 · 2 Apr · Shift 2","prism","Minimum deviation","A ray of light suffers minimum deviation when incident on a prism having angle of the prism equal to 60°. The refractive index of the prism material is √2. The angle of incidence (in degrees) is ______.",45,"At minimum deviation r = {A;2} = 30°. sin i = √2 sin 30° = {1;√2}.\ni = 45°."),
N("JEE Main 2024 · 6 Apr · Shift 1","prism","Minimum deviation","The refractive index of prism is μ = √3 and the ratio of the angle of minimum deviation to the angle of prism is one. The value of angle of prism is ______ degrees.",60,"δₘ = A gives μ = {sin A;sin(A/2)} = 2cos({A;2}) = √3.\ncos({A;2}) = {√3;2}, so A/2 = 30° and A = 60°."),
N("JEE Advanced 2024","prism","Prism: deviation","Two equilateral-triangular prisms P₁ and P₂ are kept with their sides parallel to each other, in vacuum, as shown in the figure. A light ray enters prism P₁ at an angle of incidence θ such that the outgoing ray undergoes minimum deviation in prism P₂. If the respective refractive indices of P₁ and P₂ are √({3;2}) and √3, then θ = sin⁻¹[√({3;2}) sin({π;β})], where the value of β is ______.",12,"Minimum deviation in P₂: r = 30°, so the incidence on P₂ is sin⁻¹(√3 sin 30°) = 60°.\nThe faces between the prisms are parallel, so the exit angle from P₁ is also 60°: √({3;2}) sin r₂ = sin 60°, giving r₂ = 45°.\nIn P₁: r₁ = A − r₂ = 60° − 45° = 15°, so sin θ = √({3;2}) sin 15° = √({3;2}) sin({π;12}).\nβ = 12."),
N("JEE Main 2023 · 15 Apr · Shift 1","prism","Minimum deviation","The refractive index of a transparent liquid filled in an equilateral hollow prism is √2. The angle of minimum deviation for the liquid will be ______.",30,"{sin((A + δ)/2);sin(A/2)} = √2 with A = 60°: sin({60° + δ;2}) = √2 × {1;2} = sin 45°.\n{60° + δ;2} = 45°, so δ = 30° (degrees)."),
N("JEE Main 2022 · 27 Jul · Shift 2","prism","Dispersion and dispersive power","A thin prism of angle 6° and refractive index for yellow light (n_Y) is 1.5 is combined with another prism of angle 5° and n_Y = 1.55 (the two prisms are placed with opposite orientations, as in the figure). The combination produces no dispersion. The net average deviation (δ) produced by the combination is ({1;x})°. The value of x is ______.",4,"Each thin prism deviates yellow light by (n − 1)A, and they act oppositely: 0.5 × 6° = 3° and 0.55 × 5° = 2.75°.\nδ = 3° − 2.75° = 0.25° = ({1;4})°, so x = 4."),
N("JEE Main 2021 · 31 Aug · Shift 2","prism","Minimum deviation","Cross-section view of a prism is the equilateral triangle ABC shown in the figure (each side of the triangle is 10 cm). The minimum deviation is observed using this prism when the angle of incidence is equal to the prism angle. The time taken by light to travel from P (midpoint of BC) to A is ______ × 10⁻¹⁰ s. (Given, speed of light in vacuum = 3 × 10⁸ m/s and cos 30° = {√3;2})",5,"Minimum deviation with i = A: n = {sin((A + δ)/2);sin(A/2)} = {sin A;sin(A/2)} = 2cos 30° = √3.\nSpeed in the prism = {c;√3} = √3 × 10⁸ m/s. PA is the altitude: 10 × cos 30° = 5√3 cm = 0.0866 m.\nt = {0.0866;1.732 × 10⁸} = 5 × 10⁻¹⁰ s."),
N("JEE Main 2021 · 22 Jul · Shift 2","prism","Minimum deviation","A ray of light passing through a prism (μ = √3) suffers minimum deviation. It is found that the angle of incidence is double the angle of refraction within the prism. Then, angle of prism is ______ (in degrees).",60,"i = 2r and at minimum deviation r = {A;2}, so i = A.\nn = {sin 2r;sin r} = 2cos r = √3, so r = 30° and A = 2r = 60°."),
N("JEE Main 2021 · 27 Jul · Shift 1","prism","Dispersion and dispersive power","A prism of refractive index n₁ and another prism of refractive index n₂ are stuck together (as shown in figure; BC is the common face). n₁ and n₂ depend on λ, the wavelength of light, according to the relation n₁ = 1.2 + {10.8 × 10⁻¹⁴;λ²} and n₂ = 1.45 + {1.8 × 10⁻¹⁴;λ²}. The wavelength for which rays incident at any angle on the interface BC pass through without bending at that interface will be ______ nm.",600,"No bending at the interface at any angle needs n₁ = n₂.\n1.2 + {10.8 × 10⁻¹⁴;λ²} = 1.45 + {1.8 × 10⁻¹⁴;λ²} gives {9 × 10⁻¹⁴;λ²} = 0.25.\nλ² = 3.6 × 10⁻¹³ m², λ = 6 × 10⁻⁷ m = 600 nm."),
N("JEE Main 2020 · 5 Sep · Shift 2","prism","Prism: deviation","A prism of angle A = 1° has a refractive index μ = 1.5. A good estimate of the minimum angle of deviation (in degrees) is close to {N;10}. Value of N is ______.",5,"For a thin prism δₘ = (μ − 1)A = 0.5 × 1° = 0.5° = {5;10}°.\nSo N = 5."),
N("JEE Advanced 2019","prism","Total internal reflection","A monochromatic light is incident from air on a refracting surface of a prism of angle 75° and refractive index n₀ = √3. The other refracting surface of the prism is coated by a thin film of material of refractive index n as shown in figure. The light suffers total internal reflection at the coated prism surface for an incidence angle of θ ≤ 60°. The value of n² is ______.",1.5,"For θ = 60°: sin r₁ = {sin 60°;√3} = {1;2}, r₁ = 30°, so the incidence at the coated face is r₂ = 75° − 30° = 45°. Smaller θ gives a larger r₂, so 45° is the limiting case.\nTIR begins when √3 sin 45° = n: n = √({3;2}), so n² = 1.5."),
N("JEE Advanced 2019","refr","Optical fibre","A planar structure of length L and width W is made of two different optical media of refractive indices n₁ = 1.5 and n₂ = 1.44 as shown in figure (a core of n₁ with n₂ above and below it). If L ≫ W, a ray entering from end AB will emerge from end CD only if the total internal reflection condition is met inside the structure. For L = 9.6 m, if the incident angle θ is varied, the maximum time taken by a ray to exit plane CD is t × 10⁻⁹ s, where t is ______. [Speed of light c = 3 × 10⁸ m/s]",50,"The longest path is for the ray that just meets the critical angle: sin θc = {1.44;1.5} = 0.96, so the ray makes an angle α with the length where cos α = sin θc = 0.96.\nPath length = {L;cos α} = {9.6;0.96} = 10 m, at speed {c;n₁} = 2 × 10⁸ m/s.\nt = {10;2 × 10⁸} = 5 × 10⁻⁸ s = 50 × 10⁻⁹ s."),
N("JEE Advanced 2015","prism","Dispersion and dispersive power","A monochromatic beam of light is incident at 60° on one face of an equilateral prism of refractive index n and emerges from the opposite face making an angle θ(n) with the normal (see the figure). For n = √3 the value of θ is 60° and {dθ;dn} = m. The value of m is ______.",2,"sin r₁ = {sin 60°;n} and r₂ = 60° − r₁, so sin θ = n sin(60° − r₁) = {√3;2}√(n² − {3;4}) − {√3;4}.\nDifferentiating: cos θ {dθ;dn} = {√3;2} × {n;√(n² − 3/4)}. At n = √3: √(3 − 0.75) = 1.5, so the right side is {√3;2} × {√3;1.5} = 1.\nWith cos 60° = 0.5: {dθ;dn} = 2."),
M("JEE Main 2024 · 5 Apr · Shift 2","prism","Dispersion and dispersive power","Given below are two statements:\nStatement-I: When the white light passed through a prism, the red light bends lesser than yellow and violet.\nStatement-II: The refractive indices are different for different wavelengths in dispersive medium.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:",["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are true.","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are false.","Statement-I is true but Statement-II is false.","Statement-I is false but Statement-II is true."],0,"In a dispersive medium n depends on λ, so Statement II is true.\nThe refractive index is smallest for red, so red is deviated least, less than yellow and violet: Statement I is true."),
A("IIT-JEE 2001","prism","Dispersion and dispersive power","The refractive indices of the crown glass for blue and red lights are 1.51 and 1.49 respectively and those of flint glass are 1.77 and 1.73 respectively. An isosceles prism of angle 6° is made of crown glass. A beam of white light is incident at a small angle on this prism. The other flint glass isosceles prism is combined with the crown glass prism such that there is no deviation of the incident light. Determine the angle of the flint glass prism. Calculate the net dispersion of the combined system.","Flint prism angle 4°; net angular dispersion 0.04° (the flint prism's dispersion is the larger)","Mean indices: crown 1.50, flint 1.75. No deviation: (1.50 − 1)6° = (1.75 − 1)A_f, so A_f = {3;0.75} = 4°.\nDispersion of crown = (1.51 − 1.49) × 6° = 0.12°. Dispersion of flint = (1.77 − 1.73) × 4° = 0.16°.\nThe prisms act oppositely: net dispersion = 0.16° − 0.12° = 0.04°."),
A("IIT-JEE 1998","prism","Dispersion and dispersive power","A prism of refractive index μ₁ and another prism of refractive index μ₂ are stuck together without a gap as shown in figure (the combined body has faces AC and BD meeting at 60°; the angles are ∠A = 60°, ∠B = 40° + 20° = 60°, ∠C = 80° + 70°, ∠D = 90°). The angles of the prisms are as shown. μ₁ and μ₂ depend on λ, the wavelength of light, according to μ₁ = 1.20 + {10.8 × 10⁴;λ²} and μ₂ = 1.45 + {1.80 × 10⁴;λ²}, where λ is in nm.\n(a) Calculate the wavelength λ₀ for which rays incident at any angle on the interface BC pass through without bending at that interface.\n(b) For light of wavelength λ₀, find the angle of incidence i on the face AC such that the deviation produced by the combination of prisms is minimum.","(a) λ₀ = 600 nm   (b) i = sin⁻¹(0.75) ≈ 48.6°","(a) No bending at BC needs μ₁ = μ₂: {9 × 10⁴;λ²} = 0.25, so λ² = 3.6 × 10⁵ and λ₀ = 600 nm.\n(b) Both indices are then 1.2 + 0.3 = 1.5, so the assembly is one prism with its two outer faces AC and BD at 60° (the prism angle is 60°).\nMinimum deviation: r = 30°, sin i = 1.5 sin 30° = 0.75, so i ≈ 48.6°."),
A("IIT-JEE 1987","prism","Total internal reflection","A right prism is to be made by selecting a proper material and the angles A and B (B ≤ A), as shown in figure (right angle at C; face AB is the hypotenuse). It is desired that a ray of light incident normally on the face AB emerges parallel to the incident direction after two internal reflections (at AC and BC).\n(i) What should be the minimum refractive index μ for this to be possible?\n(ii) For μ = {5;3} is it possible to achieve this with the angle B equal to 30 degree?","(i) μ = √2   (ii) No","The ray meets AC at incidence A and then BC at incidence B (A + B = 90°). Both reflections must be total: B ≥ θc (B is the smaller angle).\nSince B ≤ 45°, the most favourable case is B = 45°: sin θc ≤ sin 45°, so μ ≥ √2.\nFor μ = {5;3}: sin θc = 0.6 and θc = 36.9°. With B = 30° < θc, the ray escapes at BC, so it is not possible."),
A("IIT-JEE 1978","prism","Prism: deviation","A ray of light is incident at an angle of 60° on one face of a prism which has an angle of 30°. The ray emerging out of the prism makes an angle of 30° with the incident ray. Show that the emergent ray is perpendicular to the face through which it emerges and calculate the refractive index of the material of the prism.","μ = √3","δ = i + e − A: 30° = 60° + e − 30° gives e = 0, so the emergent ray is normal to the second face.\nThen r₂ = 0 and r₁ = A = 30°.\nμ = {sin 60°;sin 30°} = √3."),
N("IIT-JEE 1997","prism","Prism: deviation","A ray of light is incident normally on one of the faces of a prism of apex angle 30° and refractive index √2. The angle of deviation of the ray is ______ degree.",15,"Normal incidence: r₁ = 0, so r₂ = A = 30°.\nsin e = √2 sin 30° = {1;√2}, e = 45°. δ = e − A = 15°."),
N("IIT-JEE 1992","prism","Minimum deviation","A ray of light undergoes deviation of 30° when incident on an equilateral prism of refractive index √2. The angle made by the ray inside the prism with the base of the prism is ______.",0,"For n = √2 and A = 60° the minimum deviation is 2sin⁻¹(√2 sin 30°) − 60° = 90° − 60° = 30°, the smallest possible deviation.\nSo the ray is at minimum deviation, travelling parallel to the base: the angle is 0°."),
M("JEE Main 2025 · 24 Jan · Shift 2","inst","Camera","A photograph of a landscape is captured by a drone camera at a height of 18 km. The size of the camera film is 2 cm × 2 cm and the area of the landscape photographed is 400 km². The focal length of the lens in the drone camera is",["2.5 cm","1.8 cm","2.8 cm","0.9 cm"],1,"The landscape is a square of side 20 km, imaged on a 2 cm side: magnification m = {2 × 10⁻²;2 × 10⁴} = 10⁻⁶.\nFor a distant object the image is at the focus: f = m × (object distance) = 10⁻⁶ × 18 × 10³ m = 0.018 m = 1.8 cm."),
M("JEE Main 2023 · 8 Apr · Shift 1","inst","Reflecting telescope","In a reflecting telescope, a secondary mirror is used to",["reduce the problem of mechanical support","move the eyepiece outside the telescopic tube","remove spherical aberration","make chromatic aberration zero."],1,"The secondary mirror redirects the converging light from the primary mirror so that the eyepiece can be placed at the side or behind the primary, outside the incoming beam."),
M("JEE Main 2023 · 29 Jan · Shift 2","inst","Resolving power","A scientist is observing a bacteria through a compound microscope. For better analysis and to improve its resolving power he should (Select the best option)",["increase the wavelength of the light","increase the refractive index of the medium between the object and objective lens","decrease the focal length of the eye piece","decrease the diameter of the objective lens."],1,"Resolving power = {2n sin β;1.22λ}. It increases with a larger refractive index of the medium next to the objective (oil immersion) and a smaller wavelength.\nThe eyepiece and the lens diameter choices do not help in this way."),
M("JEE Main 2022 · 27 Jul · Shift 2","inst","Resolving power","A microscope was initially placed in air (refractive index 1). It is then immersed in oil (refractive index 2). For a light whose wavelength in air is λ, calculate the change of microscope's resolving power due to oil and choose the correct option.",["Resolving power will be {1;4} in the oil than it was in the air","Resolving power will be twice in the oil than it was in the air","Resolving power will be four times in the oil than it was in the air","Resolving power will be {1;2} in the oil than it was in the air"],1,"Resolving power = {2n sin β;1.22λ}, with λ the wavelength in air (the same source).\nn goes from 1 to 2, so the resolving power doubles."),
M("JEE Main 2022 · 28 Jun · Shift 1","inst","Resolving power","The aperture of the objective is 24.4 cm. The resolving power of this telescope, if a light of wavelength 2440 Å is used to see the object will be",["8.1 × 10⁶","10.0 × 10⁷","8.2 × 10⁵","1.0 × 10⁻⁸"],2,"Resolving power = {D;1.22λ} = {0.244;1.22 × 2.44 × 10⁻⁷} = 8.2 × 10⁵."),
M("JEE Main 2022 · 28 Jun · Shift 1","inst","Astronomical telescope","In normal adjustment, for a refracting telescope, the distance between objective and eye-piece is 30 cm. The focal length of the objective, when the angular magnification of the telescope is 2, will be",["20 cm","30 cm","10 cm","15 cm"],0,"Normal adjustment: f₀ + fₑ = 30 cm and M = {f₀;fₑ} = 2.\nfₑ = 10 cm, f₀ = 20 cm."),
M("JEE Main 2021 · 18 Mar · Shift 1","inst","Defects of vision","Your friend is having eye sight problem. She is not able to see clearly a distant uniform window mesh and it appears to her as non-uniform and distorted. The doctor diagnosed the problem as",["Myopia with Astigmatism","Astigmatism","Myopia and hypermetropia","Presbyopia with Astigmatism"],0,"Not seeing distant things clearly is myopia. A regular grid that looks distorted and non-uniform is the sign of astigmatism (unequal curvature of the cornea in different directions).\nSo the problem is myopia with astigmatism."),
M("JEE Main 2020 · 7 Jan · Shift 1","inst","Compound microscope","If we need a magnification of 375 from a compound microscope of tube length 150 mm and an objective of focal length 5 mm, the focal length of the eye-piece, should be close to",["22 mm","12 mm","33 mm","2 mm"],0,"For the final image at the near point D = 250 mm: M = {L;f₀}(1 + {D;fₑ}) = 30(1 + {250;fₑ}) = 375.\n{250;fₑ} = 11.5, so fₑ = 21.7 mm ≈ 22 mm."),
M("JEE Main 2020 · 8 Jan · Shift 1","inst","Astronomical telescope","The magnifying power of a telescope with tube length 60 cm is 5. What is the focal length of its eye piece?",["30 cm","40 cm","10 cm","20 cm"],2,"L = f₀ + fₑ = 60 cm and M = {f₀;fₑ} = 5.\n6fₑ = 60, so fₑ = 10 cm."),
M("JEE Main 2020 · 9 Jan · Shift 1","inst","Resolving power","The aperture diameter of a telescope is 5 m. The separation between the moon and the earth is 4 × 10⁵ km. With light of wavelength of 5500 Å, the minimum separation between objects on the surface of moon, so that they are just resolved, is close to",["600 m","20 m","60 m","200 m"],2,"θ = {1.22λ;D} = {1.22 × 5.5 × 10⁻⁷;5} = 1.34 × 10⁻⁷ rad.\nSeparation = θ × distance = 1.34 × 10⁻⁷ × 4 × 10⁸ m ≈ 54 m, closest to 60 m."),
M("JEE Main 2019 · 8 Apr · Shift 2","inst","Resolving power","Calculate the limit of resolution of a telescope objective having a diameter of 200 cm, if it has to detect light of wavelength 500 nm coming from a star.",["610 × 10⁻⁹ radian","152.5 × 10⁻⁹ radian","457.5 × 10⁻⁹ radian","305 × 10⁻⁹ radian"],3,"θ = {1.22λ;D} = {1.22 × 500 × 10⁻⁹;2} = 305 × 10⁻⁹ rad."),
M("JEE Main 2019 · 9 Apr · Shift 2","inst","Resolving power","Diameter of the objective lens of a telescope is 250 cm. For light of wavelength 600 nm coming from a distant object, the limit of resolution of the telescope is close to",["2.0 × 10⁻⁷ rad","1.5 × 10⁻⁷ rad","4.5 × 10⁻⁷ rad","3.0 × 10⁻⁷ rad"],3,"θ = {1.22λ;D} = {1.22 × 6 × 10⁻⁷;2.5} = 2.9 × 10⁻⁷ rad ≈ 3.0 × 10⁻⁷ rad."),
M("JEE Main 2019 · 12 Apr · Shift 1","inst","Resolving power","The value of numerical aperture of the objective lens of a microscope is 1.25. If light of wavelength 5000 Å is used, the minimum separation between two points, to be seen as distinct, will be",["0.48 μm","0.12 μm","0.38 μm","0.24 μm"],3,"Smallest separation = {0.61λ;NA} = {0.61 × 0.5 μm;1.25} = 0.24 μm."),
M("JEE Main 2016 · Online","inst","Resolving power","Two stars are 10 light years away from the earth. They are seen through a telescope of objective diameter 30 cm. The wavelength of light is 600 nm. To see the stars just resolved by the telescope, the minimum distance between them should be (1 light year = 9.46 × 10¹⁵ m) of the order of",["10⁸ km","10¹⁰ km","10¹¹ km","10⁶ km"],0,"θ = {1.22λ;D} = {1.22 × 6 × 10⁻⁷;0.3} = 2.4 × 10⁻⁶ rad.\nSeparation = θ × distance = 2.4 × 10⁻⁶ × 9.46 × 10¹⁶ m ≈ 2 × 10¹¹ m = 2 × 10⁸ km, of the order of 10⁸ km."),
M("JEE Main 2016","inst","Astronomical telescope","An observer looks at a distant tree of height 10 m with a telescope of magnifying power of 20. To the observer the tree appears",["10 times taller","10 times nearer","20 times taller","20 times nearer"],3,"Angular magnification 20 means the tree subtends 20 times the angle it would subtend at the naked eye, which is what it would look like from a point 20 times nearer. Its height is not changed."),
M("JEE Main 2015 · Online","inst","Astronomical telescope","A telescope has an objective lens of focal length 150 cm and an eyepiece of focal length 5 cm. If a 50 m tall tower at a distance of 1 km is observed through this telescope in normal setting, the angle formed by the image of the tower is θ, then θ is close to",["1°","15°","30°","60°"],3,"M = {150;5} = 30. The tower subtends tan θ₀ = {50;1000} = 0.05.\nThe angle is large, so use tan θ = M tan θ₀ = 1.5, giving θ ≈ 56°, closest to 60°."),
M("JEE Main 2015","inst","Resolving power","Assuming human pupil to have a radius of 0.25 cm and a comfortable viewing distance of 25 cm, the minimum separation between two objects that human eye can resolve at 500 nm wavelength is",["100 μm","300 μm","1 μm","30 μm"],3,"θ = {1.22λ;D} = {1.22 × 5 × 10⁻⁷;5 × 10⁻³} = 1.22 × 10⁻⁴ rad.\nSeparation = θ × 25 cm = 3.05 × 10⁻³ cm ≈ 30 μm."),
M("AIEEE 2003","inst","Compound microscope","The image formed by an objective of a compound microscope is",["virtual and diminished","real and diminished","real and enlarged","virtual and enlarged."],2,"The object is just outside the focus of the objective, which therefore forms a real, inverted, enlarged image; the eyepiece then acts as a magnifier on it."),
M("IIT-JEE 1995","inst","Compound microscope","The focal lengths of the objective and the eye piece of a compound microscope are 2.0 cm and 3.0 cm, respectively. The distance between the objective and the eyepiece is 15.0 cm. The final image formed by the eyepiece is at infinity. The two lenses are thin. The distance, in cm, of the object and the image produced by the objective, measured from the objective lens, are respectively",["2.4 and 12.0","2.4 and 15.0","2.0 and 12.0","2.0 and 3.0."],0,"For a final image at infinity the objective's image lies at the focus of the eyepiece: 3 cm from it, so 15 − 3 = 12 cm from the objective: v = 12 cm.\n{1;u} = {1;v} − {1;f}: with f = 2 cm, u = −{12 × 2;12 − 2} = −2.4 cm.\nThe object is 2.4 cm and the image 12.0 cm from the objective."),
M("IIT-JEE 1992","inst","Astronomical telescope","A planet is observed by an astronomical refracting telescope having an objective of focal length 16 m and an eyepiece of focal length 2 cm.",["The distance between the objective and the eyepiece is 16.02 m.","The angular magnification of the planet is −800.","The image of the planet is inverted.","The objective is larger than the eyepiece."],[0,1,2,3],"In normal adjustment L = f₀ + fₑ = 16 m + 0.02 m = 16.02 m.\nM = −{f₀;fₑ} = −{1600;2} = −800, the minus sign meaning an inverted image.\nTo gather light the objective has the larger aperture: all four statements are correct."),
N("JEE Main 2021 · 20 Jul · Shift 1","inst","Compound microscope","An object viewed from a near point distance of 25 cm, using a microscopic lens with magnification '6' gives an unresolved image. A resolved image is observed at infinite distance with a total magnification double the earlier using an eyepiece along with the given lens and a tube of length 0.6 m, if the focal length of the eyepiece is equal to ______ cm.",25,"A single lens at the near point gives M = 1 + {25;f} = 6, so the given lens has f₀ = 5 cm.\nCompound microscope, final image at infinity: M = {L;f₀} × {D;fₑ} = {60;5} × {25;fₑ} = 12.\n{300;fₑ} = 12, so fₑ = 25 cm."),
N("JEE Main 2020 · 4 Sep · Shift 1","inst","Compound microscope","In a compound microscope, the magnified virtual image is formed at a distance of 25 cm from the eye-piece. The focal length of its objective lens is 1 cm. If the magnification is 100 and the tube length of the microscope is 20 cm, then the focal length of the eye-piece lens (in cm) is ______.",6.25,"M = {L;f₀}(1 + {D;fₑ}) = {20;1}(1 + {25;fₑ}) = 100.\n1 + {25;fₑ} = 5, so fₑ = 6.25 cm."),
N("JEE Main 2020 · 5 Sep · Shift 1","inst","Compound microscope","A compound microscope consists of an objective lens of focal length 1 cm and an eye piece of focal length 5 cm with a separation of 10 cm. The distance between an object and the objective lens, at which the strain on the eye is minimum is {n;40} cm. The value of n is ______.",50,"Minimum strain: the final image is at infinity, so the objective's image is at the focus of the eyepiece, 5 cm from it and 10 − 5 = 5 cm from the objective.\n{1;u} = {1;v} − {1;f} = {1;5} − 1 = −{4;5}, so u = −{5;4} cm = {50;40} cm.\nn = 50."),
A("IIT-JEE 1992","inst","Resolving power","The resolving power of an electron microscope is higher than that of an optical microscope because the wavelength of electrons is ______ than the wavelength of visible light.","smaller","Resolving power is inversely proportional to the wavelength. Accelerated electrons have a de Broglie wavelength of the order of 0.01 nm to 0.1 nm, far smaller than visible light (400–700 nm)."),
A("IIT-JEE 1980","inst","Astronomical telescope","A telescope has an objective of focal length 50 cm and an eyepiece of focal length 5 cm. The least distance of distinct vision is 25 cm. The telescope is focussed for distinct vision on a scale 200 cm away from the objective. Calculate (a) the separation between the objective and the eyepiece and (b) the magnification produced.","(a) 70.8 cm   (b) magnification 2 (the image is inverted)","The objective images the scale at v₀ = {200 × 50;200 − 50} = 66.7 cm.\nThe eyepiece must give a virtual image at 25 cm: {1;−25} − {1;u} = {1;5}, so u = −{25;6} = −4.17 cm.\n(a) Separation = 66.7 + 4.17 = 70.8 cm.\n(b) m₀ = {66.7;200} = 0.333 and mₑ = 1 + {25;5} = 6, so the magnification is 0.333 × 6 = 2."),
M("JEE Main 2025 · 2 Apr · Shift 1","huyg","Plane wavefronts","A light wave is propagating with plane wave fronts of the type x + y + z = constant. The angle made by the direction of wave propagation with the x-axis is",["cos⁻¹({1;√3})","cos⁻¹({2;3})","cos⁻¹({1;3})","cos⁻¹({√2;√3})"],0,"The wave travels along the normal to the wavefront, which is along (1, 1, 1)/√3.\ncos α = {1;√3}."),
M("JEE Main 2025 · 28 Jan · Shift 2","huyg","Wave theory of light","Which of the following phenomena cannot be explained by wave theory of light?",["Refraction of light","Compton effect","Reflection of light","Diffraction of light"],1,"Reflection, refraction and diffraction follow from Huygens' wave theory. The Compton effect (scattering of an X-ray photon with change of wavelength) needs the particle (photon) picture."),
M("JEE Main 2024 · 5 Apr · Shift 1","huyg","Wavefront shapes","Light emerges out of a convex lens when a source of light is kept at its focus. The shape of the wavefront of the light is",["plane","cylindrical","both spherical and cylindrical","spherical"],0,"A source at the focus gives a parallel beam, and the wavefront of a parallel beam is a plane."),
M("JEE Main 2015","huyg","Huygens' principle","On a hot summer night, the refractive index of air is smallest near the ground and increases with height from the ground. When a light beam is directed horizontally, the Huygens' principle leads us to conclude that as it travels, the light beam",["bends downwards","bends upwards","becomes narrower","goes horizontally without any deflection"],1,"The speed c/n is larger near the ground, so the lower part of each wavefront runs ahead of the upper part.\nThe wavefront therefore tilts, and the ray (normal to it) bends upwards."),
M("AIEEE 2010","huyg","Wavefront shapes","An initially parallel cylindrical beam travels in a medium of refractive index μ(I) = μ₀ + μ₂I, where μ₀ and μ₂ are positive constants and I is the intensity of the light beam. The intensity of the beam is decreasing with increasing radius.\nThe initial shape of the wavefront of the beam is",["planar","convex","concave","convex near the axis and concave near the periphery"],0,"A parallel beam has a plane wavefront."),
M("AIEEE 2010","refr","Refractive index, speed and wavelength","An initially parallel cylindrical beam travels in a medium of refractive index μ(I) = μ₀ + μ₂I, where μ₀ and μ₂ are positive constants and I is the intensity of the light beam. The intensity of the beam is decreasing with increasing radius.\nThe speed of light in the medium is",["maximum on the axis of the beam","minimum on the axis of the beam","the same everywhere in the beam","directly proportional to the intensity I"],1,"The intensity is highest on the axis, so the refractive index μ₀ + μ₂I is highest there. The speed c/μ is therefore lowest on the axis."),
M("AIEEE 2010","huyg","Huygens' principle","An initially parallel cylindrical beam travels in a medium of refractive index μ(I) = μ₀ + μ₂I, where μ₀ and μ₂ are positive constants and I is the intensity of the light beam. The intensity of the beam is decreasing with increasing radius.\nAs the beam enters the medium, it will",["travel as a cylindrical beam","diverge","converge","diverge near the axis and converge near the periphery"],2,"The central part of each wavefront travels slowest, so it lags behind the outer part. The wavefront becomes concave (curved towards the direction of travel at the edges), and the beam converges (self-focusing)."),
M("JEE Main 2023 · 6 Apr · Shift 2","yd","Phase difference","Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.\nAssertion A: The phase difference of two light waves change if they travel through different media having same thickness.\nReason R: The wavelengths of waves are different in different media.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.",["Both A and R are correct but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.","A is not correct but R is correct.","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A.","A is correct but R is not correct."],2,"Over a thickness t the phase is 2π{t;λ_medium} = 2π{nt;λ₀}. For different media (different λ_medium) the same thickness gives a different phase, so A is true.\nThe wavelength differing from medium to medium (λ = λ₀/n) is exactly the reason."),
M("JEE Main 2025 · 3 Apr · Shift 2","yd","Superposition and intensity","Two monochromatic light beams have intensities in the ratio 1 : 9. An interference pattern is obtained by these beams. The ratio of intensities of maximum to minimum is",["3 : 1","9 : 1","4 : 1","8 : 1"],2,"Amplitudes are in the ratio 1 : 3, so {I_max;I_min} = ({3 + 1;3 − 1})² = 2² = 4.\nThe ratio is 4 : 1."),
M("JEE Main 2025 · 3 Apr · Shift 2","yd","Superposition and intensity","Width of one of the two slits in a Young's double slit experiment is half of the other slit. The ratio of the maximum to the minimum intensity in the interference pattern is",["(3 + 2√2) : (3 − 2√2)","3 : 1","(2√2 + 1) : (2√2 − 1)","9 : 1"],0,"Intensity is proportional to the slit width, so I₂ = 2I₁ and the amplitudes are in the ratio 1 : √2.\n{I_max;I_min} = ({√2 + 1;√2 − 1})² = (√2 + 1)⁴ = (3 + 2√2)² = {3 + 2√2;3 − 2√2}, because (3 + 2√2)(3 − 2√2) = 1."),
M("JEE Main 2025 · 4 Apr · Shift 1","yd","Fringe width","In a Young's double slit experiment, the slits are separated by 0.2 mm. If the slits separation is increased to 0.4 mm, the percentage change of the fringe width is",["0%","50%","100%","25%"],1,"β = {λD;d}. Doubling d halves β: it falls from β to {β;2}, a 50% change (a decrease)."),
M("JEE Main 2025 · 8 Apr · Shift 2","yd","Coherence","In a Young's double slit experiment, the source is white light. One of the slits is covered by red filter and another by green filter. In this case",["there shall be an interference pattern for red distinct from that for green.","there shall be alternate interference fringes of red and green.","there shall be no interference fringes.","there shall be an interference pattern, where each fringe's pattern center is green and outer edges is red."],2,"The two beams now have different wavelengths (and so different frequencies); they are not coherent and the phase difference between them changes randomly.\nNo stable interference fringes form."),
M("JEE Main 2025 · 22 Jan · Shift 2","yd","Thin film interference","A transparent film of refractive index, 2.0 is coated on a glass slab of refractive index, 1.45. What is the minimum thickness of transparent film to be coated for the maximum transmission of green light of wavelength 550 nm? [Assume that the light is incident nearly perpendicular to the glass surface.]",["94.8 nm","275 nm","137.5 nm","68.7 nm"],2,"Reflection from air to film (film denser) has a phase jump of π; reflection from film to glass (glass less dense than the film) has none.\nMaximum transmission means minimum reflection: destructive reflection, 2nt = λ for the least non-zero thickness.\nt = {λ;2n} = {550;4} = 137.5 nm."),
M("JEE Main 2025 · 23 Jan · Shift 2","yd","Superposition and intensity","The width of one of the two slits in Young's double slit experiment is d while that of the other slit is xd. If the ratio of the maximum to the minimum intensity in the interference pattern on the screen is 9 : 4 then what is the value of x? (Assume that the field strength varies according to the slit width.)",["4","5","3","2"],1,"The amplitudes are proportional to the widths: A₁ : A₂ = 1 : x.\n{I_max;I_min} = ({x + 1;x − 1})² = {9;4}, so {x + 1;x − 1} = {3;2}.\n2x + 2 = 3x − 3 gives x = 5."),
M("JEE Main 2025 · 24 Jan · Shift 1","yd","Two wavelengths","The Young's double slit interference experiment is performed using light consisting of 480 nm and 600 nm wavelengths to form interference patterns. The least number of the bright fringes of 480 nm light that are required for the first coincidence with the bright fringes formed by 600 nm light is",["5","6","4","8"],0,"Coincidence needs n₁ × 480 = n₂ × 600, so {n₁;n₂} = {600;480} = {5;4}.\nThe smallest integers are n₁ = 5 (and n₂ = 4)."),
M("JEE Main 2025 · 24 Jan · Shift 2","yd","Fringe width","Young's double slit interference apparatus is immersed in a liquid of refractive index 1.44. It has slit separation of 1.5 mm. The slits are illuminated by a parallel beam of light whose wavelength in air is 690 nm. The fringe-width on a screen placed behind the plane of slits at a distance of 0.72 m, will be",["0.46 mm","0.63 mm","0.33 mm","0.23 mm"],3,"In the liquid λ = {690;1.44} = 479 nm.\nβ = {λD;d} = {479 × 10⁻⁹ × 0.72;1.5 × 10⁻³} = 2.3 × 10⁻⁴ m = 0.23 mm."),
M("JEE Main 2024 · 4 Apr · Shift 2","yd","Superposition and intensity","The width of one of the two slits in a Young's double slit experiment is 4 times that of the other slit. The ratio of the maximum to the minimum intensity in the interference pattern is",["4 : 1","1 : 1","9 : 1","16 : 1"],2,"Intensities are in the ratio of the widths, 4 : 1, so the amplitudes are 2 : 1.\n{I_max;I_min} = ({2 + 1;2 − 1})² = 9."),
M("JEE Main 2024 · 29 Jan · Shift 1","yd","Superposition and intensity","In Young's double slit experiment, light from two identical sources are superimposing on a screen. The path difference between the two lights reaching at a point on the screen is 7{λ;4}. The ratio of intensity of fringe at this point with respect to the maximum intensity of the fringe is",["{1;3}","{1;2}","{3;4}","{1;4}"],1,"Phase difference φ = 2π × {7;4} = {7π;2}. I = I_max cos²({φ;2}) = I_max cos²({7π;4}) = I_max × {1;2}.\nThe ratio is {1;2}."),
M("JEE Main 2023 · 8 Apr · Shift 2","yd","Fringe width","The width of fringe is 2 mm on the screen in a double slits experiment for the light of wavelength 400 nm. The width of the fringe for the light of wavelength 600 nm will be",["2 mm","4 mm","3 mm","1.33 mm"],2,"β ∝ λ: {β₂;β₁} = {600;400}, so β₂ = 2 × 1.5 = 3 mm."),
M("JEE Main 2023 · 10 Apr · Shift 2","yd","Superposition and intensity","The ratio of intensities at two points P and Q on the screen in a Young's double slit experiment where phase difference between two waves of same amplitude are {π;3} and {π;2}, respectively are",["1 : 3","3 : 2","2 : 3","3 : 1"],1,"I = 4I₀cos²({φ;2}). At P: 4I₀cos²(30°) = 3I₀. At Q: 4I₀cos²(45°) = 2I₀.\nI_P : I_Q = 3 : 2."),
M("JEE Main 2023 · 13 Apr · Shift 2","yd","Superposition and intensity","In a Young's double slits experiment, the ratio of amplitude of light coming from slits is 2 : 1. The ratio of the maximum to minimum intensity in the interference pattern is",["25 : 9","9 : 4","2 : 1","9 : 1"],3,"{I_max;I_min} = ({2 + 1;2 − 1})² = 9 : 1."),
M("JEE Main 2023 · 15 Apr · Shift 1","diff","Single slit diffraction","A single slit of width a is illuminated by a monochromatic light of wavelength 600 nm. The value of 'a' for which first minimum appears at θ = 30° on the screen will be",["0.6 μm","1.2 μm","1.8 μm","3 μm"],1,"First minimum: a sin θ = λ, so a = {600 nm;sin 30°} = 1200 nm = 1.2 μm."),
M("JEE Main 2023 · 25 Jan · Shift 1","yd","Young's double slit: fringes","In Young's double slit experiment, the position of 5ᵗʰ bright fringe from the central maximum is 5 cm. The distance between slit and screen is 1 m and wavelength of used monochromatic light is 600 nm. The separation between the slits is",["12 μm","60 μm","36 μm","48 μm"],1,"Bright fringes: y = {nλD;d}, so d = {5 × 6 × 10⁻⁷ × 1;0.05} = 6 × 10⁻⁵ m = 60 μm."),
M("JEE Main 2023 · 29 Jan · Shift 1","yd","Young's double slit: fringes","In a Young's double slit experiment, two slits are illuminated with a light of wavelength 800 nm. The line joining A₁P is perpendicular to A₁A₂ as shown in the figure (screen at D = 5 cm; P is on the screen opposite A₁). If the first minimum is detected at P, the value of slits separation 'a' will be (the distance of screen from slits D = 5 cm)",["0.4 mm","0.2 mm","0.5 mm","0.1 mm"],1,"The path difference at P is A₂P − A₁P = √(a² + D²) − D = {λ;2} for the first minimum.\nSquaring: a² + D² = D² + Dλ + {λ²;4}, so a² ≈ Dλ = 0.05 × 8 × 10⁻⁷ = 4 × 10⁻⁸ m².\na = 2 × 10⁻⁴ m = 0.2 mm."),
M("JEE Main 2022 · 26 Jul · Shift 1","yd","Fringe width","In Young's double slit experiment, the fringe width is 12 mm. If the entire arrangement is placed in water of refractive index {4;3}, then the fringe width becomes (in mm)",["16","9","48","12"],1,"β ∝ λ and in water λ becomes {λ;n}: β′ = {12;4/3} = 9 mm."),
M("JEE Main 2022 · 24 Jun · Shift 2","yd","Superposition and intensity","Two light beams of intensities in the ratio of 9 : 4 are allowed to interfere. The ratio of the intensity of maxima and minima will be",["2 : 3","16 : 81","25 : 169","25 : 1"],3,"Amplitudes are in the ratio 3 : 2: {I_max;I_min} = ({3 + 2;3 − 2})² = 25 : 1."),
M("JEE Main 2022 · 25 Jun · Shift 1","yd","Superposition and intensity","The two light beams having intensities I and 9I interfere to produce a fringe pattern on a screen. The phase difference between the beams is {π;2} at point P and π at point Q. Then the difference between the resultant intensities at P and Q will be",["2I","6I","5I","7I"],1,"I_res = I₁ + I₂ + 2√(I₁I₂) cos φ with √(I₁I₂) = 3I.\nAt P (φ = π/2): 10I. At Q (φ = π): 10I − 6I = 4I.\nDifference = 6I."),
M("JEE Main 2022 · 25 Jun · Shift 2","yd","Superposition and intensity","The interference pattern is obtained with two coherent light sources of intensity ratio 4 : 1. And the ratio {I_max + I_min;I_max − I_min} is {5;x}. Then, the value of x will be equal to",["3","4","2","1"],1,"The amplitudes are in the ratio 2 : 1, so I_max : I_min = 9 : 1.\n{9 + 1;9 − 1} = {10;8} = {5;4}, so x = 4."),
M("JEE Main 2022 · 28 Jun · Shift 2","yd","Shift of fringes","In young's double slit experiment performed using a monochromatic light of wavelength λ, when a glass plate (μ = 1.5) of thickness xλ is introduced in the path of the one of the interfering beams, the intensity at the position where the central maximum occurred previously remains unchanged. The value of x will be",["3","2","1.5","0.5"],1,"The plate adds an optical path (μ − 1)xλ = 0.5xλ.\nThe intensity there stays the same (a maximum) if this is a whole number of wavelengths: 0.5x = 1 gives x = 2 (x = 3 would give 1.5λ, a minimum)."),
M("JEE Main 2022 · 29 Jun · Shift 1","yd","Fringe width","Using Young's double slit experiment, a monochromatic light of wavelength 5000 Å produces fringes of fringe width 0.5 mm. If another monochromatic light of wavelength 6000 Å is used and the separation between the slits is doubled, then the new fringe width will be",["0.5 mm","1.0 mm","0.6 mm","0.3 mm"],3,"β = {λD;d}: β′ = 0.5 × {6000;5000} × {1;2} = 0.3 mm."),
M("JEE Main 2021 · 27 Aug · Shift 2","yd","Superposition and intensity","The light waves from two coherent sources have same intensity I₁ = I₂ = I₀. In interference pattern the intensity of light at minima is zero. What will be the intensity of light at maxima?",["I₀","2I₀","4I₀","5I₀"],2,"I_max = (√I₁ + √I₂)² = (2√I₀)² = 4I₀."),
M("JEE Main 2021 · 25 Jul · Shift 1","yd","Fringe width","In the Young's double slit experiment, the distance between the slits varies in time as d(t) = d₀ + a₀ sin ωt; where d₀, ω and a₀ are constants. The difference between the largest fringe width and the smallest fringe width obtained over time is given as",["{λD;d₀ + a₀}","{λD;d₀²}a₀","{2λDa₀;d₀² − a₀²}","{2λD(d₀);d₀² − a₀²}"],2,"β = {λD;d}. Largest: {λD;d₀ − a₀}; smallest: {λD;d₀ + a₀}.\nDifference = λD({1;d₀ − a₀} − {1;d₀ + a₀}) = {2λDa₀;d₀² − a₀²}."),
M("JEE Main 2021 · 27 Jul · Shift 1","yd","Fringe width","In Young's double slit experiment, if the source of light changes from orange to blue, then",["the central bright fringe will become a dark fringe","the distance between consecutive fringes will decrease","the distance between consecutive fringes will increase","the intensity of the minima will increase."],1,"The fringe width β = {λD;d} is proportional to λ. Blue has a smaller wavelength than orange, so the fringes come closer. The central fringe stays bright (zero path difference)."),
M("JEE Main 2021 · 18 Mar · Shift 1","yd","Fringe width","In a Young's double slit arrangement, slits are separated by a gap of 0.5 mm, and the screen is placed at a distance of 0.5 m from them. The distance between the first and the third bright fringe formed when the slits are illuminated by a monochromatic light of 5890 Å is",["5890 × 10⁻⁷ m","1178 × 10⁻⁹ m","1178 × 10⁻¹² m","1178 × 10⁻⁶ m"],3,"The first and third bright fringes are 2β apart: 2 × {λD;d} = {2 × 5.89 × 10⁻⁷ × 0.5;5 × 10⁻⁴} = 1.178 × 10⁻³ m = 1178 × 10⁻⁶ m."),
M("JEE Main 2021 · 24 Feb · Shift 1","yd","Superposition and intensity","In a Young's double slit experiment, the width of one of the slit is three times the other slit. The amplitude of the light coming from a slit is proportional to the slit-width. Find the ratio of the maximum to the minimum intensity in the interference pattern.",["1 : 4","3 : 1","4 : 1","2 : 1"],2,"The amplitudes are in the ratio 3 : 1, so {I_max;I_min} = ({3 + 1;3 − 1})² = 4.\nThe ratio is 4 : 1."),
M("JEE Main 2021 · 24 Feb · Shift 2","yd","Fringe width","If the source of light used in a Young's double slit experiment is changed from red to violet",["the intensity of minima will increase.","the central bright fringe will become a dark fringe.","the fringes will become brighter.","consecutive fringe lines will come closer."],3,"β ∝ λ and violet has a shorter wavelength than red, so consecutive fringes come closer."),
M("JEE Main 2021 · 25 Feb · Shift 1","yd","Superposition and intensity","Two coherent light sources having intensity in the ratio 2x produce an interference pattern. The ratio {I_max − I_min;I_max + I_min} will be",["{2√(2x);x + 1}","{√(2x);x + 1}","{√(2x);2x + 1}","{2√(2x);2x + 1}"],3,"With I₁ : I₂ = 2x : 1 the amplitudes are √(2x) : 1.\n{I_max − I_min;I_max + I_min} = {2a₁a₂;a₁² + a₂²} = {2√(2x);2x + 1}."),
M("JEE Main 2021 · 26 Feb · Shift 1","yd","Fringe width","In a Young's double slit experiment two slits are separated by 2 mm and the screen is placed one meter away. When a light of wavelength 500 nm is used, the fringe separation will be",["0.75 mm","0.25 mm","1 mm","0.50 mm"],1,"β = {λD;d} = {500 × 10⁻⁹ × 1;2 × 10⁻³} = 2.5 × 10⁻⁴ m = 0.25 mm."),
M("JEE Main 2020 · 2 Sep · Shift 1","yd","Young's double slit: fringes","Interference fringes are observed on a screen by illuminating two thin slits 1 mm apart with a light source (λ = 632.8 nm). The distance between the screen and the slits is 100 cm. If a bright fringe is observed on a screen at a distance of 1.27 mm from the central bright fringe, then the path difference between the waves, which are reaching this point from the slits is close to",["1.27 μm","2.87 nm","2 nm","2.05 μm"],0,"Path difference = {dy;D} = {1 × 10⁻³ × 1.27 × 10⁻³;1} = 1.27 × 10⁻⁶ m = 1.27 μm."),
M("JEE Main 2020 · 2 Sep · Shift 2","yd","Fringe width","In a Young's double slit experiment, 16 fringes are observed in a certain segment of the screen when light of wavelength 700 nm is used. If the wavelength of light is changed to 400 nm, the number of fringes observed in the same segment of the screen would be",["24","28","30","18"],1,"The number of fringes in a fixed segment is inversely proportional to λ: N = 16 × {700;400} = 28."),
M("JEE Main 2020 · 3 Sep · Shift 1","yd","Young's double slit: fringes","In a Young's double slit experiment, light of 500 nm is used to produce an interference pattern. When the distance between the slits is 0.05 mm, the angular spacing (in degree) of the fringes formed on the distance screen is close to",["0.17°","0.57°","1.7°","0.07°"],1,"Angular fringe width = {λ;d} = {5 × 10⁻⁷;5 × 10⁻⁵} = 0.01 rad.\n0.01 rad = 0.01 × {180°;π} = 0.57°."),
M("JEE Main 2020 · 6 Sep · Shift 1","yd","Superposition and intensity","In the given figure, P and Q are two equally intense coherent sources emitting radiation of wavelength 20 m. The separation between P and Q is 5 m and the phase of P is ahead of that of Q by 90°. A, B and C are three distinct points of observation, each equidistant from the midpoint of PQ (A on the line PQ beyond Q, C on the line PQ beyond P, and B on the perpendicular bisector of PQ). The intensities of radiation at A, B, C will be in the ratio",["0 : 1 : 4","2 : 1 : 0","0 : 1 : 2","4 : 1 : 0"],1,"The separation is {λ;4}, a path difference of 90°.\nAt A the wave from P travels 5 m farther (lags 90°), which cancels P's 90° lead: in phase, I = 4I₀.\nAt C the wave from Q travels farther and Q already lags: 180° apart, I = 0.\nAt B the phase difference is 90°: I = 2I₀. So A : B : C = 4 : 2 : 0 = 2 : 1 : 0."),
M("JEE Main 2020 · 7 Jan · Shift 2","yd","Fringe width","In a Young's double slit experiment, the separation between the slits is 0.15 mm. In the experiment, a source of light of wavelength 589 nm is used and the interference pattern is observed on a screen kept 1.5 m away. The separation between the successive bright fringes on the screen is",["5.9 mm","4.9 mm","6.9 mm","3.9 mm"],0,"β = {λD;d} = {589 × 10⁻⁹ × 1.5;0.15 × 10⁻³} = 5.89 × 10⁻³ m ≈ 5.9 mm."),
M("JEE Main 2020 · 8 Jan · Shift 2","yd","Superposition and intensity","In a double-slit experiment, at a certain point on the screen the path difference between the two interfering waves is {1;8}ᵗʰ of a wavelength. The ratio of the intensity of light at that point to that at the centre of a bright fringe is",["0.568","0.853","0.760","0.672"],1,"φ = {2π;λ} × {λ;8} = {π;4}. I = I_max cos²({φ;2}) = I_max cos²({π;8}).\ncos²(22.5°) = 0.854, so the ratio is 0.853."),
M("JEE Main 2019 · 8 Apr · Shift 1","yd","Superposition and intensity","In an interference experiment the ratio of amplitudes of coherent waves is {a₁;a₂} = {1;3}. The ratio of maximum and minimum intensities of fringes will be",["4","9","2","18"],0,"{I_max;I_min} = ({a₁ + a₂;a₂ − a₁})² = ({1 + 3;3 − 1})² = 4."),
M("JEE Main 2019 · 10 Apr · Shift 2","yd","Superposition and intensity","In a Young's double slit experiment, the ratio of the slit's width is 4 : 1. The ratio of the intensity of maxima to minima, close to the central fringe on the screen, will be",["(√3 + 1)⁴ : 16","9 : 1","25 : 9","4 : 1"],1,"Intensity from a slit is proportional to its width: I₁ : I₂ = 4 : 1, so the amplitudes are 2 : 1.\n{I_max;I_min} = ({2 + 1;2 − 1})² = 9 : 1."),
M("JEE Main 2019 · 12 Apr · Shift 1","yd","Shift of fringes","In a double slit experiment, when a thin film of thickness t having refractive index μ is introduced in front of one of the slits, the maximum at the centre of the fringe pattern shifts by one fringe width. The value of t is (λ is the wavelength of the light used)",["{λ;μ − 1}","{λ;2μ − 1}","{2λ;μ − 1}","{λ;2(μ − 1)}"],0,"The film adds an optical path (μ − 1)t. A shift of one fringe width means this equals one wavelength: (μ − 1)t = λ.\nt = {λ;μ − 1}."),
M("JEE Main 2019 · 9 Jan · Shift 1","yd","Superposition and intensity","Two coherent sources produce waves of different intensities which interfere. After interference, the ratio of the maximum intensity to the minimum intensity is 16. The intensity of the waves are in the ratio",["16 : 9","5 : 3","25 : 9","4 : 1"],2,"{I_max;I_min} = ({a₁ + a₂;a₁ − a₂})² = 16, so {a₁ + a₂;a₁ − a₂} = 4 and a₁ = {5;3}a₂.\nThe intensities are in the ratio 25 : 9."),
M("JEE Main 2019 · 9 Jan · Shift 2","yd","Young's double slit: fringes","In a Young's double slit experiment, the slits are placed 0.320 mm apart. Light of wavelength λ = 500 nm is incident on the slits. The total number of bright fringes that are observed in the angular range −30° ≤ θ ≤ 30° is",["641","321","640","320"],0,"Bright fringes: d sin θ = mλ. At θ = 30°: m = {0.320 × 10⁻³ × 0.5;500 × 10⁻⁹} = 320.\nm runs from −320 to +320, including 0: 641 fringes."),
M("JEE Main 2019 · 10 Jan · Shift 1","yd","Two wavelengths","In a Young's double slit experiment with slit separation 0.1 mm, one observes a bright fringe at angle {1;40} rad by using light of wavelength λ₁. When the light of wavelength λ₂ is used a bright fringe is seen at the same angle in the same set up. Given that λ₁ and λ₂ are in visible range (380 nm to 740 nm), their values are",["400 nm, 500 nm","625 nm, 500 nm","380 nm, 525 nm","380 nm, 500 nm"],1,"d sin θ ≈ dθ = 0.1 × 10⁻³ × {1;40} = 2.5 × 10⁻⁶ m = mλ.\nλ = {2500 nm;m}: m = 4 gives 625 nm, m = 5 gives 500 nm, m = 6 gives 417 nm. Of the options only (625 nm, 500 nm) are both of this form."),
M("JEE Main 2019 · 10 Jan · Shift 2","yd","Young's double slit: fringes","Consider a Young's double slit experiment as shown in figure (the slit separation is d, the screen is at a distance 2d from the slits, and the point P on the screen is directly in front of the slit S₁). What should be the slit separation d in terms of wavelength λ such that the first minima occurs directly in front of the slit (S₁)?",["{λ;2(√5 − 2)}","{λ;(5 − √2)}","{λ;(√5 − 2)}","{λ;2(5 − √2)}"],0,"P is opposite S₁. The paths are S₁P = 2d and S₂P = √(d² + (2d)²) = d√5.\nFirst minimum: path difference d(√5 − 2) = {λ;2}.\nd = {λ;2(√5 − 2)}."),
M("JEE Main 2019 · 11 Jan · Shift 1","yd","Superposition and intensity","In a Young's double slit experiment, the path difference, at a certain point on the screen, between two interfering waves is {1;8}ᵗʰ of wavelength. The ratio of the intensity at this point to that at the centre of a bright fringe is close to",["0.80","0.94","0.85","0.74"],2,"φ = {2π;λ} × {λ;8} = {π;4}, so {I;I_max} = cos²({φ;2}) = cos²(22.5°) = 0.854.\nThe ratio is about 0.85."),
M("JEE Main 2017","yd","Two wavelengths","In a Young's double slit experiment, slits are separated by 0.5 mm and the screen is placed 150 cm away. A beam of light consisting of two wavelengths, 650 nm and 520 nm, is used to obtain interference fringes on the screen. The least distance from the common central maximum to the point where the bright fringes due to both the wavelengths coincide is",["1.56 mm","7.8 mm","9.75 mm","15.6 mm"],1,"Coincidence: n₁ × 650 = n₂ × 520, so {n₁;n₂} = {4;5}. The first coincidence is the 4th fringe of 650 nm.\ny = {4λD;d} = {4 × 650 × 10⁻⁹ × 1.5;0.5 × 10⁻³} = 7.8 mm."),
M("JEE Main 2016 · Online","yd","Fringe width","In Young's double slit experiment, the distance between the slits and the screen is 1.0 m and monochromatic light of 600 nm is being used. A person standing near the slits is looking at the fringe pattern. When the separation between the slits is varied, the interference pattern disappears for a particular distance d₀ between the slits. If the angular resolution of the eye is {1°;60}, the value of d₀ is close to",["1 mm","3 mm","2 mm","4 mm"],2,"The pattern disappears when the angular fringe width {λ;d₀} equals the eye's resolution {1°;60} = {π;10800} = 2.9 × 10⁻⁴ rad.\nd₀ = {6 × 10⁻⁷;2.9 × 10⁻⁴} ≈ 2 mm."),
M("JEE Main 2015 · Online","yd","Superposition and intensity","In a Young's double slit experiment with light of wavelength λ the separation of slits is d and distance of screen is D such that D ≫ d ≫ λ. If the fringe width is β, the distance from point of maximum intensity to the point where intensity falls to half of maximum intensity on either side is",["{β;2}","{β;4}","{β;3}","{β;6}"],1,"I = I_max cos²({φ;2}) = {I_max;2} when φ = {π;2}, which is a path difference of {λ;4}.\nThe distance is {λ;4} × {D;d} = {β;4}."),
M("JEE Main 2014 · Online","yd","Shift of fringes","Using monochromatic light of wavelength λ, an experimentalist sets up the Young's double slit experiment in three ways: (i) slits S₁, S₂ separated by d with the screen at distance D, giving fringe width β; (ii) the same, with a transparent mica sheet of thickness t = 1.8 μm and refractive index μ = 1.6 in front of one slit, the central fringe shifting to a point at distance y from O; (iii) the same as (i) but with the screen at distance 2D, giving fringe width β′. If she observes that y = β′, the wavelength of light used is",["520 nm","540 nm","560 nm","580 nm"],1,"With the mica sheet the pattern shifts by y = {(μ − 1)tD;d}. With the screen at 2D the fringe width is β′ = {2λD;d}.\ny = β′ gives (μ − 1)t = 2λ, so λ = {0.6 × 1.8 μm;2} = 0.54 μm = 540 nm."),
M("JEE Main 2014 · Online","yd","Reflection and interference","An interference pattern is observed at P due to superposition of two rays coming out from a source S as shown in the figure (S and P are 2l apart on a line parallel to a perfectly reflecting surface R; one ray goes directly and the other reaches the surface at 30° and is reflected to P). The value of l for which maxima is obtained at P is (R is perfect reflecting surface)",["l = {2nλ;√3 − 1}","l = {(2n − 1)λ;2(√3 − 1)}","l = {(2n − 1)λ√3;4(2 − √3)}","l = {(2n − 1)λ;√3 − 1}"],2,"Direct path 2l. The reflected ray travels l/cos 30° on each way: {4l;√3}.\nPath difference = {4l;√3} − 2l = {2l(2 − √3);√3}. Reflection from the surface adds a phase jump of π, so maxima need a path difference of (2n − 1){λ;2}.\n{2l(2 − √3);√3} = (2n − 1){λ;2}, giving l = {(2n − 1)λ√3;4(2 − √3)}."),
M("JEE Main 2013","yd","Young's double slit: fringes","Two coherent point sources S₁ and S₂ are separated by a small distance 'd' as shown (the line S₁S₂ is perpendicular to the screen, at distance D). The fringes obtained on the screen will be",["concentric circles","points","straight lines","semi-circles"],0,"The sources lie on a line perpendicular to the screen. For a given path difference nλ the points form a circle about this axis: the fringes are concentric circles."),
M("JEE Main 2013 · Online","yd","Two wavelengths","The source that illuminates the double-slit in 'double-slit interference experiment' emits two distinct monochromatic waves of wavelength 500 nm and 600 nm, each of them producing its own pattern on the screen. At the central point of the pattern when path difference is zero, maxima of both the patterns coincide and the resulting interference pattern is most distinct at the region of zero path difference. But as one moves out of this central region, the two fringe systems are gradually out of step such that maximum due to one wavelength coincides with the minimum due to the other and the combined fringe system becomes completely indistinct. This may happen when path difference (in nm) is",["2000","3000","1000","1500"],3,"We need a path difference Δ that is a whole number of 500 nm and a half-odd number of 600 nm (or the reverse).\nΔ = 1500 nm: 1500/500 = 3 (a maximum for 500 nm) and 1500/600 = 2.5 (a minimum for 600 nm).\nThe other options fail (2000 gives 4 and 3.33, 1000 gives 2 and 1.67, 3000 gives 6 and 5, maxima for both)."),
M("JEE Advanced 2013","yd","Superposition and intensity","In the Young's double slit experiment using a monochromatic light of wavelength λ, the path difference (in terms of an integer n) corresponding to any point having half the peak intensity is",["(2n + 1){λ;2}","(2n + 1){λ;4}","(2n + 1){λ;8}","(2n + 1){λ;16}"],1,"I = I_max cos²({φ;2}) = {I_max;2} requires {φ;2} = {π;4} + {nπ;2}, i.e. φ = (2n + 1){π;2}.\nPath difference = {λ;2π}φ = (2n + 1){λ;4}."),
M("AIEEE 2012","yd","Superposition and intensity","In Young's double slit experiment, one of the slit is wider than other, so that the amplitude of the light from one slit is double of that from other slit. If I_m be the maximum intensity, the resultant intensity I when they interfere at phase difference φ is given by",["{I_m;3}(1 + 2cos²{φ;2})","{I_m;5}(1 + 4cos²{φ;2})","{I_m;9}(1 + 8cos²{φ;2})","{I_m;9}(4 + 5cosφ)"],2,"With amplitudes 2a and a: I = a²(4 + 1 + 4cosφ) = a²(5 + 4cosφ), and I_m = 9a².\nUsing 4cosφ = 8cos²({φ;2}) − 4: I = a²(1 + 8cos²{φ;2}) = {I_m;9}(1 + 8cos²{φ;2})."),
M("AIEEE 2012 · Online","yd","Young's double slit: fringes","The maximum number of possible interference maxima for slit separation equal to 1.8 λ, where λ is the wavelength of light used, in a Young's double slit experiment is",["zero","3","infinite","5"],1,"Maxima need d sin θ = mλ with |sin θ| ≤ 1, so |m| ≤ {1.8λ;λ} = 1.8.\nm = 0, ±1: three maxima."),
M("IIT-JEE 2012","yd","Fringe width","Young's double slit experiment is carried out by using green, red and blue light, one colour at a time. The fringe widths recorded are β_G, β_R and β_B, respectively. Then,",["β_G > β_B > β_R","β_B > β_G > β_R","β_R > β_B > β_G","β_R > β_G > β_B"],3,"β ∝ λ. Red has the longest wavelength and blue the shortest: β_R > β_G > β_B."),
M("AIEEE 2009","yd","Two wavelengths","A mixture of light, consisting of wavelength 590 nm and an unknown wavelength, illuminates Young's double slit and gives rise to two overlapping interference patterns on the screen. The central maximum of both lights coincide. Further, it is observed that the third bright fringe of known light coincides with the 4th bright fringe of the unknown light. From this data, the wavelength of the unknown light is",["393.4 nm","885.0 nm","442.5 nm","776.8 nm"],2,"3 × 590 = 4 × λ, so λ = {1770;4} = 442.5 nm."),
M("AIEEE 2007","yd","Superposition and intensity","In a Young's double slit experiment the intensity at a point where the path difference is {λ;6} (λ being the wavelength of light used) is I. If I₀ denotes the maximum intensity, {I;I₀} is equal to",["{3;4}","{1;√2}","{√3;2}","{1;2}"],0,"φ = {2π;λ} × {λ;6} = {π;3}. {I;I₀} = cos²({φ;2}) = cos²(30°) = {3;4}."),
M("AIEEE 2005","yd","Young's double slit: fringes","A Young's double slit experiment uses a monochromatic source. The shape of the interference fringes formed on a screen is",["straight line","parabola","hyperbola","circle"],2,"Fringes are the loci of points with a constant difference of distances from the two slits (a constant path difference): hyperbolas. On a screen far away, a small part of a hyperbola looks like a straight line, but the actual shape is a hyperbola."),
M("IIT-JEE 2005","yd","Superposition and intensity","In Young's double slit experiment intensity at a point is ({1;4}) of the maximum intensity. Angular position of this point is",["sin⁻¹({λ;d})","sin⁻¹({λ;2d})","sin⁻¹({λ;3d})","sin⁻¹({λ;4d})."],2,"{I;I_max} = cos²({φ;2}) = {1;4}, so {φ;2} = 60° and φ = {2π;3}: the path difference is {λ;3}.\nd sin θ = {λ;3}, so θ = sin⁻¹({λ;3d})."),
M("AIEEE 2004","yd","Young's double slit: fringes","The maximum number of possible interference maxima for slit-separation equal to twice the wavelength in Young's double-slit experiment is",["infinite","five","three","zero"],1,"d = 2λ: |m| ≤ {d;λ} = 2, so m = 0, ±1, ±2: five maxima (the two at ±2 are at grazing 90°, counted as the limit)."),
M("IIT-JEE 2004","yd","Two wavelengths","Monochromatic light of wavelength 400 nm and 560 nm are incident simultaneously and normally on double slit apparatus whose slit separation is 0.1 mm and screen distance is 1 m. Distance between areas of total darkness will be",["4 mm","5.6 mm","14 mm","28 mm."],3,"Total darkness needs a minimum for both: (2m + 1) × 200 nm = (2n + 1) × 280 nm, so (2m + 1) : (2n + 1) = 7 : 5.\nThe smallest path difference is 1400 nm, the next 4200 nm (the odd multiples 7, 21, …).\ny = {Δ D;d}: 14 mm and 42 mm, a spacing of 28 mm."),
M("AIEEE 2003","yd","Coherence","To demonstrate the phenomenon of interference we require two sources which emit radiation of",["nearly the same frequency","the same frequency","different wavelength","the same frequency and having a definite phase relationship."],3,"A steady pattern needs coherent sources: the same frequency and a constant phase difference."),
M("IIT-JEE 2002","yd","Shift of fringes","In the ideal double-slit experiment, when a glass-plate (refractive index 1.5) of thickness t is introduced in the path of one of the interfering beams (wavelength λ), the intensity at the position where the central maximum occurred previously remains unchanged. The minimum thickness of the glass-plate is",["2λ","{2λ;3}","{λ;3}","λ"],0,"The extra optical path (μ − 1)t = 0.5t must be a whole number of wavelengths for the intensity at that point to stay the same.\nThe smallest nonzero value is 0.5t = λ, so t = 2λ."),
M("IIT-JEE 2001","yd","Fringe width","In Young's double slit experiment, 12 fringes are observed to be formed in a certain segment of the screen when light of wavelength 600 nm is used. If the wavelength of light is changed to 400 nm, number of fringes observed in the same segment of the screen is given by",["12","18","24","30"],1,"The number of fringes in a given segment is inversely proportional to λ: 12 × {600;400} = 18."),
M("IIT-JEE 2001","yd","Superposition and intensity","Two beams of light having intensities I and 4I interfere to produce a fringe pattern on a screen. The phase difference between the beams is {π;2} at point A and π at point B. Then the difference between the resultant intensities at A and B is",["2I","4I","5I","7I"],1,"I_res = I + 4I + 2√(4)I cosφ = 5I + 4I cosφ.\nAt A: 5I. At B: 5I − 4I = I. Difference = 4I."),
M("IIT-JEE 2000","yd","Superposition and intensity","In a double slit experiment instead of taking slits of equal widths, one slit is made twice as wide as the other. Then, in the interference pattern",["the intensities of both the maxima and the minima increase","the intensity of the maxima increases and the minima has zero intensity","the intensity of the maxima decreases and that of the minima increases","the intensity of the maxima decreases and the minima has zero intensity."],0,"Widening one slit adds light: I_max = (√I₁ + √I₂)² rises above its equal-slit value, and I_min = (√I₂ − √I₁)² rises from 0 to a small non-zero value (for example with 2I₁ it is 0.17I₁).\nSo both rise."),
M("IIT-JEE 1988","yd","Superposition and intensity","Two coherent monochromatic light beams of intensities I and 4I are superposed. The maximum and minimum possible intensities in the resulting beam are",["5I and I","5I and 3I","9I and I","9I and 3I."],2,"I_max = (√I + √(4I))² = 9I and I_min = (2√I − √I)² = I."),
M("IIT-JEE 1981","yd","Fringe width","In Young's double-slit experiment, the separation between the slits is halved and the distance between the slits and the screen is doubled. The fringe width is",["unchanged","halved","doubled","quadrupled."],3,"β = {λD;d}. With D doubled and d halved: β′ = {2D;d/2}·λ = 4β."),
M("JEE Advanced 2022","yd","Phase difference at the detector","A double slit setup is shown in the figure. One of the slits is in medium 2 of refractive index n₂. The other slit is at the interface of this medium with another medium 1 of refractive index n₁ (≠ n₂). The line joining the slits is perpendicular to the interface and the distance between the slits is d. The slit widths are much smaller than d. A monochromatic parallel beam of light is incident on the slits from medium 1. A detector is placed in medium 2 at a large distance from the slits, and at an angle θ from the line joining them, so that θ equals the angle of refraction of the beam. Consider two approximately parallel rays from the slits received by the detector. Which of the following statement(s) is(are) correct?",["The phase difference between the two rays is independent of d.","The two rays interfere constructively at the detector.","The phase difference between the two rays depends on n₁ but is independent of n₂.","The phase difference between the two rays vanishes only for certain values of d and the angle of incidence of the beam, with θ being the corresponding angle of refraction."],[0,1],"The wave refracted into medium 2 has wave-vector k₂ along the direction θ. The phase of that wave at the second slit differs from the first by k₂ d cos θ (displacement along the line of slits).\nThe detector lies along the same direction, so the extra distance travelled by the ray from the first slit is exactly d cos θ with the same k₂, and the two cancel.\nSo the phase difference is zero for every d (independent of d and of n₁ and n₂): the rays interfere constructively. Only (a) and (b) are true."),
M("JEE Advanced 2019","yd","Shift of the pattern with angle of incidence","In a Young's double slit experiment, the slit separation d is 0.3 mm and the screen distance D is 1 m. A parallel beam of light of wavelength 600 nm is incident on the slits at angle α as shown in figure. On the screen, the point O is equidistant from the slits and distance PO is 11.0 mm. Which of the following statement(s) is/are correct?",["For α = 0, there will be constructive interference at point P.","For α = {0.36;π} degree, there will be destructive interference at point P.","For α = {0.36;π} degree, there will be destructive interference at point O.","Fringe spacing depends on α."],1,"For α = 0: path difference at P = {d y;D} = {0.3 × 10⁻³ × 11 × 10⁻³;1} = 3.3 μm = 5.5λ: destructive, so (a) is false.\nα = {0.36;π}° = 0.002 rad adds a path difference d sin α = 0.6 μm = 1λ at O: constructive, so (c) is false. At P the total is 5.5λ ± 1λ = 4.5λ or 6.5λ: destructive, so (b) is true.\nThe fringe width {λD;d} does not depend on α, so (d) is false."),
M("JEE Advanced 2017","yd","Fringes on a circle","Two coherent monochromatic point sources S₁ and S₂ of wavelength λ = 600 nm are placed symmetrically on either side of the center of a circle (on a diameter). The sources are separated by a distance d = 1.8 mm. This arrangement produces interference fringes visible as alternate bright and dark spots on the circumference of the circle. P₁ is the point of the circle on the perpendicular bisector of S₁S₂, and P₂ is the point on the circle at the end of the diameter through the sources. The angular separation between two consecutive bright spots is Δθ. Which of the following options is/are correct?",["The angular separation between two consecutive bright spots decreases as we move from P₁ to P₂ along the first quadrant.","At P₂ the order of the fringe will be maximum.","A dark spot will be formed at the point P₂.","The total number of fringes produced between P₁ and P₂ in the first quadrant is close to 3000."],[1,3],"At angle θ from the axis the path difference is d cos θ, so consecutive bright spots satisfy d sin θ Δθ = λ and Δθ = {λ;d sin θ}. This grows as θ falls from 90° (P₁) to 0° (P₂): (a) is false.\nAt P₂ the path difference is d = {1.8 × 10⁻³;6 × 10⁻⁷}λ = 3000λ: the largest order, and a bright spot, so (b) is true and (c) is false.\nFrom order 0 at P₁ to 3000 at P₂ there are about 3000 fringes: (d) is true."),
M("JEE Advanced 2016","yd","Fringes on a screen beyond the source line","While conducting the Young's double slit experiment, a student replaced the two slits with a large opaque plate in the x-y plane containing two small holes that act as two coherent point sources (S₁, S₂) emitting light of wavelength 600 nm. The student mistakenly placed the screen parallel to the x-z plane (for z > 0) at a distance D = 3 m from the mid-point of S₁S₂, as shown schematically in the figure. The distance between the sources d = 0.6003 mm. The origin O is at the intersection of the screen and the line joining S₁S₂. Which of the following is (are) true of the intensity pattern on the screen?",["Hyperbolic bright and dark bands with foci symmetrically placed about O in the x-direction","Straight bright and dark bands parallel to the x-axis","Semi circular bright and dark bands centered at point O","The region very close to the point O will be dark"],[2,3],"The sources lie on the y-axis, which meets the screen at O. By symmetry about this axis, points at the same distance from O have the same path difference, so the bands are circles about O; only the half z > 0 is on the screen, giving semicircles: (c) is true.\nAt O the path difference is the full separation: d = 0.6003 mm = {0.6003 × 10⁻³;6 × 10⁻⁷}λ = 1000.5λ, a half-integer multiple, so O is dark: (d) is true."),
M("JEE Advanced 2014","yd","Two wavelengths","A light source, which emits two wavelengths λ₁ = 400 nm and λ₂ = 600 nm, is used in a Young's double slit experiment. If recorded fringe widths for λ₁ and λ₂ are β₁ and β₂ and the number of fringes for them within a distance y on one side of the central maximum are m₁ and m₂, respectively, then",["β₂ > β₁","m₁ > m₂","From the central maximum, 3ʳᵈ maximum of λ₂ overlaps with 5ᵗʰ minimum of λ₁.","The angular separation of fringes for λ₁ is greater than λ₂."],[0,1,2],"β = {λD;d} ∝ λ, so β₂ > β₁ (a) and the number of fringes in a fixed distance, {y;β}, is larger for λ₁ (b).\n3rd maximum of λ₂: 3 × 600 = 1800 nm. 5th minimum of λ₁: 4.5 × 400 = 1800 nm: they coincide (c).\nThe angular separation {λ;d} is smaller for λ₁, so (d) is false."),
M("IIT-JEE 2008","yd","Superposition and intensity","In a Young's double slit experiment, the separation between the two slits is d and the wavelength of the light is λ. The intensity of light falling on slit 1 is four times the intensity of light falling on slit 2. Choose the correct choice(s).",["If d = λ, the screen will contain only one maximum.","If λ < d < 2λ, at least one more maximum (besides the central maximum) will be observed on the screen.","If the intensity of light falling on slit 1 is reduced so that it becomes equal to that of slit 2, the intensities of the observed dark and bright fringes will increase.","If the intensity of light falling on slit 2 is increased so that it becomes equal to that of slit 1, the intensities of the observed dark and bright fringes will increase."],[0,1],"Maxima need d sin θ = mλ with |sin θ| < 1 on a screen: d = λ allows only m = 0 (m = ±1 would be at 90°); λ < d < 2λ allows m = ±1 as well.\nWith unequal intensities I_min is non-zero; making them equal lowers I_min to zero and lowers I_max (from 9I₂ to 4I₂), so (c) is false. In (d) equalising at I₁ raises I_max (to 4I₁) but lowers I_min to 0, so (d) is false."),
M("IIT-JEE 1984","diff","Missing wavelengths","White light is used to illuminate the two slits in Young's double slit experiment. The separation between the slits is b and the screen is at a distance d (> b) from the slits. At a point on the screen directly in front of one of the slits, certain wavelengths are missing. Some of these missing wavelengths are",["λ = {b²;d}","λ = {2b²;d}","λ = {b²;3d}","λ = {2b²;3d}"],[0,2],"Directly in front of one slit the path difference is √(d² + b²) − d ≈ {b²;2d}.\nA wavelength is missing when this equals (2n + 1){λ;2}: λ = {b²;(2n + 1)d}, i.e. {b²;d}, {b²;3d}, {b²;5d}, …\nSo (a) and (c)."),
M("IIT-JEE 1982","yd","Superposition and intensity","In Young's double slit experiment, the interference pattern is found to have an intensity ratio between the bright and dark fringes as 9. This implies that",["the intensities at the screen due to the two slits are 5 units and 4 units respectively","the intensities at the screen due to the two slits are 4 units and 1 units respectively.","the amplitude ratio is 3","the amplitude ratio is 2"],[1,3],"{I_max;I_min} = ({a + 1;a − 1})² = 9 gives {a + 1;a − 1} = 3 and a = 2, the amplitude ratio.\nThe intensities are in the ratio a² : 1 = 4 : 1."),
N("JEE Main 2025 · 3 Apr · Shift 1","yd","Superposition and intensity","Two coherent monochromatic light beams of intensities 4I and 9I are superimposed. The difference between maximum and minimum intensities in the resulting interference pattern is xI. The value of x is ______.",24,"I_max = (2 + 3)²I = 25I and I_min = (3 − 2)²I = I. The difference is 24I, so x = 24."),
N("JEE Main 2025 · 28 Jan · Shift 1","yd","Fringe width","A double slit interference experiment performed with a light of wavelength 600 nm forms an interference fringe pattern on a screen with 10ᵗʰ bright fringe having its centre at a distance of 10 mm from the central maximum. Distance of the centre of the same 10ᵗʰ bright fringe from the central maximum when the source of light is replaced by another source of wavelength 660 nm would be ______ mm.",11,"y = {nλD;d} is proportional to λ: y′ = 10 × {660;600} = 11 mm."),
N("JEE Main 2025 · 28 Jan · Shift 2","yd","Thin film interference","A thin transparent film with refractive index 1.4, is held on a circular ring of radius 1.8 cm. The fluid in the film evaporates such that transmission through the film at wavelength 560 nm goes to a minimum every 12 seconds. Assuming that the film is flat on its two sides, the rate of evaporation is ______ π × 10⁻¹³ m³/s.",54,"Successive transmission minima (reflection maxima) occur when the thickness changes by {λ;2n} = {560;2.8} = 200 nm.\nVolume lost per 12 s = πR² × 200 nm = π × (0.018)² × 2 × 10⁻⁷ = π × 6.48 × 10⁻¹¹ m³.\nRate = {6.48 × 10⁻¹¹;12} π = 5.4 × 10⁻¹² π = 54 × 10⁻¹³ π m³/s."),
N("JEE Main 2024 · 4 Apr · Shift 1","yd","Two wavelengths","Two wavelengths λ₁ and λ₂ are used in Young's double slit experiment. λ₁ = 450 nm and λ₂ = 650 nm. The minimum order of fringe produced by λ₂ which overlaps with the fringe produced by λ₁ is n. The value of n is ______.",9,"Overlap: n₁ × 450 = n₂ × 650, so {n₁;n₂} = {13;9}. The smallest order for λ₂ is n = 9."),
N("JEE Main 2024 · 5 Apr · Shift 1","yd","Young's double slit: fringes","In Young's double slit experiment, carried out with light of wavelength 5000 Å, the distance between the slits is 0.3 mm and the screen is at 200 cm from the slits. The central maximum is at x = 0 cm. The value of x for third maxima is ______ mm.",10,"x = {3λD;d} = {3 × 5 × 10⁻⁷ × 2;3 × 10⁻⁴} = 10⁻² m = 10 mm."),
N("JEE Main 2024 · 6 Apr · Shift 2","yd","Superposition and intensity","Two coherent monochromatic light beams of intensities I and 4I are superimposed. The difference between maximum and minimum possible intensities in the resulting beam is xI. The value of x is ______.",8,"I_max = 9I and I_min = I, so the difference is 8I and x = 8."),
N("JEE Main 2024 · 8 Apr · Shift 2","diff","Double slit with finite slit width","Two slits are 1 mm apart and the screen is located 1 m away from the slits. A light of wavelength 500 nm is used. The width of each slit to obtain 10 maxima of the double slit pattern within the central maximum of the single slit pattern is ______ × 10⁻⁴ m.",2,"Fringe width of the double slit: β = {λD;d} = {5 × 10⁻⁷ × 1;10⁻³} = 0.5 mm. Ten maxima fill a central width of 10β = 5 mm.\nThe single-slit central maximum has width {2λD;a} = 5 mm, so a = {2 × 5 × 10⁻⁷;5 × 10⁻³} = 2 × 10⁻⁴ m."),
N("JEE Main 2024 · 9 Apr · Shift 1","yd","Superposition and intensity","In a Young's double slit experiment, the intensity at a point is ({1;4})ᵗʰ of the maximum intensity, the minimum distance of the point from the central maximum is ______ μm. [Given: λ = 600 nm, d = 1.0 mm, D = 1.0 m]",200,"I = I_max cos²({φ;2}) = {I_max;4} gives {φ;2} = 60°, so φ = {2π;3}: path difference {λ;3} = 200 nm.\ny = {Δ D;d} = {200 × 10⁻⁹ × 1;10⁻³} = 2 × 10⁻⁴ m = 200 μm."),
N("JEE Main 2024 · 9 Apr · Shift 2","yd","Shift of fringes","Monochromatic light of wavelength 500 nm is used in Young's double slit experiment. An interference pattern is obtained on a screen. When one of the slits is covered with a very thin glass plate (refractive index = 1.5), the central maximum is shifted to a position previously occupied by the 4ᵗʰ bright fringe. The thickness of the glass-plate is ______ μm.",4,"The plate adds an optical path (μ − 1)t = 4λ.\nt = {4 × 500 nm;0.5} = 4000 nm = 4 μm."),
N("JEE Main 2024 · 1 Feb · Shift 2","yd","Superposition and intensity","In Young's double slit experiment, monochromatic light of wavelength 5000 Å is used. The slits are 1.0 mm apart and screen is placed at 1.0 m away from slits. The distance from the centre of the screen where intensity becomes half of the maximum intensity for the first time is ______ × 10⁻⁶ m.",125,"Half the maximum intensity first occurs at φ = {π;2}, i.e. a path difference of {λ;4} = 125 nm.\ny = {Δ D;d} = {1.25 × 10⁻⁷ × 1;10⁻³} = 1.25 × 10⁻⁴ m = 125 × 10⁻⁶ m."),
N("JEE Main 2024 · 29 Jan · Shift 1","yd","Reflection and interference","In a double slit experiment, when light of wavelength 400 nm is used, dark fringe is observed at P (figure: a source on the axis at distance D to the left of the slit plane; slit S₂ lies on the axis and S₁ is a distance d above it; the point P is on the axis at distance D to the right of the slits). If D = 0.2 m, the minimum distance between the slits S₁ and S₂ is ______ mm.",0.2,"Path via S₂: D + D = 2D. Path via S₁: 2√(D² + d²) ≈ 2D + {d²;D}.\nPath difference {d²;D} = {λ;2} for the first dark fringe.\nd² = {λD;2} = {4 × 10⁻⁷ × 0.2;2} = 4 × 10⁻⁸ m², so d = 2 × 10⁻⁴ m = 0.2 mm."),
N("JEE Main 2024 · 31 Jan · Shift 1","yd","Superposition and intensity","Two waves of intensity ratio 1 : 9 cross each other at a point. The resultant intensities at that point, when (a) waves are incoherent is I₁ (b) waves are coherent is I₂ and differ in phase by 60°. If {I₁;I₂} = {10;x}, then x = ______.",13,"Take intensities I and 9I. Incoherent: I₁ = 10I.\nCoherent with φ = 60°: I₂ = I + 9I + 2 × 3I × cos 60° = 13I.\n{I₁;I₂} = {10;13}, so x = 13."),
N("JEE Advanced 2024","yd","Oscillating slit separation","In a Young's double slit experiment, each of the two slits A and B are oscillating about their fixed centre and with a mean separation of 0.8 mm. The distance between the slits at time t is given by d = (0.8 + 0.04 sin ωt) mm, where ω = 0.08 rad s⁻¹. The distance of the screen from the slits is 1 m and the wavelength of the light used to illuminate the slits is 6000 Å. The interference pattern on the screen changes with time, while the central bright fringe (zeroth fringe) remains fixed at point O.\nThe 8ᵗʰ bright fringe above the point O oscillates with time between two extreme positions. The separation between these two extreme positions, in micrometer (μm), is ______.",601.5,"The 8th bright fringe is at y = {8λD;d}, with 8λD = 4.8 × 10⁻⁶ m².\nThe slit separation varies between 0.76 mm and 0.84 mm: y_max = 6.3158 mm and y_min = 5.7143 mm.\nThe separation is 0.6015 mm = 601.5 μm."),
N("JEE Main 2023 · 6 Apr · Shift 1","yd","Two wavelengths","A beam of light consisting of two wavelengths 7000 Å and 5500 Å is used to obtain interference pattern in Young's double slit experiment. The distance between the slits is 2.5 mm and the distance between the plane of the slits and the screen is 150 cm. The least distance from the central fringe, where the bright fringes due to both the wavelengths coincide, is n × 10⁻⁵ m. The value of n is ______.",462,"Coincidence: n₁ × 7000 = n₂ × 5500, so {n₁;n₂} = {11;14}. The first coincidence is the 11th bright fringe of 7000 Å.\ny = {11 × 7 × 10⁻⁷ × 1.5;2.5 × 10⁻³} = 4.62 × 10⁻³ m = 462 × 10⁻⁵ m."),
N("JEE Main 2023 · 1 Feb · Shift 2","yd","Shift of fringes","As shown in the figure, in Young's double slit experiment, a thin plate of thickness t = 10 μm and refractive index μ = 1.2 is inserted in front of slit S₁. The experiment is conducted in air (μ = 1) and uses a monochromatic light of wavelength λ = 500 nm. Due to the insertion of the plate, central maxima is shifted by a distance xβ₀. β₀ is the fringe-width before the insertion of the plate. The value of x is ______.",4,"The plate adds a path (μ − 1)t = 0.2 × 10 μm = 2 μm = 4λ.\nThe pattern shifts by {4λ;λ} = 4 fringe widths, so x = 4."),
N("JEE Main 2023 · 25 Jan · Shift 1","huyg","Phase difference and wavelength","The distance between two consecutive points with phase difference of 60° in a wave of frequency 500 Hz is 6.0 m. The velocity with which wave is travelling is ______ km/s.",18,"60° is {1;6} of a cycle, so {λ;6} = 6.0 m and λ = 36 m.\nv = fλ = 500 × 36 = 18 000 m/s = 18 km/s."),
N("JEE Main 2023 · 30 Jan · Shift 1","yd","Shift of fringes","In Young's double slit experiment, two slits S₁ and S₂ are 'd' distance apart and the separation from slits to screen is D (as shown in figure). Now if two transparent slabs of equal thickness 0.1 mm but refractive index 1.51 and 1.55 are introduced in the path of beam (λ = 4000 Å) from S₁ and S₂ respectively. The central bright fringe spot will shift by ______ number of fringes.",10,"The path difference introduced is (1.55 − 1.51) × 0.1 mm = 4 × 10⁻⁶ m.\nNumber of fringes = {4 × 10⁻⁶;4 × 10⁻⁷} = 10."),
N("JEE Main 2023 · 30 Jan · Shift 2","yd","Superposition and intensity","In a Young's double slit experiment, the intensities at two points, for the path differences {λ;4} and {λ;3} (λ being the wavelength of light used) are I₁ and I₂ respectively. If I₀ denotes the intensity produced by each one of the individual slits, then {I₁ + I₂;I₀} = ______.",3,"I = 4I₀cos²({φ;2}). For {λ;4}: φ = {π;2}, I₁ = 4I₀ × {1;2} = 2I₀.\nFor {λ;3}: φ = {2π;3}, I₂ = 4I₀ × {1;4} = I₀.\n{I₁ + I₂;I₀} = 3."),
N("JEE Main 2023 · 31 Jan · Shift 2","yd","Two wavelengths","Two light waves of wavelengths 800 and 600 nm are used in Young's double slit experiment to obtain interference fringes on a screen placed 7 m away from plane of slits. If the two slits are separated by 0.35 mm, then shortest distance from the central bright maximum to the point where the bright fringes of the two wavelength coincide will be ______ mm.",48,"Coincidence: n₁ × 800 = n₂ × 600, so {n₁;n₂} = {3;4}.\ny = {3 × 800 × 10⁻⁹ × 7;0.35 × 10⁻³} = 4.8 × 10⁻² m = 48 mm."),
N("JEE Main 2022 · 27 Jul · Shift 1","yd","Superposition and intensity","Two beams of light having intensities I and 4I interfere to produce a fringe pattern on a screen. The phase difference between the two beams are {π;2} and {π;3} at points A and B respectively. The difference between the resultant intensities at the two points is xI. The value of x will be ______.",2,"I_res = 5I + 4I cos φ. At A: 5I. At B: 5I + 4I × 0.5 = 7I.\nThe difference is 2I, so x = 2."),
N("JEE Main 2022 · 28 Jul · Shift 1","yd","Fringe width","In a Young's double slit experiment, a laser light of 560 nm produces an interference pattern with consecutive bright fringes' separation of 7.2 mm. Now another light is used to produce an interference pattern with consecutive bright fringes' separation of 8.1 mm. The wavelength of second light is ______ nm.",630,"β ∝ λ: λ₂ = 560 × {8.1;7.2} = 630 nm."),
N("JEE Main 2022 · 29 Jul · Shift 1","yd","Superposition and intensity","Two light beams of intensities 4I and 9I interfere on a screen. The phase difference between these beams on the screen at point A is zero and at point B is π. The difference of resultant intensities, at the point A and B, will be ______ I.",24,"At A: (2 + 3)²I = 25I. At B: (3 − 2)²I = I.\nThe difference is 24I."),
N("JEE Main 2022 · 27 Jun · Shift 1","yd","Young's double slit: fringes","In Young's double slit experiment the two slits are 0.6 mm distance apart. Interference pattern is observed on a screen at a distance 80 cm from the slits. The first dark fringe is observed on the screen directly opposite to one of the slits. The wavelength of light will be ______ nm.",450,"Opposite one slit the path difference is √(D² + d²) − D ≈ {d²;2D}. For the first dark fringe this equals {λ;2}.\nλ = {d²;D} = {(6 × 10⁻⁴)²;0.8} = 4.5 × 10⁻⁷ m = 450 nm."),
N("JEE Main 2022 · 28 Jun · Shift 2","yd","Fringe width","In a Young's double slit experiment, an angular width of the fringe is 0.35° on a screen placed 2 m away for particular wavelength of 450 nm. The angular width of the fringe, when whole system is immersed in a medium of refractive index {7;5}, is {1;α}. The value of α is ______.",4,"The angular fringe width {λ;d} falls by the factor n: {0.35°;1.4} = 0.25° = {1;4}°.\nSo α = 4."),
N("JEE Main 2022 · 29 Jun · Shift 2","yd","Fringe width","In a double slit experiment with monochromatic light, fringes are obtained on a screen placed at some distance from the plane of slits. If the screen is moved by 5 × 10⁻² m towards the slits, the change in fringe width is 3 × 10⁻³ cm. If the distance between the slits is 1 mm, then the wavelength of the light will be ______ nm.",600,"Δβ = {λ ΔD;d}: λ = {Δβ d;ΔD} = {3 × 10⁻⁵ × 10⁻³;5 × 10⁻²} = 6 × 10⁻⁷ m = 600 nm."),
N("JEE Main 2021 · 26 Aug · Shift 1","yd","Fringe width","White light is passed through a double slit and interference is observed on a screen 1.5 m away. The separation between the slits is 0.3 mm. The first violet and red fringes are formed 2.0 mm and 3.5 mm away from the central white fringes. The difference in wavelengths of red and violet light is ______ nm.",300,"y = {λD;d}, so Δλ = {Δy d;D} = {1.5 × 10⁻³ × 0.3 × 10⁻³;1.5} = 3 × 10⁻⁷ m = 300 nm."),
N("JEE Main 2021 · 31 Aug · Shift 2","yd","Fringe width","In a Young's double slit experiment, the slits are separated by 0.3 mm and the screen is 1.5 m away from the plane of slits. Distance between fourth bright fringes on both sides of central bright fringe is 2.4 cm. The frequency of light used is ______ × 10¹⁴ Hz.",5,"The 4th bright fringes on both sides are 8β apart: 8β = 2.4 cm, so β = 3 mm.\nλ = {βd;D} = {3 × 10⁻³ × 3 × 10⁻⁴;1.5} = 6 × 10⁻⁷ m, and f = {c;λ} = 5 × 10¹⁴ Hz."),
N("JEE Main 2021 · 1 Sep · Shift 2","yd","Superposition and intensity","The width of one of the two slits in a Young's double slit experiment is three times the other slit. If the amplitude of the light coming from a slit is proportional to the slit-width, the ratio of minimum to maximum intensity in the interference pattern is x : 4 where x is ______.",1,"The amplitudes are in the ratio 3 : 1, so {I_min;I_max} = ({3 − 1;3 + 1})² = {1;4}.\nSo x = 1."),
N("JEE Main 2021 · 27 Aug · Shift 2","huyg","Optical path","The difference in the number of waves when yellow light propagates through air and vacuum columns of the same thickness is one. The thickness of the air column is ______ mm. [Refractive index of air = 1.0003, wavelength of yellow light in vacuum = 6000 Å]",2,"Number of waves in the air column: {n t;λ₀}; in vacuum: {t;λ₀}.\nThe difference is {(n − 1)t;λ₀} = 1, so t = {6 × 10⁻⁷;3 × 10⁻⁴} = 2 × 10⁻³ m = 2 mm."),
N("JEE Main 2021 · 16 Mar · Shift 1","yd","Fringe width","A fringe width of 6 mm was produced for two slits separated by 1 mm apart. The screen is placed 10 m away. The wavelength of light used is 'x' nm. The value of 'x' to the nearest integer is ______.",600,"λ = {βd;D} = {6 × 10⁻³ × 10⁻³;10} = 6 × 10⁻⁷ m = 600 nm."),
N("JEE Main 2020 · 9 Jan · Shift 2","yd","Fringe width","In a Young's double slit experiment 15 fringes are observed on a small portion of the screen when light of wavelength 500 nm is used. Ten fringes are observed on the same section of the screen when another light source of wavelength λ is used. Then the value of λ is (in nm) ______.",750,"The number of fringes in a fixed portion is inversely proportional to λ: λ = 500 × {15;10} = 750 nm."),
N("JEE Main 2019 · 6 Sep · Shift 2","yd","Superposition and intensity","A Young's double-slit experiment is performed using monochromatic light of wavelength λ. The intensity of light at a point on the screen, where the path difference is λ, is K units. The intensity of light at a point where the path difference is {λ;6} is given by {nK;12}, where n is an integer. The value of n is ______.",9,"A path difference of λ is a maximum: K = 4I₀.\nFor {λ;6}: φ = {π;3}, I = K cos²({π;6}) = {3K;4} = {9K;12}, so n = 9."),
N("JEE Advanced 2015","yd","Slits in air and water","A Young's double slit interference arrangement with slits S₁ and S₂ is immersed in water (refractive index = {4;3}) as shown in the figure (the slit S₁ is a distance d above the water surface, in air, and the slit S₂ a distance d below it, in water; x is the distance along the surface from the plane of the slits). The positions of maxima on the surface of water are given by x² = p²m²λ² − d², where λ is the wavelength of light in air (refractive index = 1), 2d is the separation between the slits and m is an integer. The value of p is ______.",3,"For a point on the surface at distance x, each slit is √(x² + d²) away. From S₁ the light travels in air (optical path √(x² + d²)); from S₂ it travels in water (optical path {4;3}√(x² + d²)).\nPath difference = {1;3}√(x² + d²) = mλ, so x² + d² = 9m²λ² and x² = 9m²λ² − d².\nHence p = 3."),
A("IIT-JEE 2009","yd","Sheet in front of a slit","Column I shows four situations of standard Young's double slit arrangement with the screen placed far away from the slits S₁ and S₂ (S₂ is above S₁). In each of these cases S₁P₀ = S₂P₀, S₁P₁ − S₂P₁ = {λ;4} and S₁P₂ − S₂P₂ = {λ;3}, where λ is the wavelength of the light used and P₀, P₁, P₂ are points on the screen, in this order going up from the central point P₀. In the cases b, c and d, a transparent sheet of refractive index μ and thickness t is pasted on slit S₂. The thicknesses of the sheets are different in different cases. The phase difference between the light waves reaching a point P on the screen from the two slits is denoted by δ(P) and the intensity by I(P). Match each situation given in Column I with the statement(s) in Column II valid for that situation.\nColumn I: (a) no sheet; (b) (μ − 1)t = {λ;4}; (c) (μ − 1)t = {λ;2}; (d) (μ − 1)t = {3λ;4}.\nColumn II: (p) δ(P₀) = 0; (q) δ(P₁) = 0; (r) I(P₁) = 0; (s) I(P₀) > I(P₁); (t) I(P₂) > I(P₁).","(a) → p, s   (b) → q   (c) → t   (d) → r, s, t","The sheet on S₂ adds (μ − 1)t to the optical path of that slit, lowering the path difference S₁P − S₂P by (μ − 1)t. Use I = 4I₀cos²({φ;2}).\n(a) Differences 0, {λ;4}, {λ;3}: I = 4I₀, 2I₀, I₀. So δ(P₀) = 0 (p) and I(P₀) > I(P₁) (s).\n(b) Differences −{λ;4}, 0, {λ;12}: I(P₁) = 4I₀ is the largest, with δ(P₁) = 0 only (q).\n(c) Differences −{λ;2}, −{λ;4}, −{λ;6}: I = 0, 2I₀, 3I₀. Only I(P₂) > I(P₁) (t).\n(d) Differences −{3λ;4}, −{λ;2}, −{5λ;12}: I = 2I₀, 0, 0.27I₀. I(P₁) = 0 (r), I(P₀) > I(P₁) (s), I(P₂) > I(P₁) (t)."),
M("JEE Main 2025 · 22 Jan · Shift 1","yd","Fringe width in a medium","Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\nAssertion (A): If Young's double slit experiment is performed in an optically denser medium than air, then the consecutive fringes come closer.\nReason (R): The speed of light reduces in an optically denser medium than air while its frequency does not change.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.",["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is true but (R) is false","(A) is false but (R) is true","Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)"],0,"In a medium the wavelength is λ = {λ₀;n} (the speed falls and the frequency stays the same), so the fringe width {λD;d} shrinks and the fringes come closer.\nThe reason explains the assertion."),
M("JEE Main 2025 · 22 Jan · Shift 2","yd","Fringe width","Given below are two statements. One is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\nAssertion (A): In Young's double slit experiment, the fringes produced by red light are closer as compared to those produced by blue light.\nReason (R): The fringe width is directly proportional to the wavelength of light.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.",["(A) is true but (R) is false.","Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).","(A) is false but (R) is true.","Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)."],2,"β = {λD;d} is proportional to λ, so (R) is true.\nRed has a longer wavelength than blue, so its fringes are wider, not closer: (A) is false."),
M("AIEEE 2011","yd","Newton's rings","A thin air film is formed by putting the convex surface of a plane-convex lens over a plane glass plate. With monochromatic light, this film gives an interference pattern due to light reflected from the top (convex) surface and the bottom (glass plate) surface of the film.\nStatement-1: When light reflects from the air-glass plate interface, the reflected wave suffers a phase change of π.\nStatement-2: The centre of the interference pattern is dark.",["Statement-1 is true, Statement-2 is false.","Statement-1 is true, Statement-2 is true, Statement-2 is the correct explanation of Statement-1.","Statement-1 is true, Statement-2 is true, Statement-2 is not the correct explanation of Statement-1.","Statement-1 is false, Statement-2 is true."],2,"Reflection at a rarer-to-denser boundary (air to glass) gives a phase change of π: Statement 1 is true.\nAt the centre the film thickness is zero; the two reflected waves differ only by this π, so the centre is dark: Statement 2 is true.\nStatement 2 follows from Statement 1, not the other way round, so Statement 2 does not explain Statement 1."),
A("IIT-JEE 2004","yd","Two wavelengths","In Young's double slit experiment, a light containing two wavelengths 500 nm and 700 nm are used. Find the minimum distance where maxima of two wavelengths coincide. Given {D;d} = 10³, where D is the distance between the slits and the screen and d is the distance between the slits.","3.5 mm","Coincidence: n₁ × 500 = n₂ × 700, so {n₁;n₂} = {7;5}. The first coincidence is the 7th maximum of 500 nm.\ny = {n₁λ₁D;d} = 7 × 500 × 10⁻⁹ × 10³ = 3.5 × 10⁻³ m."),
A("IIT-JEE 2003","yd","Thin film interference","Shown in the figure is a prism of an angle 30° and refractive index μ_p = √3. Face AC of the prism is covered with a thin film of refractive index μ_f = 2.2. A monochromatic light of wavelength λ = 550 nm falls on the face AB at an angle of incidence of 60°. Calculate (a) angle of emergence, (b) minimum value of thickness t so that intensity of emergent ray is maximum.","(a) 0° (the ray leaves normally)   (b) 125 nm","(a) At AB: sin r₁ = {sin 60°;√3} = {1;2}, so r₁ = 30°. Then r₂ = A − r₁ = 0°: the ray meets face AC normally and leaves along the normal.\n(b) The reflection at prism-film (1.73 to 2.2) has a phase change of π, but film-air (2.2 to 1) has none. For the transmitted intensity to be a maximum the reflected waves must cancel: 2μ_f t = λ (the π shift and the path difference of 2μ_f t give destructive reflection).\nt = {λ;2μ_f} = {550;4.4} = 125 nm."),
A("IIT-JEE 2002","yd","Lloyd's mirror","A point source S emitting light of wavelength 600 nm is placed at a very small height h above a flat reflecting surface AB (see figure; the screen is parallel to the surface, a very large distance D above it, and P is the point of the screen directly above S). The intensity of the reflected light is 36% of the incident intensity. Interference fringes are observed on a screen placed parallel to the reflecting surface at a very large distance D from it.\n(a) What is the shape of the interference fringes on the screen?\n(b) Calculate the ratio of the minimum to the maximum intensities in the interference fringes formed near the point P.\n(c) If the intensity at point P corresponds to a maximum, calculate the minimum distance through which the reflecting surface AB should be shifted so that the intensity at P again becomes maximum.","(a) concentric circles centred at P   (b) {1;16}   (c) 300 nm (λ/2)","(a) The source and its mirror image lie on one line perpendicular to the screen. Every point at the same distance from P has the same path difference, so the fringes are concentric circles about P.\n(b) The amplitude of the reflected beam is √0.36 = 0.6 of the direct one: {I_min;I_max} = ({1 − 0.6;1 + 0.6})² = {1;16}.\n(c) The path difference at P is about 2h. Moving the surface by x changes it by 2x. The next maximum needs 2x = λ, so x = {λ;2} = 300 nm."),
A("IIT-JEE 2001","yd","Slits sealed with glass plates","A vessel ABCD of 10 cm width has two small slits S₁ and S₂ sealed with identical glass plates of equal thickness (in the wall AB). The distance between the slits is 0.8 mm. POQ is the line perpendicular to the plane AB and passing through O, the middle point of S₁ and S₂ (P is outside the vessel, Q is on the opposite wall CD). A monochromatic source is kept at S, 40 cm below P and 2 m from the vessel, to illuminate the slits. Calculate the position of the central bright fringe on the other wall CD with respect to the line OQ. Now, a liquid is poured into the vessel and filled up to OQ. The central bright fringe is found to be at Q. Calculate the refractive index of the liquid.","2 cm above Q (on the side opposite to S); refractive index 1.0016","Let the slits be separated by d = 0.8 mm. S is below the axis by 40 cm at 2 m, so the paths from S to the two slits differ by d × {0.4;2} = 0.16 mm (the lower slit is nearer).\nThe central fringe is where this is balanced by the path from the slits to the wall: {d y;L} = 0.16 mm with L = 10 cm, giving y = {0.16 × 100;0.8} = 20 mm = 2 cm above Q.\nWith liquid up to OQ the light from the lower slit passes through 10 cm of liquid, adding (μ − 1)L to its optical path. For the central fringe at Q: (μ − 1) × 0.1 m = 0.16 mm, so μ = 1.0016."),
A("IIT-JEE 2000","yd","Thin film interference","A glass plate of refractive index 1.5 is coated with a thin layer of thickness t and refractive index 1.8. Light of wavelength λ travelling in air is incident normally on the layer. It is partly reflected at the upper and the lower surfaces of the layer and the two reflected rays interfere. Write the condition for their constructive interference. If λ = 648 nm, obtain the least value of t for which the rays interfere constructively.","2μt = (2m + 1){λ;2} (m = 0, 1, 2, …); least t = 90 nm","Reflection at the top surface (air to the denser film) gives a phase change of π. At the lower surface the light goes from film (1.8) to glass (1.5), a rarer medium, so there is no change.\nThe reflected rays therefore differ by an extra {λ;2}: constructive interference needs 2μt = (m + ½)λ = (2m + 1){λ;2}.\nLeast t (m = 0): t = {λ;4μ} = {648;7.2} = 90 nm."),
A("IIT-JEE 1999","yd","Slit covered by a glass sheet","The Young's double slit experiment is done in a medium of refractive index 4/3. A light of 600 nm wavelength is falling on the slits having 0.45 mm separation. The lower slit S₂ is covered by a thin glass sheet of thickness 10.4 μm and refractive index 1.5. The interference pattern is observed on a screen placed 1.5 m from the slits as shown in figure.\n(a) Find the location of the central maximum (bright fringe with zero path difference) on the y-axis.\n(b) Find the light intensity at point O relative to the maximum fringe intensity.\n(c) Now, if 600 nm light is replaced by white light of range 400 to 700 nm, find the wavelengths of the light that form maxima exactly at point O.\n[All wavelengths in this problem are for the given medium of refractive index 4/3. Ignore dispersion]","(a) about 4.33 mm below O (towards the covered slit)   (b) {3;4} of the maximum   (c) 650 nm and 433 nm","In the medium the sheet's relative index is {1.5;4/3} = {9;8}, so it adds a path (9/8 − 1) × 10.4 μm = 1.3 μm = 2.167 wavelengths (λ = 600 nm in the medium).\n(a) β = {λD;d} = {600 × 10⁻⁹ × 1.5;0.45 × 10⁻³} = 2 mm. The pattern shifts by 2.167β = 4.33 mm towards S₂ (downwards).\n(b) At O the phase difference is 2π × 2.167, equivalent to 60°: I = I_max cos²(30°) = {3;4}I_max.\n(c) Maxima at O need 1.3 μm = nλ: λ = {1300 nm;n}. In 400–700 nm this gives n = 2: 650 nm and n = 3: 433 nm."),
A("IIT-JEE 1998","yd","Oblique incidence on the slits","A coherent parallel beam of microwaves of wavelength λ = 0.5 mm falls on a Young's double slit apparatus. The separation between the slits is 1.0 mm. The intensity of microwaves is measured on a screen placed parallel to the plane of the slits at a distance of 1.0 m from it, as shown in figure (the slits are along the y-axis, the screen is at x = 1.0 m).\n(a) If the incident beam falls normally on the double slit apparatus, find the y-coordinates of all the interference minima on the screen.\n(b) If the incident beam makes an angle of 30° with the x-axis (as in the dotted arrow shown in figure, travelling upwards), find the y-coordinates of the first minima on either side of the central maximum.","(a) y = ±0.26 m and ±1.13 m   (b) y = 0.26 m and 1.13 m (above O; the central maximum is at 0.58 m)","(a) Minima: d sin θ = (m + ½)λ, so sin θ = ±0.25 or ±0.75 (larger values would exceed 1). With y = D tan θ: tan θ = 0.258 and 1.134, giving y = ±0.26 m and ±1.13 m.\n(b) The oblique beam adds a path d sin 30° between the slits, so the central maximum is at θ = 30° (y = 0.577 m). Minima: d(sin θ − sin 30°) = ±{λ;2}, so sin θ = 0.5 ± 0.25 = 0.75 or 0.25.\nThe first minima on either side are at y = 1.13 m and y = 0.26 m."),
A("IIT-JEE 1997","yd","Slits covered by glass plates","In Young's experiment, the upper slit is covered by a thin glass plate of refractive index 1.4 while the lower slit is covered by another glass plate, having the same thickness as the first one but having refractive index 1.7. Interference pattern is observed using light of wavelength 5400 Å. It is found that the point P on the screen where the central maximum (n = 0) fell before the glass plates were inserted now has {3;4}ᵗʰ of the original intensity. It is further observed that what used to be the fifth maximum earlier, lies below the point P while the sixth minimum lies above P. Calculate the thickness of the glass plate. (Absorption of light by glass plate may be neglected.)","9.3 μm","The lower plate adds the extra path (1.7 − 1.4)t = 0.3t, so the pattern shifts down by N = {0.3t;λ} fringes.\nAt P: I = I_max cos²(πN) = {3;4}I_max gives N = k ± {1;6}.\nThe 5th maximum (at 5 − N from P) lies below P, so N > 5; the 6th minimum (at 5.5 − N) lies above P, so N < 5.5. Hence N = 5 + {1;6} = {31;6}.\nt = {31 × 0.54 μm;6 × 0.3} = 9.3 μm."),
A("IIT-JEE 1997","yd","Shift of the central fringe","In Young's experiment, the source is red light of wavelength 7 × 10⁻⁷ m. When a thin glass plate of refractive index 1.5 at this wavelength is put in the path of one of the interfering beams, the central bright fringe shifts by 10⁻³ m to the position previously occupied by the 5ᵗʰ bright fringe. Find the thickness of the plate. When the source is now changed to green light of wavelength 5 × 10⁻⁷ m, the central fringe shifts to a position initially occupied by the 6ᵗʰ bright fringe due to red light. Find the refractive index of glass for green light. Also estimate the change in fringe width due to the change in wavelength.","t = 7 μm; μ (green) = 1.6; the fringe width falls from 0.2 mm to 0.14 mm (a decrease of about 0.057 mm)","Red: the shift equals 5 fringes, so (μ − 1)t = 5λ_r = 3.5 × 10⁻⁶ m and t = {3.5 × 10⁻⁶;0.5} = 7 μm. Also β_r = {10⁻³;5} = 0.2 mm.\nGreen: β_g = β_r × {5;7} = 0.143 mm. The shift is 6β_r = 1.2 mm = 8.4β_g, so (μ_g − 1)t = 8.4λ_g = 4.2 × 10⁻⁶ m and μ_g = 1 + {4.2;7} = 1.6.\nThe fringe width changes by 0.143 − 0.2 = −0.057 mm."),
A("IIT-JEE 1996","yd","Double slit in a liquid","A double-slit apparatus is immersed in a liquid of refractive index 1.33. It has slit separation of 1 mm, and distance between the plane of slits and screen is 1.33 m. The slits are illuminated by a parallel beam of light whose wavelength in air is 6300 Å.\n(i) Calculate the fringe-width.\n(ii) One of the slits of the apparatus is covered by a thin glass sheet of refractive index 1.53. Find the smallest thickness of the sheet to bring the adjacent minimum on the axis.","(i) 0.63 mm   (ii) 1.575 μm","(i) In the liquid λ = {6300;1.33} = 4737 Å. β = {λD;d} = {4.737 × 10⁻⁷ × 1.33;10⁻³} = 6.3 × 10⁻⁴ m = 0.63 mm.\n(ii) A minimum on the axis needs an extra optical path of {λ_air;2}: (1.53 − 1.33)t = {6.3 × 10⁻⁷;2}, so t = {3.15 × 10⁻⁷;0.2} = 1.575 × 10⁻⁶ m."),
A("IIT-JEE 1993","yd","Split lens (Billet)","In the figure, S is a monochromatic point source emitting light of wavelength λ = 500 nm. A thin lens of circular shape and focal length 0.10 m is cut into two identical halves L₁ and L₂ by a plane passing through a diameter. The two halves are placed symmetrically about the central axis SO with a gap of 0.5 mm. The distance along the axis from S to L₁ and L₂ is 0.15 m while that from L₁ and L₂ to O is 1.30 m. The screen at O is normal to SO.\n(i) If the third intensity maximum occurs at the point A on the screen, find the distance OA.\n(ii) If the gap between L₁ and L₂ is reduced from its original value of 0.5 mm, will the distance OA increase, decrease, or remain the same?","(i) OA = 1.0 mm   (ii) it increases","Each half-lens forms an image of S at v = {0.15 × 0.10;0.15 − 0.10} = 0.30 m (magnification 2). The optical centres of the halves are 0.25 mm off the axis, and the ray through each centre is undeviated, so the two images are 3 × 0.25 mm = 0.75 mm on either side of the axis: coherent sources 1.5 mm apart.\nThe screen is 1.30 − 0.30 = 1.0 m from them: β = {λD;d} = {5 × 10⁻⁷ × 1;1.5 × 10⁻³} = 0.33 mm. The third maximum is at 3β = 1.0 mm.\nReducing the gap brings the two images closer (smaller d), so β and OA increase."),
A("IIT-JEE 1990","yd","Superposition and intensity","A narrow monochromatic beam of light of intensity I is incident on a glass plate as shown in figure. Another identical glass plate is kept close to the first one and parallel to it. Each glass plate reflects 25 percent of the light incident on it and transmits the remaining. Find the ratio of the minimum and the maximum intensities in the interference pattern formed by the two beams obtained after one reflection at each plate.","1 : 49","Beam 1 (reflected from the first plate): 0.25I.\nBeam 2 goes through the first plate (0.75), is reflected by the second (0.25), and comes back through the first (0.75): 0.75 × 0.25 × 0.75 = 0.1406I.\nAmplitudes are in the ratio √0.25 : √0.1406 = 0.5 : 0.375 = 4 : 3.\n{I_min;I_max} = ({4 − 3;4 + 3})² = {1;49}."),
A("IIT-JEE 1989","yd","Superposition and intensity","In a modified Young's double slit experiment, a monochromatic uniform and parallel beam of light of wavelength 6000 Å and intensity ({10;π}) W m⁻² is incident normally on two circular apertures A and B of radii 0.001 m and 0.002 m respectively. A perfectly transparent film of thickness 2000 Å and refractive index 1.5 for the wavelength of 6000 Å is placed in front of aperture A. Calculate the power (in watt) received at the focal spot F of the lens. The lens is symmetrically placed with respect to the apertures. Assume that 10% of the power received by each aperture goes in the original direction and is brought to the focal spot.","7 × 10⁻⁶ W","Power through A: {10;π} × π(10⁻³)² = 10⁻⁵ W, of which 10% reaches F: P_A = 10⁻⁶ W. Through B: {10;π} × π(2 × 10⁻³)² = 4 × 10⁻⁵ W, so P_B = 4 × 10⁻⁶ W.\nThe film adds a path (1.5 − 1) × 2000 Å = 1000 Å = {λ;6}, a phase difference of 60°.\nP = P_A + P_B + 2√(P_AP_B) cos 60° = 1 + 4 + 2 = 7 × 10⁻⁶ W."),
A("IIT-JEE 1985","yd","Two wavelengths","A beam of light consisting of two wavelengths, 6500 Å and 5200 Å, is used to obtain interference fringes in Young's double slit experiment:\n(i) Find the distance of the third bright fringe on the screen from the central maximum for wavelength 6500 Å.\n(ii) What is the least distance from the central maximum where the bright fringes due to both the wavelengths coincide?\nThe distance between the slits is 2 mm and the distance between the plane of the slits and the screen is 120 cm.","(i) 1.17 mm   (ii) 1.56 mm","(i) y = {3λD;d} = {3 × 6.5 × 10⁻⁷ × 1.2;2 × 10⁻³} = 1.17 × 10⁻³ m.\n(ii) n₁ × 6500 = n₂ × 5200 gives n₁ : n₂ = 4 : 5. The first coincidence is the 4th fringe of 6500 Å: y = 4 × {6.5 × 10⁻⁷ × 1.2;2 × 10⁻³} = 1.56 mm."),
A("IIT-JEE 1983","yd","Shift of fringes","In Young's double slit experiment using monochromatic light, the fringe pattern shifts by a certain distance on the screen, when a mica sheet of refractive index 1.6 and thickness 1.964 micron is introduced in the path of one of the interfering waves. The mica sheet is then removed and the distance between the slits and the screen is doubled. It is found that the distance between successive maxima (or minima) now is the same as the observed fringe shift upon the introduction of the mica sheet. Calculate the wavelength of the monochromatic light used in the experiment.","5892 Å","With the sheet, the shift is {(μ − 1)tD;d}.\nWithout it but with the screen at 2D, the fringe width is {2λD;d}.\nEqual: (μ − 1)t = 2λ, so λ = {0.6 × 1.964 μm;2} = 0.5892 μm = 5892 Å."),
M("IIT-JEE 1987","yd","Coherence","In Young's double slit experiment, performed with a source of white light, only black and white fringes are observed. (True or False)",["True","False"],1,"Each colour makes its own fringe pattern with its own fringe width, so the central fringe is white and the next ones are coloured (violet nearest the centre), which fade out after a few orders. The statement is false."),
M("IIT-JEE 1984","yd","Coherence","The two slits in a Young's double slit experiment are illuminated by two different sodium lamps emitting light of the same wavelength. No interference pattern will be observed on the screen. (True or False)",["True","False"],0,"Two separate lamps are independent sources: the phase difference between them changes randomly and rapidly, so they are not coherent and no steady fringes appear. The statement is true."),
A("IIT-JEE 1989","huyg","Intensity and amplitude","A point source emits waves equally in all directions in a non-absorbing medium. Two points P and Q are at a distance of 9 metre and 25 metre respectively from the source. The ratio of amplitudes of the waves at P and Q is ______.","25 : 9","The power is spread over a sphere, so intensity ∝ {1;r²} and the amplitude ∝ {1;r}.\n{A_P;A_Q} = {25;9}."),
A("IIT-JEE 1986","yd","Coherent and incoherent sources","In Young's double-slit experiment, the two slits act as coherent sources of equal amplitude A and of wavelength λ. In another experiment with the same set-up the two slits are sources of equal amplitude A and wavelength λ, but are incoherent. The ratio of the intensity of light at the midpoint of the screen in the first case to that in the second case is ______.","2 : 1","At the midpoint the coherent waves are in phase: I = (A + A)² = 4A². Incoherent sources add intensities: A² + A² = 2A².\nThe ratio is 2 : 1."),
M("JEE Main 2024 · 1 Feb · Shift 1","diff","Single slit diffraction","A monochromatic light of wavelength 6000 Å is incident on the single slit of width 0.01 mm. If the diffraction pattern is formed at the focus of the convex lens of focal length 20 cm, the linear width of the central maximum is",["120 mm","12 mm","60 mm","24 mm"],3,"Width of the central maximum = {2λf;a} = {2 × 6 × 10⁻⁷ × 0.2;10⁻⁵} = 0.024 m = 24 mm."),
M("JEE Main 2024 · 1 Feb · Shift 2","diff","Single slit diffraction","A microwave of wavelength 2.0 cm falls normally on a slit of width 4.0 cm. The angular spread of the central maxima of the diffraction pattern obtained on a screen 1.5 m away from the slit, will be",["60°","45°","15°","30°"],0,"The first minima are at sin θ = {λ;a} = {2;4} = 0.5, so θ = 30° on each side.\nThe angular spread of the central maximum is 2θ = 60°."),
M("JEE Main 2024 · 30 Jan · Shift 1","diff","Single slit diffraction","The diffraction pattern of a light of wavelength 400 nm diffracting from a slit of width 0.2 mm is focused on the focal plane of a convex lens of focal length 100 cm. The width of 1ˢᵗ secondary maxima will be",["0.2 mm","2 cm","2 mm","0.02 mm"],2,"The secondary maxima lie between successive minima, each of width {λf;a} = {4 × 10⁻⁷ × 1;2 × 10⁻⁴} = 2 × 10⁻³ m = 2 mm."),
M("JEE Main 2021 · 25 Feb · Shift 2","diff","Single slit diffraction","Consider the diffraction pattern obtained from the sunlight incident on a pinhole of diameter 0.1 μm. If the diameter of the pinhole is slightly increased, it will affect the diffraction pattern such that",["its size decreases, and intensity increases","its size increases, and intensity decreases","its size decreases, and intensity decreases","its size increases, and intensity increases"],0,"The angular size of the central spot is about {1.22λ;a}, so a larger hole gives a smaller pattern.\nMore light passes through the larger hole and is spread over a smaller area, so the intensity increases."),
M("JEE Main 2020 · 7 Jan · Shift 1","diff","Single slit diffraction","Visible light of wavelength 6000 × 10⁻⁸ cm falls normally on a single slit and produces a diffraction pattern. It is found that the second diffraction minimum is at 60° from the central maximum. If the first minimum is produced at θ₁, then θ₁ is close to",["45°","30°","25°","20°"],2,"Second minimum: a sin 60° = 2λ. First minimum: a sin θ₁ = λ.\nsin θ₁ = {sin 60°;2} = 0.433, so θ₁ = 25.7°, close to 25°."),
M("JEE Main 2019 · 11 Jan · Shift 2","diff","Double slit with finite slit width","In a double-slit experiment, green light (5303 Å) falls on a double slit having a separation of 19.44 μm and a width of 4.05 μm. The number of bright fringes between the first and the second diffraction minima is",["10","04","05","09"],2,"The single-slit minima are at sin θ = {mλ;a}, and the double-slit bright fringes at sin θ = {nλ;d}.\nThe first diffraction minimum is therefore at fringe order {d;a} = {19.44;4.05} = 4.8 and the second at 9.6.\nThe bright fringes between them have orders 5, 6, 7, 8, 9: five of them."),
M("JEE Main 2018","diff","Double slit with finite slit width","The angular width of the central maximum in a single slit diffraction pattern is 60°. The width of the slit is 1 μm. The slit is illuminated by monochromatic plane waves. If another slit of same width is made near it, Young's fringes can be observed on a screen placed at a distance 50 cm from the slits. If the observed fringe width is 1 cm, what is slit separation distance? (i.e. distance between the centres of each slit.)",["25 μm","50 μm","75 μm","100 μm"],0,"The first minima are at ±30°: a sin 30° = λ, so λ = 0.5 μm.\nFringe width β = {λD;d}, so d = {0.5 × 10⁻⁶ × 0.5;0.01} = 2.5 × 10⁻⁵ m = 25 μm."),
M("JEE Main 2018 · Online","diff","Single slit diffraction","Light of wavelength 550 nm falls normally on a slit of width 22.0 × 10⁻⁵ cm. The angular position of the second minima from the central maximum will be (in radians)",["{π;8}","{π;12}","{π;6}","{π;4}"],2,"a = 22 × 10⁻⁵ cm = 2.2 μm. Second minimum: sin θ = {2λ;a} = {1100;2200} = 0.5.\nθ = {π;6}."),
M("JEE Main 2017 · Online","diff","Single slit diffraction","A single slit of width b is illuminated by a coherent monochromatic light of wavelength λ. If the second and fourth minima in the diffraction pattern at a distance 1 m from the slit are at 3 cm and 6 cm respectively from the central maximum, what is the width of the central maximum? (i.e. distance between first minimum on either side of the central maximum)",["6.0 cm","1.5 cm","4.5 cm","3.0 cm"],3,"The minima are at y_n = {nλD;b}. The second is at 3 cm, so the first minimum is at 1.5 cm (and the fourth at 6 cm, consistent).\nThe central maximum extends from −1.5 cm to +1.5 cm: 3.0 cm."),
M("JEE Main 2017 · Online","diff","Single slit diffraction","A single slit of width 0.1 mm is illuminated by a parallel beam of light of wavelength 6000 Å and diffraction bands are observed on a screen 0.5 m from the slit. The distance of the third dark band from the central bright band is",["3 mm","1.5 mm","9 mm","4.5 mm"],2,"y = {3λD;a} = {3 × 6 × 10⁻⁷ × 0.5;10⁻⁴} = 9 × 10⁻³ m = 9 mm."),
M("JEE Main 2016","diff","Pinhole camera","The box of a pin hole camera, of length L, has a hole of radius a. It is assumed that when the hole is illuminated by a parallel beam of light of wavelength λ the spread of the spot (obtained on the opposite wall of the camera) is the sum of its geometrical spread and the spread due to diffraction. The spot would then have its minimum size (say b_min) when",["a = {λ²;L} and b_min = ({2λ²;L})","a = √(λL) and b_min = ({2λ²;L})","a = √(λL) and b_min = √(4λL)","a = {λ²;L} and b_min = √(4λL)"],2,"Take the spread of the spot as the geometrical spread a plus the diffraction spread {λL;a}: b = a + {λL;a}.\nIt is least when {db;da} = 1 − {λL;a²} = 0, i.e. a = √(λL).\nThen b_min = 2√(λL) = √(4λL)."),
M("JEE Main 2014 · Online","diff","Single slit diffraction","In an experiment of single slit diffraction pattern, first minimum for red light coincides with first maximum of some other wavelength. If wavelength of red light is 6600 Å, then wavelength of first maximum will be",["3300 Å","4400 Å","5500 Å","6600 Å"],1,"First minimum for red: a sin θ = 6600 Å. First secondary maximum of the other: a sin θ = {3λ′;2}.\nλ′ = {2;3} × 6600 = 4400 Å."),
M("AIEEE 2012 · Online","diff","Single slit diffraction","The first diffraction minimum due to the single slit diffraction is seen at θ = 30° for a light of wavelength 5000 Å falling perpendicularly on the slit. The width of the slit is",["2.5 × 10⁻⁵ cm","1.25 × 10⁻⁵ cm","10 × 10⁻⁵ cm","5 × 10⁻⁵ cm"],2,"a sin θ = λ: a = {5000 × 10⁻¹⁰;0.5} = 10⁻⁶ m = 10 × 10⁻⁵ cm."),
M("AIEEE 2005","inst","Resolving power","Two point white dots are 1 mm apart on a black paper. They are viewed by eye of pupil diameter 3 mm. Approximately, what is the maximum distance at which these dots can be resolved by the eye? [Take wavelength of light = 500 nm]",["6 m","3 m","5 m","1 m"],2,"The limit of resolution is θ = {1.22λ;D} = {1.22 × 5 × 10⁻⁷;3 × 10⁻³} = 2.0 × 10⁻⁴ rad.\nDistance = {1 mm;2.0 × 10⁻⁴} ≈ 5 m."),
M("AIEEE 2002","inst","Resolving power","An astronomical telescope has a large aperture to",["reduce spherical aberration","have high resolution","increase span of observation","have low dispersion."],1,"Resolving power = {D;1.22λ} grows with the aperture D. A large aperture also gathers more light."),
M("AIEEE 2002","inst","Resolving power","Wavelength of light used in an optical instrument are λ₁ = 4000 Å and λ₂ = 5000 Å, then ratio of their respective resolving powers (corresponding to λ₁ and λ₂) is",["16 : 25","9 : 1","4 : 5","5 : 4"],3,"Resolving power ∝ {1;λ}: {R₁;R₂} = {λ₂;λ₁} = {5000;4000} = 5 : 4."),
M("IIT-JEE 1999","diff","Single slit diffraction","Yellow light is used in a single slit diffraction experiment with slit width of 0.6 mm. If yellow light is replaced by X-rays, then the observed pattern will reveal,",["that the central maximum is narrower","more number of fringes","less number of fringes","no diffraction pattern."],3,"X-rays have a wavelength of about 10⁻¹⁰ m, much smaller than the slit width. Diffraction is noticeable only when the slit width is comparable to the wavelength, so no diffraction pattern is seen."),
M("IIT-JEE 1995","diff","Single slit diffraction","Consider Fraunhofer diffraction pattern obtained with a single slit illuminated at normal incidence. At the angular position of the first diffraction minimum, the phase difference (in radian) between the wavelets from the opposite edges of the slit is",["{π;4}","{π;2}","2π","π."],2,"At the first minimum the path difference between the edge rays is a sin θ = λ.\nThe phase difference is {2π;λ} × λ = 2π."),
M("IIT-JEE 1994","diff","Single slit diffraction","A beam of light of wavelength 600 nm, from a distant source, falls on a single slit 1 mm wide and a resulting diffraction pattern is observed on a screen 2 m away. The distance between the first dark fringes on either side of central bright fringe is",["1.2 cm","1.2 mm","2.4 cm","2.4 mm."],3,"Width between the first minima = {2λD;a} = {2 × 6 × 10⁻⁷ × 2;10⁻³} = 2.4 × 10⁻³ m = 2.4 mm."),
M("IIT-JEE 1998","diff","Single slit diffraction","A parallel monochromatic beam of light is incident normally on a narrow slit. A diffraction pattern is formed on a screen placed perpendicular to the direction of the incident beam. At the first minimum of the diffraction pattern, the phase difference between the rays coming from the two edges of the slit is",["0","{π;2}","π","2π"],3,"At the first minimum the path difference between the edge rays is one wavelength, so the phase difference is 2π."),
N("JEE Main 2025 · 2 Apr · Shift 1","diff","Single slit diffraction","If the measured angular separation between the second minimum to the left of the central maximum and the third minimum to the right of the central maximum is 30° in a single slit diffraction pattern recorded using 628 nm light, then the width of the slit is ______ μm.",6,"For small angles the minima are at θ = {mλ;a}. The second minimum on the left and the third on the right are (2 + 3) = 5 orders apart: {5λ;a} = 30° = {π;6} rad.\na = {5 × 0.628 μm;0.5236} = 6 μm."),
N("JEE Main 2025 · 4 Apr · Shift 2","diff","Double slit with finite slit width","In a Young's double slit experiment, two slits are located 1.5 mm apart. The distance of screen from slits is 2 m and the wavelength of the source is 400 nm. If the 20 maxima of the double slit pattern are contained within the central maximum of the single slit diffraction pattern, then the width of each slit is x × 10⁻³ cm, where x-value is ______.",15,"Double-slit fringe width β = {λD;d} = {4 × 10⁻⁷ × 2;1.5 × 10⁻³} = 5.33 × 10⁻⁴ m. Twenty fringes span 20β = 10.67 mm.\nThe central maximum of the single slit has width {2λD;a} = 10.67 mm, so a = {2 × 4 × 10⁻⁷ × 2;1.067 × 10⁻²} = 1.5 × 10⁻⁴ m = 15 × 10⁻³ cm."),
N("JEE Advanced 2025","diff","Error in slit width","A single slit diffraction experiment is performed to determine the slit width using the equation, {bd;D} = mλ, where b is the slit width, D the shortest distance between the slit and the screen, d the distance between the mᵗʰ diffraction maximum and the central maximum, and λ is the wavelength. D and d are measured with scales of least count 1 cm and 1 mm, respectively. The values of λ and m are known precisely to be 600 nm and 3, respectively. The absolute error (in μm) in the value of b estimated using the diffraction maximum that occurs for m = 3 with d = 5 mm and D = 1 m is ______.",75.6,"b = {mλD;d} = {3 × 600 × 10⁻⁹ × 1;5 × 10⁻³} = 3.6 × 10⁻⁴ m = 360 μm.\nOnly D and d carry errors: {Δb;b} = {ΔD;D} + {Δd;d} = {0.01;1} + {1;5} = 0.21.\nΔb = 0.21 × 360 = 75.6 μm."),
N("JEE Main 2024 · 5 Apr · Shift 2","diff","Single slit diffraction","In a single slit experiment, a parallel beam of green light of wavelength 550 nm passes through a slit of width 0.20 mm. The transmitted light is collected on a screen 100 cm away. The distance of first order minima from the central maximum will be x × 10⁻⁵ m. The value of x is ______.",275,"y = {λD;a} = {5.5 × 10⁻⁷ × 1;2 × 10⁻⁴} = 2.75 × 10⁻³ m = 275 × 10⁻⁵ m."),
N("JEE Main 2024 · 8 Apr · Shift 1","diff","Single slit diffraction","A parallel beam of monochromatic light of wavelength 600 nm passes through single slit of width 0.4 mm. Angular divergence corresponding to second order minima would be ______ × 10⁻³ rad.",6,"Second-order minima are at sin θ = {2λ;a} = {1.2 × 10⁻⁶;4 × 10⁻⁴} = 3 × 10⁻³ rad on each side.\nThe angular divergence between the second minima on the two sides is 2 × 3 × 10⁻³ = 6 × 10⁻³ rad."),
N("JEE Main 2024 · 27 Jan · Shift 2","diff","Single slit diffraction","A parallel beam of monochromatic light of wavelength 5000 Å is incident normally on a single narrow slit of width 0.001 mm. The light is focused by convex lens on screen, placed on its focal plane. The first minima will be formed for the angle of diffraction of ______ (degree).",30,"First minimum: sin θ = {λ;a} = {5 × 10⁻⁷;10⁻⁶} = 0.5, so θ = 30°."),
N("JEE Main 2024 · 29 Jan · Shift 2","diff","Single slit diffraction","In a single slit diffraction pattern, a light of wavelength 6000 Å is used. The distance between the first and third minima in the diffraction pattern is found to be 3 mm when the screen is placed 50 cm away from slits. The width of the slit is ______ × 10⁻⁴ m.",2,"The first and third minima are two orders apart: y₃ − y₁ = {2λD;a} = 3 × 10⁻³ m.\na = {2 × 6 × 10⁻⁷ × 0.5;3 × 10⁻³} = 2 × 10⁻⁴ m."),
N("JEE Main 2022 · 24 Jun · Shift 1","diff","Single slit diffraction","Sodium light of wavelengths 650 nm and 655 nm is used to study diffraction at a single slit of aperture 0.5 mm. The distance between the slit and the screen is 2.0 m. The separation between the positions of the first maxima of diffraction pattern obtained in the two cases is ______ × 10⁻⁵ m.",3,"The first secondary maximum is at y ≈ {3λD;2a}. The difference for the two wavelengths is {3Δλ D;2a} = {3 × 5 × 10⁻⁹ × 2;2 × 5 × 10⁻⁴} = 3 × 10⁻⁵ m."),
N("JEE Main 2020 · 4 Sep · Shift 2","diff","Single slit diffraction","Orange light of wavelength 6000 × 10⁻¹⁰ m illuminates a single slit of width 0.6 × 10⁻⁴ m. The maximum possible number of diffraction minima produced on both sides of the central maximum is ______.",200,"Minima: a sin θ = mλ, so m ≤ {a;λ} = {6 × 10⁻⁵;6 × 10⁻⁷} = 100.\nTaking m up to 100 on each side (the last at 90°), the total is 200 minima."),
A("IIT-JEE 1997","diff","Single slit diffraction","A slit of width d is placed in front of a lens of focal length 0.5 m and is illuminated normally with light of wavelength 5.89 × 10⁻⁷ m. The first diffraction minima on either side of the central diffraction maximum are separated by 2 × 10⁻³ m. The width d of the slit is ______ m.","2.945 × 10⁻⁴ m","The first minima on both sides are {2λf;d} apart: {2 × 5.89 × 10⁻⁷ × 0.5;d} = 2 × 10⁻³.\nd = {5.89 × 10⁻⁷;2 × 10⁻³} = 2.945 × 10⁻⁴ m."),
M("JEE Main 2025 · 4 Apr · Shift 2","pol","Malus' law","Two polarisers P₁ and P₂ are placed in such a way that the intensity of the transmitted light will be zero. A third polariser P₃ is inserted in between P₁ and P₂, at particular angle between P₂ and P₃. The transmitted intensity of the light passing through all three polarisers is maximum. The angle between the polarisers P₂ and P₃ is",["{π;8}","{π;4}","{π;3}","{π;6}"],1,"P₁ and P₂ are crossed. If P₃ makes θ with P₂ it makes 90° − θ with P₁.\nI = {I₀;2} cos²(90° − θ) cos²θ = {I₀;8} sin²2θ, which is greatest for 2θ = 90°, i.e. θ = {π;4}."),
M("JEE Main 2025 · 7 Apr · Shift 1","pol","Superposition of polarised waves","Two plane polarized light waves combine at a certain point whose electric field components are\nE₁ = E₀ sin ωt\nE₂ = E₀ sin(ωt + {π;3})\nFind the amplitude of the resultant wave.",["E₀","1.7E₀","0.9E₀","3.4E₀"],1,"For two equal amplitudes with a phase difference φ the resultant amplitude is 2E₀cos({φ;2}).\nHere φ = {π;3}: 2E₀cos 30° = √3 E₀ = 1.7E₀."),
M("JEE Main 2024 · 27 Jan · Shift 2","pol","Malus' law","When a polaroid sheet is rotated between two crossed polaroids then the transmitted light intensity will be maximum for a rotation of",["60°","30°","45°","90°"],2,"With the middle sheet at angle θ from the first, I = {I₀;2}cos²θ sin²θ = {I₀;8}sin²2θ, which is greatest at θ = 45°."),
M("JEE Main 2024 · 30 Jan · Shift 2","pol","Malus' law","A beam of unpolarised light of intensity I₀ is passed through a polaroid A and then through another polaroid B which is oriented so that its principal plane makes an angle of 45° relative to that of A. The intensity of emergent light is",["{I₀;4}","{I₀;8}","{I₀;2}","I₀"],0,"After A: {I₀;2}. After B: {I₀;2}cos²45° = {I₀;4}."),
M("JEE Main 2024 · 31 Jan · Shift 2","pol","Brewster's law","When unpolarised light is incident at an angle of 60° on a transparent medium from air, the reflected ray is completely polarised. The angle of refraction in the medium is",["30°","45°","90°","60°"],0,"At Brewster's angle the reflected and refracted rays are at 90°: r = 90° − 60° = 30°."),
M("JEE Main 2023 · 1 Feb · Shift 1","pol","Malus' law","'n' polarizing sheets are arranged such that each makes an angle 45° with the preceding sheet. An unpolarized light of intensity I is incident into this arrangement. The output intensity is found to be {I;64}. The value of n will be",["6","5","3","4"],0,"After the first sheet: {I;2}. Each further sheet multiplies the intensity by cos²45° = {1;2}.\nI_out = {I;2} × ({1;2})ⁿ⁻¹ = {I;2ⁿ} = {I;64}, so n = 6."),
M("JEE Main 2023 · 31 Jan · Shift 1","pol","Malus' law","Two polaroid A and B are placed in such a way that the pass-axis of polaroids are perpendicular to each other. Now, another polaroid C is placed between A and B bisecting angle between them. If intensity of unpolarized light is I₀ then intensity of transmitted light after passing through polaroid B will be",["{I₀;8}","Zero","{I₀;2}","{I₀;4}"],0,"After A: {I₀;2}. C is at 45° to A: {I₀;2}cos²45° = {I₀;4}. B is at 45° to C: {I₀;4}cos²45° = {I₀;8}."),
M("JEE Main 2022 · 29 Jul · Shift 2","pol","Malus' law","An unpolarised light beam of intensity 2I₀ is passed through a polaroid P and then through another polaroid Q which is oriented in such a way that its passing axis makes an angle of 30° relative to that of P. The intensity of the emergent light is",["{I₀;4}","{I₀;2}","{3I₀;4}","{3I₀;2}"],2,"After P: I₀. After Q: I₀cos²30° = {3I₀;4}."),
M("JEE Main 2020 · 4 Sep · Shift 1","pol","Malus' law","A beam of plane polarized light of large cross-sectional area and uniform intensity of 3.3 W m⁻² falls normally on a polarizer (cross sectional area 3 × 10⁻⁴ m²) which rotates about its axis with an angular speed of 31.4 rad/s. The energy of light passing through the polariser per revolution, is close to",["1.0 × 10⁻⁴ J","5.0 × 10⁻⁴ J","1.0 × 10⁻⁵ J","1.5 × 10⁻⁴ J"],0,"The transmitted intensity is I₀cos²ωt, with average {I₀;2} over a revolution.\nTime per revolution T = {2π;31.4} = 0.2 s.\nE = {I₀;2} A T = 1.65 × 3 × 10⁻⁴ × 0.2 ≈ 1.0 × 10⁻⁴ J."),
M("JEE Main 2020 · 7 Jan · Shift 2","pol","Malus' law","A polarizer - analyser set is adjusted such that the intensity of light coming out of the analyser is just 10% of the original intensity. Assuming that the polarizer - analyser set does not absorb any light, the angle by which the analyser need to be rotated further to reduce the output intensity to zero, is",["71.6°","90°","18.4°","45°"],2,"Taking the light reaching the analyser as plane polarised along the polariser axis, I = I₀cos²θ = 0.1I₀ gives cos θ = 0.316 and θ = 71.6°.\nThe output is zero when the axes are crossed (θ = 90°), so a further rotation of 90° − 71.6° = 18.4° is needed."),
M("JEE Main 2019 · 12 Apr · Shift 2","pol","Malus' law","A system of three polarizers P₁, P₂, P₃ is set up such that the pass axis of P₃ is crossed with respect to that of P₁. The pass axis of P₂ is inclined at 60° to the pass axis of P₃. When a beam of unpolarized light of intensity I₀ is incident on P₁, the intensity of light transmitted by the three polarizers is I. The ratio ({I₀;I}) equals (nearly)",["10.67","5.33","1.80","16.00"],0,"P₂ is 60° from P₃ and so 30° from P₁.\nI = {I₀;2}cos²30° cos²60° = {I₀;2} × {3;4} × {1;4} = {3I₀;32}.\n{I₀;I} = {32;3} = 10.67."),
M("JEE Main 2019 · 9 Jan · Shift 1","pol","Brewster's law","Consider a tank made of glass (refractive index 1.5) with a thick bottom. It is filled with a liquid of refractive index μ. A student finds that, irrespective of what the incident angle i (see figure) is for a beam of light entering the liquid, the light reflected from the liquid-glass interface is never completely polarized. For this to happen, the minimum value of μ is",["√({5;3})","{4;3}","{3;√5}","{5;√3}"],2,"The refraction angle r in the liquid is at most sin⁻¹({1;μ}) (grazing incidence on the liquid), so tan r < {1;√(μ² − 1)}.\nComplete polarisation at the liquid-glass surface needs tan r = {1.5;μ}. It can never happen if {1;√(μ² − 1)} < {1.5;μ}, i.e. μ² < 2.25(μ² − 1).\nThis gives μ² > 1.8, so the minimum value is μ = √1.8 = {3;√5}."),
M("JEE Main 2018","pol","Malus' law","Unpolarized light of intensity I passes through an ideal polarizer A. Another identical polarizer B is placed behind A. The intensity of light beyond B is found to be {I;2}. Now another identical polarizer C is placed between A and B. The intensity beyond B is now found to be {I;8}. The angle between polarizer A and C is",["0°","30°","45°","60°"],2,"B is parallel to A (it transmits all of {I;2}). With C at θ to A and B parallel to A: {I;2}cos²θ cos²θ = {I;8}.\ncos⁴θ = {1;4}, so cos θ = {1;√2} and θ = 45°."),
M("JEE Main 2018 · Online","pol","Malus' law","A plane polarized light is incident on a polariser with its pass axis making angle θ with x-axis, as shown in the figure. At four different values of θ, θ = 8°, 38°, 188° and 218°, the observed intensities are same. What is the angle between the direction of polarization and x-axis?",["203°","128°","98°","45°"],0,"I ∝ cos²(θ − φ) has period 180°. Equal intensities at 8° and 38° mean they lie symmetrically about a maximum: φ = 23° (mod 180°). Equal intensities at 188° and 218° give 203° (mod 180°), the same line.\nOf the options, 203° = 23° + 180° is the one that is a valid direction."),
M("JEE Main 2018 · Online","pol","Malus' law","Unpolarized light of intensity I is incident on a system of two polarizers, A followed by B. The intensity of emergent light is {I;2}. If a third polarizer C is placed between A and B, the intensity of emergent light is reduced to {I;3}. The angle between the polarizers A and C is θ. Then",["cos θ = ({1;3})^{1/2}","cos θ = ({2;3})^{1/4}","cos θ = ({2;3})^{1/2}","cos θ = ({1;3})^{1/4}"],1,"B is parallel to A, transmitting all of {I;2}. With C between them: {I;2}cos⁴θ = {I;3}.\ncos⁴θ = {2;3}, so cos θ = ({2;3})^{1/4}."),
M("JEE Main 2015 · Online","pol","Brewster's law","Unpolarized light of intensity I₀ is incident on surface of a block of glass at Brewster's angle. In that case, which one of the following statements is true?",["Transmitted light is partially polarized with intensity {I₀;2}.","Transmitted light is completely polarized with intensity less than {I₀;2}.","Reflected light is completely polarized with intensity less than {I₀;2}.","Reflected light is partially polarized with intensity {I₀;2}."],2,"At Brewster's angle the reflected ray is completely polarised, but only a part of the perpendicular component (which is {I₀;2}) is reflected.\nSo the reflected light is completely polarised with intensity less than {I₀;2}; the transmitted light is partially polarised."),
M("JEE Main 2014","pol","Malus' law","Two beams, A and B, of plane polarized light with mutually perpendicular planes of polarization are seen through a polaroid. From the position when the beam A has maximum intensity (and beam B has zero intensity), a rotation of polaroid through 30° makes the two beams appear equally bright. If the initial intensities of the two beams are I_A and I_B respectively, then {I_A;I_B} equals",["{1;3}","3","{3;2}","1"],0,"After a rotation of 30°: A is I_A cos²30° = {3I_A;4}, and B is I_B sin²30° = {I_B;4}.\nEqual brightness: 3I_A = I_B, so {I_A;I_B} = {1;3}."),
M("JEE Main 2013","pol","Malus' law","A beam of unpolarised light of intensity I₀ is passed through a polaroid A and then through another polaroid B which is oriented so that its principal plane makes an angle of 45° relative to that of A. The intensity of the emergent light is",["{I₀;8}","I₀","{I₀;2}","{I₀;4}"],3,"After A: {I₀;2}. After B: {I₀;2}cos²45° = {I₀;4}."),
M("JEE Main 2013 · Online","pol","Brewster's law","A person lives in a high-rise building on the bank of a river 50 m wide. Across the river is a well lit tower of height 40 m. When the person, who is at a height of 10 m, looks through a polaroid at an appropriate angle at light of the tower reflecting from the river surface, he notes that intensity of light coming from distance X from his building is the least and this corresponds to the light coming from light bulbs at height 'Y' on the tower. The values of X and Y are respectively close to (refractive index of water = {4;3})",["25 m, 10 m","13 m, 27 m","22 m, 13 m","17 m, 30 m"],1,"The reflected light is least through the polaroid when the ray strikes the water at Brewster's angle: tan i = {4;3}.\nThe person is 10 m up, so X = 10 tan i = 13.3 m from his building. The bulb, a horizontal distance 50 − X = 36.7 m away, is at Y = {36.7;tan i} = 27.5 m.\nThe values are about 13 m and 27 m."),
M("AIEEE 2005","pol","Polaroid","When an unpolarized light of intensity I₀ is incident on a polarizing sheet, the intensity of the light which does not get transmitted is",["zero","I₀","{1;2}I₀","{1;4}I₀"],2,"A polarising sheet transmits half of unpolarised light, {I₀;2}. The other {I₀;2} is not transmitted."),
M("AIEEE 2004","pol","Brewster's law","The angle of incidence at which reflected light is totally polarized for reflection from air to glass (refractive index n), is",["sin⁻¹(n)","sin⁻¹({1;n})","tan⁻¹({1;n})","tan⁻¹(n)"],3,"Brewster's law: tan i_B = n, so i_B = tan⁻¹(n)."),
N("JEE Advanced 2025","pol","Brewster's law","A solid glass sphere of refractive index n = √3 and radius R contains a spherical air cavity of radius {R;2}, as shown in the figure (the cavity touches the surface of the sphere at its lowest point O). A very thin glass layer is present at the point O so that the air cavity (refractive index n = 1) remains inside the glass sphere. An unpolarized, unidirectional and monochromatic light source S emits a light ray from a point inside the glass sphere towards the periphery of the glass sphere. If the light is reflected from the point O and is fully polarized, then the angle of incidence at the inner surface of the glass sphere is θ. The value of sin θ is ______.",0.75,"At O the ray, travelling in the air of the cavity, is reflected at Brewster's angle for air to glass: tan i = √3, i = 60° from the vertical.\nIn the cavity the chord from its entry point E to O, with the cavity radius {R;2} on both sides, forms an equilateral triangle, so the ray meets the cavity wall at E at 60°. In the glass: sin r = {sin 60°;√3} = {1;2}, r = 30°, which makes the glass ray horizontal.\nThat horizontal ray comes from the point P of the outer surface at height {R;4} above O, whose distance from the vertical axis is {√7R;4}. The radius to P (towards the centre, a height R above O) makes cos θ = {√7;4} with the horizontal ray, so the angle of incidence there has sin θ = {3;4} = 0.75."),
N("JEE Advanced 2024","pol","Malus' law","A point source S emits unpolarized light uniformly in all directions. At two points A and B, the ratio r = {I_A;I_B} of the intensities of light is 2. If a set of two polaroids having 45° angle between their pass-axes is placed just before point B, then the new value of r will be ______.",8,"Unpolarised light through a polaroid keeps {1;2} of its intensity, and the second polaroid at 45° keeps cos²45° = {1;2} of that.\nThe intensity at B becomes {I_B;4}, so r = {I_A;I_B/4} = 4 × 2 = 8."),
N("JEE Main 2023 · 10 Apr · Shift 1","pol","Malus' law","Unpolarised light of intensity 32 W m⁻² passes through the combination of three polaroids such that the pass axis of the last polaroid is perpendicular to that of first polaroid. If intensity of emerging light is 3 W m⁻², then the angle between pass axis of first two polaroids is ______°.",30,"After the first polaroid: 16 W m⁻². With θ between the first two and 90° − θ between the last two: I = 16 cos²θ sin²θ = 4 sin²2θ = 3.\nsin 2θ = {√3;2}, so θ = 30° (or 60°)."),
N("JEE Main 2023 · 29 Jan · Shift 1","pol","Malus' law","As shown in the figure, three identical polaroids P₁, P₂ and P₃ are placed one after another. The pass axis of P₂ and P₃ are inclined at angle of 60° and 90° with respect to axis of P₁. The source S has an intensity of 256 {W;m²}. The intensity of light at point O is ______ {W;m²}.",24,"After P₁: 128 W/m². P₂ is at 60°: 128 × cos²60° = 32. P₃ is 30° from P₂: 32 × cos²30° = 24 W/m²."),
N("JEE Main 2023 · 29 Jan · Shift 2","pol","Brewster's law","Unpolarised light is incident on the boundary between two dielectric media, whose dielectric constants are 2.8 (medium-1) and 6.8 (medium-2), respectively. To satisfy the condition, so that the reflected and refracted rays are perpendicular to each other, the angle of incidence should be tan⁻¹(1 + {10;θ})^{1/2}. The value of θ is ______. (Given for dielectric media, μᵣ = 1)",7,"Brewster's condition: tan i = {n₂;n₁} = √({6.8;2.8}) (n = √ε for μᵣ = 1).\ntan i = √({17;7}) = √(1 + {10;7}), so θ = 7."),
N("JEE Main 2021 · 24 Aug · Shift 1","pol","Malus' law","An unpolarized light beam is incident on the polarizer of a polarization experiment and the intensity of light beam emerging from the analyzer is measured as 100 Lumen. Now, if the analyzer is rotated around the horizontal axis (direction of light) by 30° in clockwise direction, the intensity of emerging light will be ______ Lumen.",75,"Taking the analyser to be parallel to the polariser at the start, the emerging intensity is 100 units. Rotating it by 30° gives 100 cos²30° = 75."),
N("JEE Main 2021 · 26 Aug · Shift 2","pol","Malus' law","A source of light is placed in front of a screen. Intensity of light on the screen is I. Two polaroids P₁ and P₂ are so placed in between the source of light and screen that the intensity of light on screen is {I;2}. P₂ should be rotated by an angle of ______ (degrees) so that intensity of light on the screen becomes {3I;8}.",30,"With P₁ and P₂ parallel the screen receives {I;2}. After rotating P₂ by θ: {I;2}cos²θ = {3I;8}, so cos²θ = {3;4} and θ = 30°."),
M("JEE Main 2023 · 24 Jan · Shift 1","pol","Brewster's law","Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: If the Brewster's angle for the light propagating from air to glass is θ_B, then the Brewster's angle for the light propagating from glass to air is {π;2} − θ_B.\nStatement II: The Brewster's angle for the light propagating from glass to air is tan⁻¹(μ_g) where μ_g is the refractive index of glass.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.",["Both statement I and statement II are true.","Both statement I and statement II are false.","Statement I is true but statement II is false.","Statement I is false but statement II is true."],2,"Air to glass: tan θ_B = μ_g. Glass to air: tan i′ = {1;μ_g} = cot θ_B, so i′ = {π;2} − θ_B: Statement I is true.\nThe Brewster's angle for glass to air is tan⁻¹({1;μ_g}), not tan⁻¹(μ_g): Statement II is false."),
M("JEE Main 2022 · 26 Jun · Shift 2","huyg","Doppler effect of light","For a specific wavelength 670 nm of light coming from a galaxy moving with velocity v, the observed wavelength is 670.7 nm. The value of v is",["3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹","3 × 10¹⁰ m s⁻¹","3.13 × 10⁵ m s⁻¹","4.48 × 10⁵ m s⁻¹"],2,"For v ≪ c: {Δλ;λ} = {v;c}.\nv = 3 × 10⁸ × {0.7;670} = 3.13 × 10⁵ m s⁻¹."),
M("JEE Main 2017","huyg","Doppler effect of light","An observer is moving with half the speed of light towards a stationary microwave source emitting waves at frequency 10 GHz. What is the frequency of the microwave measured by the observer? (speed of light = 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹)",["10.1 GHz","12.1 GHz","17.3 GHz","15.3 GHz"],2,"At such a speed the relativistic Doppler formula applies: f′ = f√({1 + β;1 − β}) with β = 0.5.\nf′ = 10 × √3 = 17.3 GHz."),
N("JEE Main 2021 · 18 Mar · Shift 2","huyg","Doppler effect of light","A galaxy is moving away from the earth at a speed of 286 km s⁻¹. The shift in the wavelength of a red line at 630 nm is x × 10⁻¹⁰ m. The value of x, to the nearest integer, is ______. [Take the value of speed of light c, as 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹]",6,"Δλ = λ{v;c} = 630 × 10⁻⁹ × {2.86 × 10⁵;3 × 10⁸} = 6.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ m, so x = 6."),
];
})();
